window.FK={"video":"AmUuXtVWZb4","duration":31134,"title":"Frankenstein","ann":[{"id":1,"sec":"Letter 1","p":[1,4],"start":14.6,"t":203.3,"from":"You will rejoice to hear that no disaster has …","to":"… effected by an undertaking such as mine.","q":"an enterprise which you have regarded with such evil forebodings","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Opens w/ danger warning: sister fears what he chases","b":"The very first sentence warns that ambition may end in disaster. Walton's 'enterprise' mirrors Victor's later 'undertaking'; both men ignore family worry.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Epistolary novel: told through letters. The dated header ('St. Petersburgh, Dec. 11th, 17—') hides the year with a dash, a period convention that made fiction feel like a real, discreet document."},{"id":2,"sec":"Letter 1","p":[5,6],"start":203.7,"t":307.4,"from":"These reflections have dispelled the agitation with which I …","to":"… into the channel of their earlier bent.","q":"a point on which the soul may fix its intellectual eye","tag":"Character","m":"Walton = obsessed w/ one goal, like Victor","b":"Walton finds peace only in a 'steady purpose'. This single-minded focus is the exact trait that ruins Victor, so Walton is set up as Victor's double.","lt":"Literature","l":"He wanted to be a poet 'where Homer and Shakespeare are consecrated' and failed. Mary Shelley lived among Romantic poets (Percy Shelley, Lord Byron), who prized genius and glory."},{"id":3,"sec":"Letter 1","p":[7,8],"start":307.8,"t":415.4,"from":"Six years have passed since I resolved on my …","to":"… sustain my own, when theirs are failing.","q":"I voluntarily endured cold, famine, thirst, and want of sleep","tag":"Character","m":"Self-discipline → isolation; trains alone","b":"Walton trains his body and studies at night, alone. Victor will also work through sleepless nights; isolation plus ambition is the novel's danger formula.","lt":"History","l":"Real Arctic explorers of the era were searching for the Northwest Passage, a sea route over North America. Expeditions like Franklin's (1845) ended in disaster, much as Margaret fears."},{"id":4,"sec":"Letter 1","p":[9,13],"start":415.8,"t":514.6,"from":"This is the most favourable period for travelling in …","to":"R. Walton","q":"I have no ambition to lose my life","tag":"Irony","m":"Irony: says he's careful, yet risks all","b":"Walton insists he is not reckless, yet he heads into the unknown 'emergencies' of the pole. Readers learn to doubt narrators who reassure too much.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Note the semicolons and dashes ('furs—a dress which…'). Shelley uses the dash to add an explanation mid-sentence, the way we'd use parentheses today."},{"id":5,"sec":"Letter 2","p":[14,17],"start":515.0,"t":665.7,"from":"How slowly the time passes here, encompassed as I …","to":"… me to endeavour to regulate my mind.","q":"I have no friend, Margaret","tag":"Theme","m":"THEME: loneliness / need for a companion","b":"Walton's longing for a friend who will 'sympathise' with him previews the Creature's demand for a companion. Every major character in the book craves connection.","lt":"Psych","l":"Psychologists call this the need to belong (Baumeister & Leary, 1995): humans need close bonds the way they need food. Loneliness raises health risks as much as smoking."},{"id":6,"sec":"Letter 2","p":[18,19],"start":666.1,"t":878.1,"from":"Well, these are useless complaints; I shall certainly find …","to":"… and sympathy which otherwise he would command.","q":"he is madly desirous of glory","tag":"Character","m":"Lieutenant = glory-seeker; Walton too","b":"Walton describes his lieutenant as hungry for glory but quickly corrects himself; the description fits Walton better. The master is kind but 'uneducated', showing Walton's class bias.","lt":"Language","l":"'To word my phrase more characteristically' is self-correction (epanorthosis), a rhetorical device where a speaker revises their words mid-sentence for precision."},{"id":7,"sec":"Letter 2","p":[20,21],"start":878.5,"t":997.1,"from":"Yet do not suppose, because I complain a little …","to":"… unvisited regions I am about to explore.","q":"a love for the marvellous, a belief in the marvellous","tag":"Theme","m":"Romantic spirit: science + wonder","b":"Walton admits his practical science is mixed with a love of the 'marvellous'. Victor's science will be driven by the same mix of reason and fantasy (alchemy).","lt":"Literature","l":"He mentions Coleridge's 'Ancient Mariner' here: an albatross-killing sailor cursed at sea. Shelley actually heard Coleridge recite it as a child, and it previews a cursed voyage."},{"id":8,"sec":"Letter 2","p":[22,23],"start":997.5,"t":1041.6,"from":"But to return to dearer considerations. Shall I meet …","to":"Your affectionate brother, Robert Walton","q":"Remember me with affection, should you never hear from me again","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Possible death hinted = suspense","b":"The letter closes on the chance he may die. Shelley builds suspense before the main story starts; the letters are a 'frame' around Victor's tale.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'Should you never hear' is an inverted conditional (= 'if you should never hear'). It's formal; modern English still uses it: 'Should you need help, call me.'"},{"id":9,"sec":"Letter 3","p":[24,27],"start":1042.0,"t":1102.1,"from":"I write a few lines in haste to say …","to":"… renovating warmth which I had not expected.","q":"floating sheets of ice that continually pass us","tag":"Setting","m":"ICE = danger, isolation, being 'frozen'","b":"Ice will frame the whole novel: it opens and closes in the Arctic, and Victor confronts the Creature on a glacier. Ice suggests emotional coldness and isolation.","lt":"Science","l":"Polar sea ice moves with currents and wind; ships could be 'beset' (trapped) for months. The Open Polar Sea that Walton hopes for was a real but false 1800s theory."},{"id":10,"sec":"Letter 3","p":[28,32],"start":1102.5,"t":1176.8,"from":"No incidents have hitherto befallen us that would make …","to":"R.W.","q":"What can stop the determined heart and resolved will of man?","tag":"Theme","m":"Hubris: man vs. nature (rhetorical Q)","b":"Walton's rhetorical question shows hubris, the excessive pride that tragic heroes have. Nature (the ice) will answer him by trapping his ship.","lt":"Grammar","l":"A rhetorical question is asked for effect, not an answer. Notice the chain 'Wherefore not? … Why not …? What can stop …?' Repeating questions builds emotional momentum."},{"id":11,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[33,37],"start":1177.2,"t":1269.5,"from":"So strange an accident has happened to us that …","to":"… among the distant inequalities of the ice.","q":"a being which had the shape of a man, but apparently of gigantic stature","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"1st sighting of the Creature (not named)","b":"Our first glimpse of the Creature comes before we know anything about him: a giant crossing the ice. Shelley starts at the end of the story and works back.","lt":"Language","l":"'In medias res' means starting in the middle of the action. Many films do this too, opening on a dramatic moment and then flashing back."},{"id":12,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[38,40],"start":1269.9,"t":1365.6,"from":"This appearance excited our unqualified wonder. We were, as …","to":"… you to perish on the open sea.”","q":"He was not, as the other traveller seemed to be, a savage inhabitant","tag":"Character","m":"Contrast: 'savage' giant vs. 'European'","b":"The crew judges the two travelers instantly by appearance. The Creature is assumed 'savage', and the man is trusted because he looks European. This prejudice of appearance is the novel's core injustice.","lt":"History","l":"Words like 'savage' reflect 18th-century colonial attitudes that ranked people by origin. The book will question who is truly 'savage'."},{"id":13,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[41,43],"start":1366.0,"t":1457.8,"from":"On perceiving me, the stranger addressed me in English, …","to":"… a little soup, which restored him wonderfully.","q":"will you have the kindness to inform me whither you are bound?","tag":"Character","m":"Victor won't board unless ship goes north = obsession","b":"Nearly dead, the stranger still cares only about where the ship is headed. His obsession outweighs his survival instinct, the first sign of how consumed Victor is.","lt":"Language","l":"'Whither' = to where; 'whence' = from where; 'thither' = to there. These archaic words survive in phrases like 'whither thou goest'."},{"id":14,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[44,45],"start":1458.2,"t":1533.7,"from":"Two days passed in this manner before he was …","to":"… the ice in so strange a vehicle.","q":"I never saw a more interesting creature","tag":"Language","m":"Walton calls VICTOR a 'creature'","b":"Walton calls Victor a 'creature' long before we meet Victor's Creature. The shared word blurs the line between creator and creation.","lt":"Language","l":"'Creature' comes from Latin creatura, 'a thing created'. Shelley plays on the root: everyone is someone's creature."},{"id":15,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[46,52],"start":1534.1,"t":1596.8,"from":"His countenance instantly assumed an aspect of the deepest …","to":"… you have benevolently restored me to life.”","q":"the dæmon, as he called him","tag":"Symbol","m":"'dæmon' = Victor's label for the Creature","b":"Victor names his pursuer a 'dæmon' (demon), judging him before we hear his side. Watch which names Victor uses (fiend, wretch, devil) and how they shape our view.","lt":"Language","l":"Greek 'daimon' meant a spirit or inner guiding power, not necessarily evil. The 'æ' is a ligature (a and e joined), an older spelling."},{"id":16,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[53,54],"start":1597.2,"t":1643.8,"from":"Soon after this he inquired if I thought that …","to":"… any new object should appear in sight.","q":"a new spirit of life animated the decaying frame of the stranger","tag":"Theme","m":"Revenge gives Victor life (dark irony)","b":"Victor revives only when he hears his enemy may still live. Revenge has become his reason to exist. 'Animated' echoes how he once gave life to dead matter.","lt":"Psych","l":"Obsessive revenge can become someone's whole identity. Psychologists note that rumination on revenge prolongs pain instead of relieving it."},{"id":17,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[55,56],"start":1644.2,"t":1702.3,"from":"Such is my journal of what relates to this …","to":"… possessed as the brother of my heart.","q":"I begin to love him as a brother","tag":"Theme","m":"Walton finally finds the friend he wanted","b":"Walton's wish from Letter 2 comes true, but with a broken man. Friendship and brotherhood are central to the novel: Clerval and Victor, and the Creature's longing for a companion.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Aristotle described the highest friendship as one built on shared virtue, not usefulness or pleasure. Walton and Victor bond over shared ambition. Is that virtue?"},{"id":18,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[57,60],"start":1702.7,"t":1858.6,"from":"I shall continue my journal concerning the stranger at …","to":"… will dash the cup from your lips!”","q":"Have you drunk also of the intoxicating draught?","tag":"Symbol","m":"Knowledge = a drug/poison (metaphor)","b":"Victor compares Walton's thirst for knowledge to a dangerous drink. The 'cup' metaphor warns that ambition intoxicates and blinds.","lt":"Literature","l":"This echoes Genesis: eating the fruit of knowledge brings the Fall. The subtitle 'The Modern Prometheus' refers to the Titan who stole fire (forbidden knowledge) for humans and was punished."},{"id":19,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[61,63],"start":1859.0,"t":1956.1,"from":"Such words, you may imagine, strongly excited my curiosity; …","to":"… lost everything and cannot begin life anew.”","q":"I have lost everything and cannot begin life anew","tag":"Character","m":"Victor = tragic hero, beyond hope","b":"Victor presents himself as someone already ruined, which makes us read his story as a tragedy whose ending is known. His 'friend, the most noble' is Clerval.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Greek tragedy the hero falls through a flaw (hamartia). Victor's flaw is his unchecked ambition. Keep a list of moments where he could have chosen differently."},{"id":20,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[64,66],"start":1956.5,"t":2034.8,"from":"As he said this his countenance became expressive of …","to":"… voice whose varied intonations are soul-subduing music.","q":"Such a man has a double existence","tag":"Theme","m":"'Double' = theme of doubles/doppelgangers","b":"Victor's 'double existence', suffering yet uplifted by nature, introduces the idea of doubles. Walton/Victor and Victor/Creature mirror one another.","lt":"Science","l":"Romantics believed nature could heal the mind. Modern research supports this: time in nature lowers stress hormones (cortisol) and improves mood."},{"id":21,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[67,68],"start":2035.2,"t":2156.9,"from":"Yesterday the stranger said to me, “You may easily …","to":"… the events of which it is composed.”","q":"may not be a serpent to sting you, as mine has been","tag":"Symbol","m":"Serpent = Eden; knowledge brings the Fall","b":"Victor warns that seeking knowledge stung him like a serpent. This tells us his tale is a cautionary one, told to change Walton's course.","lt":"Literature","l":"The serpent tempts Eve in Genesis. Shelley's epigraph is from Milton's 'Paradise Lost', which the Creature will later read and compare himself to."},{"id":22,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[69,70],"start":2157.3,"t":2214.2,"from":"You may easily imagine that I was much gratified …","to":"… will perceive how irrevocably it is determined.”","q":"how irrevocably it is determined","tag":"Theme","m":"Fate vs. free will: Victor blames destiny","b":"Victor claims his fate is fixed. As you read, ask whether events were really destined or whether Victor chose them. He often blames 'destiny' for his own decisions.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Determinism holds that every event is caused by prior events; free will holds that people choose. Victor uses fate as an excuse, a habit psychologists call an external locus of control."},{"id":23,"sec":"Letter 4","p":[71,71],"start":2214.6,"t":2283.8,"from":"He then told me that he would commence his …","to":"… vessel on its course and wrecked it—thus!","q":"frightful the storm which embraced the gallant vessel on its course and wrecked it","tag":"Narration","m":"FRAME ends → Victor's story begins (nested)","b":"Walton will write down Victor's words, and later Victor will relate the Creature's words. The novel is a set of nested stories, each with a narrator we must judge for reliability.","lt":"Grammar","l":"The sentence ends with a dash and 'thus!' The punctuation acts out the shipwreck, breaking the sentence the way the storm breaks the ship."},{"id":24,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[72,73],"start":2284.2,"t":2397.0,"from":"I am by birth a Genevese, and my family …","to":"… world again through his credit and assistance.","q":"I am by birth a Genevese","tag":"Narration","m":"Victor's narration begins: wealthy, respected family","b":"Victor stresses his family's honor and status. He had every advantage, so his downfall can't be blamed on a bad start, unlike the Creature's.","lt":"History","l":"Geneva was an independent Protestant republic run by elected councils ('syndics'). Mary Shelley wrote the novel near Geneva in 1816 at Lake Geneva with Byron."},{"id":25,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[74,75],"start":2397.4,"t":2480.3,"from":"Beaufort had taken effectual measures to conceal himself, and …","to":"… a pittance scarcely sufficient to support life.","q":"her courage rose to support her in her adversity","tag":"Character","m":"Caroline = strong, caring woman (ideal)","b":"Caroline Beaufort works with her hands to support her dying father. Shelley's women are often self-sacrificing caregivers; notice how passive most of them become later.","lt":"History","l":"'Plaited straw' (braiding straw for hats) was common low-paid work for poor women. Mary Shelley's mother, Mary Wollstonecraft, argued women needed education and economic independence."},{"id":26,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[76,77],"start":2480.7,"t":2626.4,"from":"Several months passed in this manner. Her father grew …","to":"… as a restorative for her weakened frame.","q":"He came like a protecting spirit to the poor girl","tag":"Theme","m":"Rescue/protection pattern (father figure)","b":"Alphonse rescues Caroline, and she in turn rescues Elizabeth. The family repeatedly adopts the helpless. That makes Victor's abandonment of his own 'child' even more shocking.","lt":"Language","l":"'Like a protecting spirit' is a simile (comparison using like/as). Note the big age gap; such marriages were common in that era."},{"id":27,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[78,79],"start":2626.8,"t":2828.3,"from":"From Italy they visited Germany and France. I, their …","to":"… a celestial stamp in all her features.","q":"I was their plaything and their idol","tag":"Theme","m":"Parents' duty to child → contrast w/ Creature","b":"Victor says his parents knew they owed duties 'towards the being to which they had given life'. Hold onto this; he will completely fail that duty toward the being HE gives life.","lt":"Psych","l":"Attachment theory (John Bowlby) says children who receive steady affection develop secure bonds. The Creature becomes a case study of what happens without it."},{"id":28,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[80,81],"start":2828.7,"t":2951.2,"from":"The peasant woman, perceiving that my mother fixed eyes …","to":"… of all my occupations and my pleasures.","q":"Elizabeth Lavenza became the inmate of my parents","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth adopted (1831 ed.) = 'gift'","b":"Elizabeth is chosen for her beauty and taken in. In the 1831 edition she is adopted; in the 1818 edition she is Victor's cousin. Note that she is valued for looks, while the Creature is rejected for his.","lt":"Language","l":"'Inmate' here simply means 'one who lives in a house'. Today it means a prisoner. This is semantic shift: words narrow or change meaning over time."},{"id":29,"sec":"Chapter 1","p":[82,82],"start":2951.6,"t":3023.1,"from":"Everyone loved Elizabeth. The passionate and almost reverential attachment …","to":"… death she was to be mine only.","q":"looked upon Elizabeth as mine","tag":"Theme","m":"Elizabeth = possession ('mine') → objectified","b":"Victor treats Elizabeth as a 'possession of my own'. This possessive love foreshadows his self-centered thinking, and 'till death' is grim foreshadowing.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Feminist critics point out that the women in Frankenstein are passive and often objectified. Mary Wollstonecraft (Shelley's mother) argued women were raised to be ornaments rather than equals."},{"id":30,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[83,84],"start":3023.5,"t":3195.2,"from":"We were brought up together; there was not quite …","to":"… sepulchre from the hands of the infidels.","q":"more deeply smitten with the thirst for knowledge","tag":"Character","m":"Victor = 'thirst for knowledge' (key trait)","b":"Elizabeth admires nature's beauty while Victor wants to know its causes. Their contrast sets art and feeling against analysis and control.","lt":"Language","l":"'Thirst for knowledge' is a metaphor that treats knowledge as something to drink. Compare the 'intoxicating draught' in Letter 4; Shelley keeps returning to the image."},{"id":31,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[85,86],"start":3195.6,"t":3275.4,"from":"No human being could have passed a happier childhood …","to":"… sense, the physical secrets of the world.","q":"the physical secrets of the world","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor wants nature's SECRETS = power","b":"Victor admits a 'violent' temper and says he was drawn to the 'secrets of heaven and earth', not languages or politics. Secrets and hidden knowledge drive him.","lt":"Language","l":"'Metaphysical' means beyond the physical (soul, being, reality); 'physical' means the material world. Victor uses both, blurring science and philosophy."},{"id":32,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[87,88],"start":3275.8,"t":3408.4,"from":"Meanwhile Clerval occupied himself, so to speak, with the …","to":"… swept away all my hopes and joys.","q":"it became the torrent which, in its course, has swept away all my hopes and joys","tag":"Symbol","m":"Metaphor: passion = river → torrent","b":"Clerval cares about people and morals; Victor cares about secrets. Victor's extended metaphor of a stream that grows into a destructive torrent shows how small obsessions grow.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Extended metaphor: one comparison developed across a whole sentence (source → river → torrent → swept away). Try spotting the stages and labelling them in the margin."},{"id":33,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[89,90],"start":3408.8,"t":3523.9,"from":"Natural philosophy is the genius that has regulated my …","to":"… continued to read with the greatest avidity.","q":"Cornelius Agrippa","tag":"Allusion","m":"Agrippa = alchemy; father's dismissal = turning point","b":"Victor blames his father for calling Agrippa 'sad trash' without explaining why. He shifts blame onto others, a pattern you'll keep seeing.","lt":"History","l":"Cornelius Agrippa (1486–1535) was a real German occultist. Alchemists sought the philosopher's stone and the elixir of life, quests that resemble Victor's goal."},{"id":34,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[91,92],"start":3524.3,"t":3627.6,"from":"When I returned home my first care was to …","to":"… and rashly and ignorantly I had repined.","q":"Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus","tag":"Allusion","m":"Old 'magic' science vs. modern science","b":"Victor prefers the grand promises of alchemy to modern science's slow, modest progress. He wants power, not just understanding.","lt":"Science","l":"Newton said he was like a boy picking up pebbles 'whilst the great ocean of truth lay all undiscovered before me'. Real science is humble and incremental; Victor wants shortcuts."},{"id":35,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[93,94],"start":3628.0,"t":3745.6,"from":"But here were books, and here were men who …","to":"… again changed the current of my ideas.","q":"the elixir of life","tag":"Theme","m":"Goal: banish disease / make man invulnerable","b":"Victor seeks the elixir of life and the glory it would bring. Even as a teen his goal mixes helping humanity with personal fame.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Today, anti-aging and life-extension research is a billion-dollar industry. The ethical question, who benefits and at what cost, is the same one Victor ignores."},{"id":36,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[95,96],"start":3746.0,"t":3899.1,"from":"When I was about fifteen years old we had …","to":"… foundations, and so worthy of my consideration.","q":"I beheld a stream of fire issue from an old and beautiful oak","tag":"Symbol","m":"LIGHTNING = power of nature / spark of life","b":"Lightning destroys the oak, showing nature's terrifying power. Electricity becomes Victor's new fascination and hints at how he'll 'spark' life.","lt":"Science","l":"Galvanism: in the 1780s Luigi Galvani made dead frog legs twitch with electricity; in 1803 his nephew Aldini 'animated' a hanged man's corpse in London. Shelley knew of these experiments."},{"id":37,"sec":"Chapter 2","p":[97,98],"start":3899.5,"t":3966.4,"from":"Thus strangely are our souls constructed, and by such …","to":"… had decreed my utter and terrible destruction.","q":"Destiny was too potent, and her immutable laws had decreed my utter and terrible destruction","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor blames DESTINY (personified) again","b":"Victor nearly turns to safe mathematics but claims destiny overruled him. He keeps framing his choices as fate, which takes away his responsibility.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Personification: Destiny is 'her' with 'laws'. Writers personify abstract forces to make them feel like characters. Here it's an excuse."},{"id":38,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[99,100],"start":3966.8,"t":4096.3,"from":"When I had attained the age of seventeen my …","to":"… hope of meeting you in another world.”","q":"the first misfortune of my life occurred","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Caroline dies of scarlet fever: an 'omen'","b":"Caroline catches scarlet fever while nursing Elizabeth and dies. Her dying wish is for Victor and Elizabeth to marry. Victor's grief over death may feed his wish to conquer it.","lt":"Science","l":"Scarlet fever (a streptococcal infection) killed many children before antibiotics. Today penicillin cures it in days. Mary Shelley herself lost babies and her mother to illness."},{"id":39,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[101,102],"start":4096.7,"t":4218.7,"from":"She died calmly, and her countenance expressed affection even …","to":"… my sweet Elizabeth in some degree consoled.","q":"the void that presents itself to the soul","tag":"Theme","m":"Death & grief: motivation to defeat death?","b":"Victor describes grief's emptiness. This loss is a turning point: soon after, he goes off to study how life and death work.","lt":"Psych","l":"The Kübler-Ross model describes grief stages (denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance). Some readers see Victor's project as extreme 'bargaining' with death."},{"id":40,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[103,104],"start":4219.1,"t":4288.7,"from":"She indeed veiled her grief and strove to act …","to":"… chained to the miserable details of commerce.","q":"not to be chained to the miserable details of commerce","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval wants education, held back by father","b":"Elizabeth hides her grief to comfort others. Clerval is kept from university by his merchant father. Note who gets education: Victor easily, Clerval with difficulty, Elizabeth never.","lt":"History","l":"In the 1700s only wealthy men attended university. 'Liberal education' meant broad learning (languages, history, philosophy) fit for a free ('liber') man."},{"id":41,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[105,106],"start":4289.1,"t":4397.4,"from":"We sat late. We could not tear ourselves away …","to":"… would, indeed, have been folly to repent.","q":"I was now alone","tag":"Theme","m":"Leaves home → isolation begins","b":"Victor leaves the warm family circle for Ingolstadt. Physical distance from family will become emotional isolation. He even admits he was bad at meeting new people.","lt":"Psych","l":"Starting college is a known period of loneliness and risk for mental-health struggles; support networks matter. Victor will cut his off completely."},{"id":42,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[107,108],"start":4397.8,"t":4468.9,"from":"I had sufficient leisure for these and many other …","to":"… spent your time in studying such nonsense?”","q":"the Angel of Destruction, which asserted omnipotent sway over me","tag":"Theme","m":"Blames 'Chance/Angel of Destruction'","b":"Victor corrects himself: not 'chance' but an 'Angel of Destruction' led him to M. Krempe. Again he narrates his life as cursed rather than chosen.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Em dashes ('Chance—or rather…') let a speaker interrupt and correct himself. Shelley's narrators do this often, showing them reshaping their story as they tell it."},{"id":43,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[109,110],"start":4469.3,"t":4547.9,"from":"I replied in the affirmative. “Every minute,” continued M. …","to":"… chemistry the alternate days that he omitted.","q":"these fancies which you have so greedily imbibed are a thousand years old","tag":"Character","m":"Krempe mocks alchemy = modern science","b":"M. Krempe bluntly dismisses Victor's alchemists. He's right about the science, but his rudeness pushes Victor away. Victor judges him by his looks, the very thing he'll condemn in others.","lt":"History","l":"The Scientific Revolution (1500s–1700s) replaced alchemy with chemistry. Lavoisier, the 'father of modern chemistry', named oxygen and hydrogen in the 1770s–80s."},{"id":44,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[111,112],"start":4548.3,"t":4680.4,"from":"I returned home not disappointed, for I have said …","to":"… he had hitherto been out of town.","q":"a repulsive countenance","tag":"Irony","m":"Victor judges Krempe by APPEARANCE (irony!)","b":"Victor rejects Krempe partly because he's 'squat' and 'repulsive'. This is the same appearance-based judgment that dooms the Creature, and Victor is guilty of it from the start.","lt":"Psych","l":"The 'halo effect' is a bias where we assume good-looking people are also good, smart or kind. The reverse (the 'horns effect') makes us judge unattractive people harshly."},{"id":45,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[113,114],"start":4680.8,"t":4802.8,"from":"Partly from curiosity and partly from idleness, I went …","to":"… the invisible world with its own shadows.”","q":"they can command the thunders of heaven, mimic the earthquake","tag":"Theme","m":"Waldman: modern science = godlike power","b":"Waldman's lecture praises modern scientists who 'penetrate into the recesses of nature'. His language of power and command ignites Victor's ambition.","lt":"Science","l":"Scientists of the era did 'command the thunders': Benjamin Franklin's kite experiment (1752) and the lightning rod showed electricity could be captured and controlled."},{"id":46,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[115,116],"start":4803.2,"t":4983.4,"from":"Such were the professor’s words—rather let me say such …","to":"… concerning the books I ought to procure.","q":"one thought, one conception, one purpose","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor's obsession is born (tricolon)","b":"This is the moment Victor's obsession takes over: he will 'pioneer a new way' and unfold 'the deepest mysteries of creation'. He speaks of himself in the third person ('the soul of Frankenstein'), as if he were a hero in a legend.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Tricolon: a list of three parallel items ('one thought, one conception, one purpose') for rhythm and emphasis. Speeches use it often: 'government of the people, by the people, for the people.'"},{"id":47,"sec":"Chapter 3","p":[117,119],"start":4983.8,"t":5067.8,"from":"“I am happy,” said M. Waldman, “to have gained …","to":"… to me; it decided my future destiny.","q":"it decided my future destiny","tag":"Character","m":"Waldman = mentor; Victor's turning point","b":"Kind Waldman welcomes Victor as a 'disciple'. Unlike Krempe, he is gentle, and that is what wins Victor over. Mentors shape us, but Victor will soon have no one to check him.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Waldman says a chemist who studies only one field is a 'sorry' one. Today 'interdisciplinary' science (bioethics, for example) exists to keep scientists from Victor's tunnel vision."},{"id":48,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[120,121],"start":5068.2,"t":5250.3,"from":"From this day natural philosophy, and particularly chemistry, in …","to":"… an incident happened that protracted my stay.","q":"became nearly my sole occupation","tag":"Character","m":"Total focus: 2 yrs, no visits home","b":"Victor studies so hard that he never goes home in two years and makes discoveries that earn the university's esteem. His talent is real; the problem is that he has no balance.","lt":"Psych","l":"'Flow' (Csikszentmihalyi) is the joy of total absorption in a task. Healthy flow ends; Victor's becomes compulsion. Burnout research shows what happens when it doesn't stop."},{"id":49,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[122,122],"start":5250.7,"t":5414.2,"from":"One of the phenomena which had peculiarly attracted my …","to":"… reserved to discover so astonishing a secret.","q":"Whence, I often asked myself, did the principle of life proceed?","tag":"Theme","m":"KEY QUESTION: where does life come from?","b":"Victor studies death (decay, corpses, charnel-houses) to understand life. He is 'surprised' that 'I alone' should discover the secret. Note the arrogance.","lt":"Science","l":"Scientists in the 1810s debated 'vitalism', the belief that life needs a special spark beyond chemistry. Today we know life is chemistry, yet we still can't build a cell from scratch."},{"id":50,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[123,124],"start":5414.6,"t":5512.5,"from":"Remember, I am not recording the vision of a …","to":"… by one glimmering and seemingly ineffectual light.","q":"Remember, I am not recording the vision of a madman","tag":"Narration","m":"Unreliable narrator insists he's sane","b":"Victor insists he's telling the truth. When narrators protest too much, readers grow suspicious. Remember that Walton is transcribing a feverish, grieving man.","lt":"Literature","l":"Compare Poe's 'The Tell-Tale Heart' (1843), whose narrator begins by insisting he is not mad. That is the classic unreliable-narrator opening."},{"id":51,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[125,126],"start":5512.9,"t":5676.8,"from":"I see by your eagerness and the wonder and …","to":"… collecting and arranging my materials, I began.","q":"Learn from me, if not by my precepts, at least by my example","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Moral: dangerous knowledge; ignorance = bliss?","b":"Victor speaks directly to Walton: the man who believes 'his native town to be the world' is happier than one who aspires 'to become greater than his nature will allow'. This is the novel's central warning. He also decides to make the being eight feet tall to make the work easier: a shortcut with huge consequences.","lt":"Ethics","l":"The 'precautionary principle' says not to move forward with a powerful technology until its risks are understood. It is debated today with AI, gene editing (CRISPR) and nuclear power."},{"id":52,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[127,128],"start":5677.2,"t":5843.8,"from":"No one can conceive the variety of feelings which …","to":"… brought my work near to a conclusion.","q":"A new species would bless me as its creator and source","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor wants to be GOD/father, worshipped","b":"Victor imagines grateful creatures who would owe him everything: 'No father could claim the gratitude of his child so completely.' He wants worship, not a relationship. Note also where his materials come from: the dissecting room and slaughter-house.","lt":"History","l":"In the 1800s 'resurrection men' robbed graves to sell bodies to anatomy schools. Britain's Anatomy Act of 1832 was passed partly in response to the Burke and Hare murders."},{"id":53,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[129,130],"start":5844.2,"t":5926.1,"from":"The summer months passed while I was thus engaged, …","to":"… habit of my nature, should be completed.","q":"my eyes were insensible to the charms of nature","tag":"Theme","m":"Cut off from NATURE + FAMILY = warning sign","b":"In a gorgeous summer, Victor sees nothing. For Romantic writers, losing your connection to nature means losing your humanity. He also puts off his feelings 'until the great object… should be completed.'","lt":"Literature","l":"Wordsworth's 'The world is too much with us' (1807) laments people who are 'out of tune' with nature, busy 'getting and spending'. Victor is the extreme case."},{"id":54,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[131,132],"start":5926.5,"t":6006.7,"from":"I then thought that my father would be unjust …","to":"… and your looks remind me to proceed.","q":"America would have been discovered more gradually","tag":"Theme","m":"Moral: no pursuit worth destroying affection","b":"Victor steps outside his story to moralize: any study that weakens your affections is 'unlawful'. He argues that slower progress (Greece, Caesar, Mexico, Peru) would have spared suffering.","lt":"History","l":"He points to the Spanish conquest of the Aztec (Mexico) and Inca (Peru) empires (1519–1533), driven by ambition and greed, which destroyed whole civilizations. It is a real-world example of ambition without ethics."},{"id":55,"sec":"Chapter 4","p":[133,133],"start":6007.1,"t":6096.7,"from":"My father made no reproach in his letters and …","to":"… these when my creation should be complete.","q":"The leaves of that year had withered","tag":"Setting","m":"Seasons pass = time & health wasted","b":"Shelley marks time through the seasons Victor ignores. His body deteriorates ('emaciated', nervous, feverish) while his 'creation' nears completion. He wastes away as it gains life.","lt":"Language","l":"Pathetic fallacy: nature reflects a character's mood. Here the withering leaves reflect Victor's decline."},{"id":56,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[134,135],"start":6097.1,"t":6181.4,"from":"It was on a dreary night of November that …","to":"… his shrivelled complexion and straight black lips.","q":"It was on a dreary night of November","tag":"Setting","m":"★ CREATION SCENE: gothic setting","b":"The creation is famously brief: rain, a dying candle, 1 a.m., and a 'dull yellow eye' opening. Victor chose 'beautiful' features, but together they are horrid. Beauty in parts does not make a beautiful whole.","lt":"Literature","l":"Mary Shelley said this scene came first, from a waking nightmare in June 1816 during Byron's ghost-story contest. This chapter was the seed of the whole novel."},{"id":57,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[136,136],"start":6181.8,"t":6337.5,"from":"The different accidents of life are not so changeable …","to":"… which I had so miserably given life.","q":"the beauty of the dream vanished, and breathless horror and disgust filled my heart","tag":"Theme","m":"Instant rejection: abandons creation","b":"After two years of work, Victor flees the moment his creation is alive. He rejects it purely for its looks. This is the novel's original sin: abandonment.","lt":"Psych","l":"Parental rejection research shows children who feel rejected are more likely to become hostile and aggressive. Psychologists call this the rejection-aggression link."},{"id":58,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[137,139],"start":6337.9,"t":6431.2,"from":"Oh! No mortal could support the horror of that …","to":"… poured from a black and comfortless sky.","q":"it became a thing such as even Dante could not have conceived","tag":"Allusion","m":"Creature reaches for Victor; V. runs","b":"The Creature comes to Victor's bed, 'one hand stretched out', smiling, perhaps reaching like a baby. Victor reads it as a threat and runs. We only get Victor's interpretation.","lt":"Literature","l":"Dante's 'Inferno' (1300s) describes the monsters of Hell. Victor also quotes Coleridge's 'Ancient Mariner' ('a frightful fiend / Doth close behind him tread'), the same poem Walton cited."},{"id":59,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[140,143],"start":6431.6,"t":6513.0,"from":"I continued walking in this manner for some time, …","to":"… at the very moment of my alighting!”","q":"Like one who, on a lonely road","tag":"Allusion","m":"Coleridge quote = guilt follows him","b":"Victor's state is captured by Coleridge's lines about being stalked by a fiend. Then, ironically, his kind friend Clerval appears, the opposite of a fiend.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Block quotations of poetry keep their line breaks and are set apart from prose. In essays, quote 1–3 lines inline with slashes (/) and longer passages as an indented block."},{"id":60,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[144,145],"start":6513.4,"t":6602.6,"from":"Nothing could equal my delight on seeing Clerval; his …","to":"… you left my father, brothers, and Elizabeth.”","q":"a voyage of discovery to the land of knowledge","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval = healing friend; balanced learner","b":"Clerval's arrival restores Victor's calm. Clerval's father finally let him study; his 'voyage of discovery' is a healthy contrast to Victor's and Walton's dangerous voyages.","lt":"Language","l":"Clerval quotes the Dutch schoolmaster in 'The Vicar of Wakefield' (Goldsmith, 1766), using humor. Allusions show a character's education and personality."},{"id":61,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[146,148],"start":6603.0,"t":6723.6,"from":"“Very well, and very happy, only a little uneasy …","to":"… for joy and ran down to Clerval.","q":"you look as if you had been watching for several nights","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor hides the truth (secrecy theme)","b":"Clerval notices Victor's illness immediately, but Victor won't explain. Secrecy is Victor's pattern: he never tells anyone about the Creature until it's far too late.","lt":"Psych","l":"Keeping a big secret increases stress and isolation; studies by Michael Slepian found that thinking about secrets, not just hiding them, harms well-being."},{"id":62,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[149,151],"start":6724.0,"t":6798.5,"from":"We ascended into my room, and the servant presently …","to":"… furiously and fell down in a fit.","q":"I imagined that the monster seized me; I struggled furiously and fell down in a fit","tag":"Character","m":"Victor's breakdown: hysteria, then collapse","b":"Victor laughs uncontrollably, then collapses. His mind can't hold what he's done. Notice the shift from 'creature' to 'monster' in his vocabulary.","lt":"Science","l":"Sleep deprivation and extreme stress can cause hallucinations and paranoia. Victor has gone months with little sleep, so his 'spectre' may be partly a symptom."},{"id":63,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[152,153],"start":6798.9,"t":6857.6,"from":"Poor Clerval! What must have been his feelings? A …","to":"… kindest action that he could towards them.","q":"This was the commencement of a nervous fever which confined me for several months","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval nurses him = true friendship","b":"Clerval gives up his whole winter of study to nurse Victor and hides the severity from Victor's family. Compare the care Victor receives with the care his creation never gets.","lt":"History","l":"'Nervous fever' was a vague diagnosis for illnesses with mental symptoms, sometimes typhus and sometimes a breakdown. Before modern medicine, nursing was often the only treatment."},{"id":64,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[154,155],"start":6858.0,"t":6937.2,"from":"But I was in reality very ill, and surely …","to":"… I was attacked by the fatal passion.","q":"The form of the monster on whom I had bestowed existence was for ever before my eyes","tag":"Theme","m":"Guilt haunts him, but still won't confess","b":"Victor raves about the monster in his fever, but recovers in spring. Nature heals him again. The Creature, meanwhile, is out alone in the world. Where is he?","lt":"Grammar","l":"'On whom I had bestowed existence': a preposition before 'whom' is formal style. Casual English would be 'who I'd given life to'. Formal writing avoids ending with a preposition."},{"id":65,"sec":"Chapter 5","p":[156,161],"start":6937.6,"t":7028.5,"from":"“Dearest Clerval,” exclaimed I, “how kind, how very good …","to":"… it is from your cousin, I believe.”","q":"How shall I ever repay you?","tag":"Character","m":"Victor asks forgiveness of Clerval, not creature","b":"Victor feels remorse toward Clerval for wasting his winter, yet none toward the being he abandoned. His moral concern only reaches people he sees as his own.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Philosopher Peter Singer writes about the 'expanding circle', the idea that moral progress means extending concern beyond our own group. Victor's circle is very small."},{"id":66,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[162,164],"start":7028.9,"t":7104.6,"from":"Clerval then put the following letter into my hands. …","to":"… this intelligence soon in your own handwriting.","q":"one word from you, dear Victor, is necessary to calm our apprehensions","tag":"Narration","m":"Elizabeth's letter = another narrator","b":"Elizabeth's letter adds another voice, a letter inside a story inside letters. Her tone is warm and domestic, a contrast with Victor's dark obsession.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Nested quotation: in British style the outer quote uses one set of marks and the inner quote another ('…\"…\"…'). Each new paragraph of a long quotation opens with a quote mark but doesn't close the previous one."},{"id":67,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[165,166],"start":7105.0,"t":7283.9,"from":"“Get well—and return to us. You will find a …","to":"… of the dignity of a human being.","q":"does not include the idea of ignorance and a sacrifice of the dignity of a human being","tag":"Character","m":"Justine introduced; Swiss 'equality' of classes","b":"Elizabeth introduces Justine Moritz, a servant welcomed into the family. Elizabeth says Geneva's republic treats lower classes with more dignity than France or England. Remember Justine. She becomes crucial.","lt":"History","l":"The French Revolution (1789) began partly over class inequality. Republics like Geneva claimed to be more equal. Mary's father, William Godwin, wrote against aristocratic privilege."},{"id":68,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[167,168],"start":7284.3,"t":7367.6,"from":"“Justine, you may remember, was a great favourite of …","to":"… but other trials were reserved for her.","q":"one glance from Justine could dissipate it","tag":"Allusion","m":"Justine: loyal, kind, Caroline's imitator","b":"Justine adored Caroline and imitated her. Elizabeth shows that Justine is gentle and devoted. Shelley is setting her up as purely innocent before what happens later.","lt":"Literature","l":"Ariosto's 'Orlando Furioso' (1516) is an Italian epic whose heroine Angelica drives knights mad with love. Characters' reading reveals their education."},{"id":69,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[169,170],"start":7368.0,"t":7499.9,"from":"“One by one, her brothers and sister died; and …","to":"… little girl of five years of age.","q":"She was a Roman Catholic; and I believe her confessor confirmed the idea","tag":"Character","m":"Justine's mother cruel → blames her","b":"Justine's mother neglected her and later blamed her for her siblings' deaths. Justine's life is a pattern of unfair blame, foreshadowing her trial. William appears here: sweet, dimpled, rosy.","lt":"History","l":"Geneva was a Protestant (Calvinist) city; Elizabeth's comment reflects Protestant suspicion of Catholic confession. Religious tension shaped Swiss life for centuries."},{"id":70,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[171,172],"start":7500.3,"t":7563.8,"from":"“Now, dear Victor, I dare say you wish to …","to":"… of yourself; and, I entreat you, write!","q":"one line—one word will be a blessing to us","tag":"Theme","m":"Family begs for contact = Victor neglects","b":"Elizabeth's gossip about Geneva marriages is light and normal. Her repeated plea, 'write!', shows how Victor has neglected the family who love him.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Dashes and repetition ('one line—one word') mimic urgent speech. Writers use these to add emotion; in formal essays use them sparingly."},{"id":71,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[173,176],"start":7564.2,"t":7751.6,"from":"“Dear, dear Elizabeth!” I exclaimed, when I had read …","to":"… to another would only impress more deeply.","q":"I could never persuade myself to confide in him","tag":"Theme","m":"Secrecy again: won't confide in Clerval","b":"Victor recovers and introduces Clerval to the professors, but the sight of his lab instruments sickens him. Henry senses something and removes them, yet Victor still won't share his secret.","lt":"Psych","l":"Avoidance (staying away from reminders of trauma) is a core symptom of PTSD. It brings short-term relief but keeps the fear alive."},{"id":72,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[177,178],"start":7752.0,"t":7830.7,"from":"M. Krempe was not equally docile; and in my …","to":"… subject that was so annoying to me.","q":"I assure you he has outstript us all","tag":"Irony","m":"Praise = torture (dramatic irony)","b":"Krempe praises Victor loudly, but every word about his scientific skill is torture to Victor. We know why; Krempe doesn't. That is dramatic irony.","lt":"Language","l":"'D—n the fellow!': writers once censored swear words with dashes. 'Outstript' is an older spelling of 'outstripped' (surpassed)."},{"id":73,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[179,180],"start":7831.1,"t":7975.5,"from":"Clerval had never sympathised in my tastes for natural …","to":"… came its beauty compensated for its dilatoriness.","q":"the oriental languages","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval studies languages: human connection","b":"Clerval studies Persian, Arabic and Sanskrit. Victor joins him to escape science. Languages connect people; Victor's science isolated him. They are opposite paths.","lt":"History","l":"In the early 1800s Europeans studied 'oriental' (Middle Eastern and Asian) languages partly for trade and the British Empire in India. The term 'Orientalism' (Edward Said, 1978) critiques how the West imagined the East."},{"id":74,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[181,182],"start":7975.9,"t":8096.6,"from":"The month of May had already commenced, and I …","to":"… throw them off, with an invincible burden.","q":"the flowers of spring bloomed in the hedges","tag":"Setting","m":"Nature heals → Victor at peace (briefly)","b":"On a walking tour with Clerval, Victor feels free again. Spring and friendship bring him back to life. Enjoy it; it's the calm before the storm.","lt":"Literature","l":"Walking tours were a Romantic tradition: Wordsworth and Coleridge walked across England and the Alps. Mary and Percy Shelley walked across war-torn France in 1814."},{"id":75,"sec":"Chapter 6","p":[183,184],"start":8097.0,"t":8154.6,"from":"Henry rejoiced in my gaiety, and sincerely sympathised in …","to":"… with feelings of unbridled joy and hilarity.","q":"I bounded along with feelings of unbridled joy and hilarity","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Peak happiness → disaster next ch.","b":"Chapter 6 ends at Victor's happiest moment. Shelley sets up a sharp reversal: the next chapter brings terrible news from Geneva.","lt":"Literature","l":"Peripeteia (Aristotle): a sudden reversal of fortune. Writers often place one right after a character's happiest moment so the fall hits harder."},{"id":76,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[185,187],"start":8155.0,"t":8218.8,"from":"On my return, I found the following letter from …","to":"… to convey to you the horrible tidings.","q":"your eye skims over the page to seek the words","tag":"Narration","m":"Father's letter: delays the bad news","b":"Alphonse knows Victor will skim ahead for the bad news, and so do we. Shelley uses the letter to build dread before the reveal.","lt":"Language","l":"Second-person address ('your eye skims') pulls the reader into the scene. It's a technique writers use to create immediacy: 'You open the door…'"},{"id":77,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[188,190],"start":8219.2,"t":8281.0,"from":"“William is dead!—that sweet child, whose smiles delighted and …","to":"… time, but that he did not return.","q":"William is dead!","tag":"Theme","m":"★ William murdered = Creature's 1st victim","b":"Victor's youngest brother is murdered. Three short exclamations (dead, sweet child, murdered) show the father's shock. The 'innocent child' victim makes the crime feel monstrous.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Short declarative sentences after long ones create impact. Notice how the rhythm shifts from the father's careful preamble to 'William is dead!'"},{"id":78,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[191,192],"start":8281.4,"t":8345.5,"from":"“This account rather alarmed us, and we continued to …","to":"… God! I have murdered my darling child!’","q":"I have murdered my darling child!","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth blames herself (guilt spreads)","b":"Elizabeth blames herself because she let William wear the miniature. Victor is the one truly responsible, yet it's the innocent who carry the guilt.","lt":"Psych","l":"'Survivor's guilt' is the irrational self-blame people feel after a loved one's death, common in grief. Elizabeth and later Justine both take on blame that isn't theirs."},{"id":79,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[193,194],"start":8345.9,"t":8411.0,"from":"“She fainted, and was restored with extreme difficulty. When …","to":"… cruel, miserable death of her youngest darling!","q":"a very valuable miniature that she possessed of your mother","tag":"Symbol","m":"Miniature (portrait of Caroline) = key clue","b":"The missing locket portrait of Caroline is assumed to be the motive. Keep track of it; it becomes the evidence that frames Justine.","lt":"History","l":"Portrait miniatures were tiny painted portraits worn in lockets before photography (invented around 1839). They were keepsakes of loved ones, often of the dead."},{"id":80,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[195,201],"start":8411.4,"t":8483.7,"from":"“Come, Victor; not brooding thoughts of vengeance against the …","to":"… he read the account of my misfortune.","q":"not with hatred for your enemies","tag":"Theme","m":"Father: forgive, don't seek revenge","b":"Alphonse asks Victor to come home with 'peace and gentleness', not vengeance. Victor will ignore this advice, and revenge will consume the rest of his life.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Many ethical traditions teach that revenge 'festers' the wound, from 'turn the other cheek' to Stoicism. Psychology agrees: forgiveness is linked to lower stress and better health."},{"id":81,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[202,204],"start":8484.1,"t":8564.2,"from":"“I can offer you no consolation, my friend,” said …","to":"… must reserve that for his miserable survivors.”","q":"A sod covers his gentle form, and he knows no pain","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval's comfort: pity the living","b":"Clerval reflects that William is at peace and the survivors are the ones to pity. That turns out to be truer than he knows: Victor will suffer far more than anyone.","lt":"Language","l":"'Sod' (grass and soil) is a metonymy for the grave: one associated thing stands in for another. Similar examples: 'the crown' for the monarch, 'the White House' for the president."},{"id":82,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[205,206],"start":8564.6,"t":8639.4,"from":"Clerval spoke thus as we hurried through the streets; …","to":"… although I was unable to define them.","q":"dreading a thousand nameless evils that made me tremble","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Dread on journey: senses something worse","b":"Victor hesitates as he nears home, sensing that more disasters are coming. His anxiety is vague but grows stronger.","lt":"Psych","l":"Anticipatory anxiety (fear of a future threat you can't name) is often worse than the threat itself. Researchers find uncertainty is more stressful than certain bad news."},{"id":83,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[207,209],"start":8639.8,"t":8720.6,"from":"I remained two days at Lausanne, in this painful …","to":"… and, more than all, thy lovely lake!","q":"the palaces of nature","tag":"Allusion","m":"Byron quote; nature calms Victor","b":"The Alps soothe Victor. 'The palaces of nature' quotes Byron's 'Childe Harold's Pilgrimage', a nod to Byron, who was at Lake Geneva with Mary in 1816.","lt":"Literature","l":"The Sublime: Romantic writers described awe-inspiring nature (mountains, storms) that is beautiful and terrifying at once. Edmund Burke defined it in 1757."},{"id":84,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[210,211],"start":8721.0,"t":8814.7,"from":"Yet, as I drew nearer home, grief and fear …","to":"… large drops, but its violence quickly increased.","q":"I was destined to become the most wretched of human beings","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Prophetic dread + approaching storm","b":"Victor 'prophesies' his misery and a storm approaches. Shelley uses weather to mirror his inner turmoil and to herald the Creature.","lt":"Language","l":"Pathetic fallacy again: the storm matches the mood. Note the verbs: 'advanced', 'clouded', 'increased'. The storm is described like an attacking army."},{"id":85,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[212,213],"start":8815.1,"t":8986.6,"from":"I quitted my seat, and walked on, although the …","to":"… He soon reached the summit, and disappeared.","q":"a vast sheet of fire","tag":"Setting","m":"Lightning reveals the Creature (callback ch.2)","b":"A lightning flash reveals the Creature near where William died. Lightning, which first inspired Victor's science, now exposes its consequence. The Creature climbs Mont Salève with superhuman speed.","lt":"Science","l":"Thunder echoing off mountains is reverberation: sound reflecting off surfaces. You see lightning before you hear thunder because light travels about 900,000 times faster than sound."},{"id":86,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[214,215],"start":8987.0,"t":9074.8,"from":"I remained motionless. The thunder ceased; but the rain …","to":"… destroy all that was dear to me.","q":"nearly in the light of my own vampire, my own spirit let loose from the grave","tag":"Symbol","m":"Creature = Victor's 'vampire'/own spirit","b":"Victor instantly decides the Creature is the murderer and calls it 'my own spirit let loose', admitting the Creature is part of himself. Many critics read it as Victor's dark double.","lt":"Literature","l":"The same 1816 contest at Lake Geneva produced John Polidori's 'The Vampyre' (1819), the first modern vampire story in English and an ancestor of Dracula."},{"id":87,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[216,217],"start":9075.2,"t":9169.8,"from":"Day dawned; and I directed my steps towards the …","to":"… to attend their usual hour of rising.","q":"These reflections determined me, and I resolved to remain silent","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor chooses SILENCE: moral failure","b":"Victor could tell the authorities but decides no one would believe him. This choice of silence will cost Justine her life. Mark it as a key decision.","lt":"Ethics","l":"The 'bystander effect' describes people who know something is wrong but don't act. Victor is worse than a bystander: he alone holds the truth."},{"id":88,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[218,219],"start":9170.2,"t":9291.3,"from":"Six years had elapsed, passed in a dream but …","to":"… father, and here I named my cousin.","q":"Six years had elapsed, passed in a dream but for one indelible trace","tag":"Setting","m":"Mother's portrait: grief + guilt at home","b":"Victor returns to his childhood home after six years. The painting of Caroline weeping by her father's coffin echoes the grief now in the house.","lt":"Language","l":"'Indelible' means impossible to erase (in-, not; delere, to delete). The same Latin root gives us 'delete'."},{"id":89,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[220,222],"start":9291.7,"t":9354.1,"from":"“She most of all,” said Ernest, “requires consolation; she …","to":"… of so frightful, so appalling a crime?”","q":"Justine Moritz, who was so amiable, and fond of all the family","tag":"Irony","m":"Justine accused (dramatic irony)","b":"Ernest reveals that Justine has been accused. We know the Creature did it; the family doesn't. Dramatic irony makes the trial agonizing to read.","lt":"History","l":"Before forensic science, trials relied heavily on witnesses and circumstantial evidence. Fingerprinting wasn't used in court until about 1900, and DNA evidence not until the 1980s."},{"id":90,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[223,225],"start":9354.5,"t":9435.7,"from":"“Justine Moritz! Poor, poor girl, is she the accused? …","to":"… measure by her extreme confusion of manner.","q":"the poor girl confirmed the suspicion in a great measure by her extreme confusion of manner","tag":"Theme","m":"Justine's nervousness → seen as guilt","b":"Justine's distress is read as guilt. People judge by outward signs. Compare how the Creature is judged by his looks.","lt":"Psych","l":"Research shows people are bad at detecting lies from behavior; nervousness looks like guilt to observers. Innocent people under pressure often appear 'suspicious'."},{"id":91,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[226,230],"start":9436.1,"t":9503.8,"from":"This was a strange tale, but it did not …","to":"… sincerely hope, that she will be acquitted.”","q":"I know the murderer. Justine, poor, good Justine, is innocent","tag":"Character","m":"Victor knows truth but won't explain it","b":"Victor insists Justine is innocent but refuses to say why. His half-truth helps no one. His father hopes the court will acquit her.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Commas set off an appositive phrase ('poor, good Justine') that renames a noun. They work like a pause that adds detail about the same person."},{"id":92,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[231,232],"start":9504.2,"t":9618.9,"from":"This speech calmed me. I was firmly convinced in …","to":"… the sad death of my little William.”","q":"My tale was not one to announce publicly","tag":"Theme","m":"Fear of being called MAD → stays silent","b":"Victor chooses his reputation over Justine's life. He tells himself that the evidence won't convict her. Is this reasonable caution or cowardice? Make a note of your view.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Whistleblowers face this dilemma: speaking up risks your reputation or career, but silence lets harm continue. Laws now protect some whistleblowers for this reason."},{"id":93,"sec":"Chapter 7","p":[233,235],"start":9619.3,"t":9670.3,"from":"“She is innocent, my Elizabeth,” said I, “and that …","to":"… shall prevent the slightest shadow of partiality.”","q":"rely on the justice of our laws","tag":"Irony","m":"Father trusts justice → it will fail","b":"Alphonse tells Elizabeth to trust the justice system. That is painful irony, because the system is about to condemn an innocent woman.","lt":"History","l":"Wrongful convictions still happen: the US Innocence Project has helped free over 200 people through DNA evidence. Mistaken eyewitnesses and false confessions are leading causes."},{"id":94,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[236,237],"start":9670.7,"t":9809.1,"from":"We passed a few sad hours until eleven o’clock, …","to":"… affection seemed to attest her utter guiltlessness.","q":"During the whole of this wretched mockery of justice I suffered living torture","tag":"Theme","m":"Trial = 'mockery of justice'","b":"Victor watches the trial as a 'mockery'. He frames himself as the one suffering, while Justine faces execution. Notice how often his narration centers on his own pain.","lt":"Language","l":"Oxymoron: 'living torture' combines contrasting ideas. Shelley often pairs life and death words, fitting a novel about bringing the dead to life."},{"id":95,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[238,239],"start":9809.5,"t":9909.9,"from":"The trial began, and after the advocate against her …","to":"… spoke in an audible although variable voice.","q":"Several strange facts combined against her","tag":"Theme","m":"Circumstantial evidence piles up","b":"Justine was out all night near where the body was found, and the miniature was in her pocket. Each fact is innocent alone but damning together. The Creature planted the evidence.","lt":"Science","l":"Circumstantial evidence requires inference; direct evidence (like an eyewitness) does not. Combining several weak clues can feel convincing even when it's wrong, a form of confirmation bias."},{"id":96,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[240,241],"start":9910.3,"t":10015.7,"from":"“God knows,” she said, “how entirely I am innocent. …","to":"… the picture she could give no account.","q":"how entirely I am innocent","tag":"Character","m":"Justine's calm, honest defense","b":"Justine explains where she was: searching for William, locked out, sheltering in a barn. Her explanation is plain and believable, but she can't explain the picture.","lt":"Language","l":"Her speech uses formal legal register ('adduced', 'favourable interpretation'). Register is the level of formality we choose for a situation, as in a courtroom versus texting."},{"id":97,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[242,243],"start":10016.1,"t":10079.8,"from":"“I know,” continued the unhappy victim, “how heavily and …","to":"… would pledge my salvation on my innocence.”","q":"I would pledge my salvation on my innocence","tag":"Theme","m":"Truth isn't enough without proof","b":"Justine's honesty can't overcome the planted evidence. Shelley shows that innocence alone doesn't guarantee justice.","lt":"Ethics","l":"'Innocent until proven guilty' (presumption of innocence) is a principle of modern law. Notice how the burden of proof seems to fall on Justine here."},{"id":98,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[244,245],"start":10080.2,"t":10218.8,"from":"Several witnesses were called who had known her for …","to":"… much do I esteem and value her.”","q":"so much do I esteem and value her","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth testifies = courage","b":"Elizabeth bravely speaks up when other witnesses are too afraid. She is the only one who publicly defends Justine; Victor, who knows the truth, stays silent.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Inversion for emphasis: 'so much do I esteem her' instead of 'I esteem her so much'. Moving the adverb to the front adds weight, as in 'Never have I seen…'"},{"id":99,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[246,247],"start":10219.2,"t":10313.9,"from":"A murmur of approbation followed Elizabeth’s simple and powerful …","to":"… were all black, and Justine was condemned.","q":"they were all black, and Justine was condemned","tag":"Symbol","m":"Black ballots = guilty verdict","b":"Elizabeth's defense backfires: the crowd sees Justine as ungrateful. The verdict comes through black ballots, unanimous and final.","lt":"History","l":"Voting with black and white balls goes back to ancient Athens. It's the origin of 'blackballing', rejecting someone from a club."},{"id":100,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[248,249],"start":10314.3,"t":10371.9,"from":"I cannot pretend to describe what I then felt. …","to":"… home, and Elizabeth eagerly demanded the result.","q":"words cannot convey an idea of the heart-sickening despair","tag":"Narration","m":"Justine 'confessed', Victor stunned","b":"Victor hears that Justine confessed and wonders if he's mad. Note his 'inexpressibility' claim: he says words fail, then describes it anyway.","lt":"Language","l":"The 'inexpressibility topos' (saying something is beyond words) is an ancient rhetorical device. It emphasizes intensity, and people still use it: 'I can't even describe it.'"},{"id":101,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[250,252],"start":10372.3,"t":10446.8,"from":"“My cousin,” replied I, “it is decided as you …","to":"… to me, yet I could not refuse.","q":"all judges had rather that ten innocent should suffer than that one guilty should escape","tag":"Irony","m":"Twists Blackstone's ratio (bitter irony)","b":"Victor bitterly reverses a famous legal principle to show how unjust the court is. Elizabeth asks how she can ever believe in human goodness again.","lt":"History","l":"Blackstone's ratio (1760s): 'better that ten guilty persons escape than that one innocent suffer'. It is a cornerstone of English and American law, and Victor flips it on purpose."},{"id":102,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[253,256],"start":10447.2,"t":10522.3,"from":"We entered the gloomy prison chamber and beheld Justine …","to":"… for a moment, but your own confession.”","q":"her hands were manacled, and her head rested on her knees","tag":"Setting","m":"Prison: Justine in chains","b":"The gloomy prison and the image of chains create a vivid picture of the innocent punished. Justine throws herself at Elizabeth's feet. Note the class difference.","lt":"Art","l":"Gothic fiction loves prisons, dungeons and confinement, which symbolize being trapped by fate or society. Look for other enclosed spaces in the novel."},{"id":103,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[257,258],"start":10522.7,"t":10607.6,"from":"“I did confess, but I confessed a lie. I …","to":"… I am to suffer ignominy and death.”","q":"I confessed, that I might obtain absolution","tag":"Theme","m":"False confession under pressure","b":"Justine confessed a lie because her confessor threatened her with damnation. She was pressured until she almost believed she was 'the monster'. The word choice connects her to the Creature.","lt":"Psych","l":"False confessions are real: about 25% of DNA exonerations involved one. Coercion, exhaustion and fear lead innocent people to confess (Kassin's research)."},{"id":104,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[259,260],"start":10608.0,"t":10668.4,"from":"“Oh, Justine! Forgive me for having for one moment …","to":"… in patience to the will of heaven!”","q":"Learn from me, dear lady, to submit in patience to the will of heaven","tag":"Character","m":"Justine accepts death (passive virtue)","b":"Justine echoes Victor's 'learn from me' but means the opposite: humble acceptance, not ambition. She is a model of passive, saintly virtue.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Compare Socrates, who accepted an unjust death sentence (399 BCE) rather than flee, saying it was right to obey the law. Is accepting injustice noble or tragic?"},{"id":105,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[261,263],"start":10668.8,"t":10750.5,"from":"During this conversation I had retired to a corner …","to":"… by you, dear lady, and your cousin.”","q":"felt not, as I did, such deep and bitter agony","tag":"Character","m":"Victor claims HIS pain > Justine's (selfish)","b":"Victor claims that he suffers more than Justine, who faces execution. This is one of the clearest examples of his self-centeredness. Readers often lose sympathy for him here.","lt":"Psych","l":"Egocentrism is the inability to see beyond your own perspective. Victor reframes every tragedy around himself, which is a red flag for an unreliable narrator."},{"id":106,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[264,265],"start":10750.9,"t":10837.6,"from":"Thus the poor sufferer tried to comfort others and …","to":"… and be happy, and make others so.”","q":"I, the true murderer, felt the never-dying worm alive in my bosom","tag":"Allusion","m":"Victor admits guilt (to us, not court)","b":"Victor calls himself 'the true murderer' but still says nothing. Justine comforts others, Victor only suffers. Innocence and guilt are clearly contrasted.","lt":"Literature","l":"'The worm that dieth not' comes from the Bible (Mark 9:48), describing the torments of hell. Shelley uses biblical allusion to show eternal guilt."},{"id":107,"sec":"Chapter 8","p":[266,268],"start":10838.0,"t":10975.1,"from":"And on the morrow Justine died. Elizabeth’s heart-rending eloquence …","to":"… first hapless victims to my unhallowed arts.","q":"the first hapless victims to my unhallowed arts","tag":"Theme","m":"Justine executed; 2 victims of V.'s science","b":"Justine is executed. Victor calls William and Justine victims of his 'unhallowed arts', admitting responsibility, though he still blames the Creature for acting.","lt":"Language","l":"'Unhallowed' means unholy (hallow = make holy, as in Halloween, 'All Hallows' Eve'). Victor admits his science crossed a sacred line."},{"id":108,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[269,270],"start":10975.5,"t":11085.2,"from":"Nothing is more painful to the human mind than, …","to":"… was my only consolation—deep, dark, deathlike solitude.","q":"solitude was my only consolation—deep, dark, deathlike solitude","tag":"Theme","m":"Isolation deepens (alliteration d-d-d)","b":"After Justine's death Victor shuts himself away. He chooses isolation, the same condition that will drive the Creature to violence.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Alliteration: repeating initial consonant sounds ('deep, dark, deathlike'). It's a sound device that adds rhythm and makes the phrase stick."},{"id":109,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[271,272],"start":11085.6,"t":11169.7,"from":"My father observed with pain the alteration perceptible in …","to":"… endeavour to hide myself from his view.","q":"Do you think, Victor,","tag":"Character","m":"Father: grieve but don't neglect duties","b":"Alphonse argues that excessive grief is selfish because it stops you from helping others. He's right, but he doesn't know the guilt behind Victor's despair.","lt":"Psych","l":"Complicated grief is grief that doesn't ease with time and disrupts daily life. Guilt makes grief harder to resolve, which is Victor's exact situation."},{"id":110,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[273,274],"start":11170.1,"t":11358.1,"from":"About this time we retired to our house at …","to":"… avenge the deaths of William and Justine.","q":"I wished to see him again, that I might wreak the utmost extent of abhorrence on his head","tag":"Theme","m":"Revenge begins; suicidal thoughts on lake","b":"Victor rows alone at night and considers drowning himself, but stays for his family. His hatred of the Creature grows, and revenge starts to define him.","lt":"Science","l":"Lake Geneva's curfew (gates closing at ten) was real: walled cities locked their gates at night for defense. Geneva's walls were only torn down in the 1850s."},{"id":111,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[275,276],"start":11358.5,"t":11527.4,"from":"Our house was the house of mourning. My father’s …","to":"… not change places with such a wretch.”","q":"he walks about the world free, and perhaps respected","tag":"Theme","m":"Elizabeth loses faith in humanity","b":"Elizabeth sees the world as full of wolves now. Her line about the murderer walking free 'and perhaps respected' unknowingly describes Victor himself.","lt":"Language","l":"Situational and dramatic irony together: Elizabeth's words accidentally point to the person beside her. The reader sees what she can't."},{"id":112,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[277,278],"start":11527.8,"t":11612.1,"from":"I listened to this discourse with the extremest agony. …","to":"… been near to rob me of her.","q":"I, not in deed, but in effect, was the true murderer","tag":"Theme","m":"Responsibility: deed vs. effect","b":"Victor distinguishes direct action from causing an outcome. It's a key question for essays: is Victor guilty of what his creation does?","lt":"Ethics","l":"Moral responsibility for consequences is debated in tech today: are social media companies, or AI makers, responsible for harms their products cause? This is the same 'in effect' question."},{"id":113,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[279,280],"start":11612.5,"t":11693.9,"from":"Thus not the tenderness of friendship, nor the beauty …","to":"… changed in those savage and enduring scenes.","q":"The wounded deer dragging its fainting limbs","tag":"Symbol","m":"Wounded deer simile: suffering alone","b":"Victor compares himself to a wounded deer dying alone. He then heads to the Alps for comfort in the 'savage and enduring' mountains.","lt":"Literature","l":"The wounded deer is a classic image of a lonely sufferer, used by Shakespeare ('As You Like It') and the poet William Cowper ('I was a stricken deer that left the herd')."},{"id":114,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[281,282],"start":11694.3,"t":11846.6,"from":"I performed the first part of my journey on …","to":"… and its tremendous _dôme_ overlooked the valley.","q":"Mont Blanc, the supreme and magnificent Mont Blanc","tag":"Setting","m":"Mont Blanc = sublime nature","b":"The journey to Chamounix shows the sublime Alps: glaciers, avalanches, Mont Blanc. Shelley drew on her own 1816 tour of the area.","lt":"Literature","l":"Percy Shelley wrote the poem 'Mont Blanc' on the same 1816 trip. The French words ('aiguilles', needles or sharp peaks; 'dôme') are mountaineering terms still used today."},{"id":115,"sec":"Chapter 9","p":[283,284],"start":11847.0,"t":11942.3,"from":"A tingling long-lost sense of pleasure often came across …","to":"… came and blessed the giver of oblivion.","q":"maternal Nature bade me weep no more","tag":"Theme","m":"Nature as MOTHER: comforts Victor","b":"Nature is personified as a mother comforting Victor. That is ironic: Victor 'gave birth' without a mother, unnaturally. Nature heals what science broke, at least for a moment.","lt":"Science","l":"'Mother Nature' is an ancient personification (Greek Gaia). White noise and nature sounds really do help sleep; the rushing Arve acting as 'a lullaby' fits modern sleep science."},{"id":116,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[285,286],"start":11942.7,"t":12147.3,"from":"I spent the following day roaming through the valley. …","to":"… destroy the solitary grandeur of the scene.","q":"the presence of another would destroy the solitary grandeur of the scene","tag":"Setting","m":"Goes up glacier ALONE (isolation)","b":"Victor goes up Montanvert without a guide because he wants solitude. The glacier is cold, vast and lonely, the perfect stage for meeting the Creature.","lt":"Science","l":"Glaciers are rivers of ice that move slowly downhill under their own weight. The Mer de Glace ('Sea of Ice') near Chamonix is real, and has retreated about 2 km since Shelley's day due to climate change."},{"id":117,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[287,288],"start":12147.7,"t":12299.3,"from":"The ascent is precipitous, but the path is cut …","to":"… his morrow; Nought may endure but mutability!","q":"Nought may endure but mutability!","tag":"Allusion","m":"P. Shelley's 'Mutability': only change lasts","b":"Victor quotes 'Mutability', a poem by Percy Shelley (Mary's husband): the only constant is change. It fits a man whose life has been overturned.","lt":"Language","l":"'Mutability' means changeability (Latin mutare, to change). Related words include mutation, mutate and commute. The poem's idea parallels the Buddhist concept of impermanence."},{"id":118,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[289,290],"start":12299.7,"t":12478.4,"from":"It was nearly noon when I arrived at the …","to":"… words expressive of furious detestation and contempt.","q":"its unearthly ugliness rendered it almost too horrible for human eyes","tag":"Character","m":"★ Victor meets Creature on glacier","b":"The Creature bounds across the ice toward Victor. Victor describes him as 'it' and focuses only on his ugliness, then explodes in rage.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Pronouns reveal attitudes: Victor uses 'it', denying the Creature personhood. Track when characters say 'he' versus 'it' for the Creature; it's great essay evidence."},{"id":119,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[291,292],"start":12478.8,"t":12560.6,"from":"“Devil,” I exclaimed, “do you dare approach me? And …","to":"… with the blood of your remaining friends.”","q":"Begone, vile insect!","tag":"Character","m":"Victor's rage vs. Creature's calm speech","b":"Victor screams insults; the Creature replies calmly and eloquently. For the first time we hear the Creature speak, and he sounds more reasonable than his maker.","lt":"Language","l":"Contrast in dialogue reveals character. The Creature uses balanced, logical sentences and conditionals ('If you will comply… but if you refuse…'). Which of them sounds like the monster?"},{"id":120,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[293,296],"start":12561.0,"t":12681.5,"from":"“Abhorred monster! Fiend that thou art! The tortures of …","to":"… happy, and I shall again be virtuous.”","q":"I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend","tag":"Theme","m":"★ KEY QUOTE: nurture made him a monster","b":"The Creature's central argument: he was born good, and suffering made him evil. 'I ought to be thy Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel.' He compares himself to characters from Paradise Lost.","lt":"Psych","l":"Nature vs. nurture: are we shaped by birth or experience? Rousseau argued humans are born good and corrupted by society. This line is the novel's clearest statement of that idea."},{"id":121,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[297,298],"start":12681.9,"t":12832.4,"from":"“Begone! I will not hear you. There can be …","to":"… will, destroy the work of your hands.”","q":"Oh, praise the eternal justice of man!","tag":"Irony","m":"Creature's sarcasm: human justice hypocrisy","b":"The Creature points out that humans condemn murder yet Victor would 'destroy your own creature' with a clear conscience. His sarcasm exposes human hypocrisy.","lt":"Language","l":"Verbal irony and sarcasm: saying the opposite of what you mean. 'Praise the eternal justice of man' actually condemns it. Tone of voice carries sarcasm in speech, while in writing context must do it."},{"id":122,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[299,300],"start":12832.8,"t":12931.8,"from":"“Why do you call to my remembrance,” I rejoined, …","to":"… the author of your own speedy ruin.”","q":"Cursed be the day, abhorred devil, in which you first saw light!","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature asks Victor to HEAR his story","b":"Victor curses the Creature's birth, the way Job curses his birthday in the Bible. The Creature asks only to be heard, and says Victor's choice will decide whether he lives harmlessly or becomes a scourge.","lt":"Ethics","l":"The right to be heard ('audi alteram partem', hear the other side) is a basic principle of fair justice. Justine got a trial; the Creature asks for the same."},{"id":123,"sec":"Chapter 10","p":[301,301],"start":12932.2,"t":13010.9,"from":"As he said this he led the way across …","to":"… had lighted, he thus began his tale.","q":"compassion confirmed my resolution","tag":"Narration","m":"Victor agrees to listen: Creature's tale begins","b":"Victor feels compassion for the first time and realizes he owes his creation duties. The hut, the fire and a story by the fire set up the novel's innermost narrative.","lt":"Literature","l":"This is the center of the 'Chinese box' or nested structure: Walton → Victor → Creature. The Creature's story sits at the heart of the novel, both literally and morally."},{"id":124,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[302,303],"start":13011.3,"t":13165.2,"from":"“It is with considerable difficulty that I remember the …","to":"… all sides, I sat down and wept.","q":"I sat down and wept","tag":"Character","m":"Creature's birth: confused senses, weeps","b":"The Creature describes waking with confused senses, cold, hungry and alone. He is like a newborn abandoned in the woods. His first act of self-expression is crying.","lt":"Science","l":"Infants really do take months to sort their senses. William James called a baby's world 'one great blooming, buzzing confusion.' The Creature describes exactly that."},{"id":125,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[304,305],"start":13165.6,"t":13283.3,"from":"“Soon a gentle light stole over the heavens and …","to":"… from me frightened me into silence again.","q":"the uncouth and inarticulate sounds which broke from me frightened me into silence again","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature tries to sing like birds","b":"The Creature delights in the moon and birdsong and tries to imitate them. His gentle wonder contrasts with how others see him.","lt":"Science","l":"Language acquisition: babies babble before they speak and learn by imitation. The Creature has an adult body but goes through infant development stages."},{"id":126,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[306,307],"start":13283.7,"t":13404.9,"from":"“The moon had disappeared from the night, and again, …","to":"… on the ground and sank into sleep.","q":"I distinguished the insect from the herb","tag":"Theme","m":"Learns by observation (empiricism)","b":"The Creature learns through experience: he finds fire, learns it burns, and figures out how to keep it alive. His mind develops step by step through his senses.","lt":"History","l":"John Locke's 'tabula rasa' (blank slate, 1690) held that the mind starts empty and is filled by sense experience. The Creature's education reads like Locke's theory in action."},{"id":127,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[308,309],"start":13405.3,"t":13521.0,"from":"“It was morning when I awoke, and my first …","to":"… cold damp substance that covered the ground.","q":"I uncovered it, and a gentle breeze quickly fanned it into a flame","tag":"Symbol","m":"FIRE = Prometheus; gift & danger","b":"The Creature discovers fire: it gives warmth and light, and it burns him. Fire is the symbol of Prometheus (the novel's subtitle), with knowledge that helps and hurts.","lt":"Science","l":"Combustion needs heat, fuel and oxygen (the fire triangle). Fanning works because it adds oxygen. The Creature discovers basic chemistry by trial and error."},{"id":128,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[310,311],"start":13521.4,"t":13716.3,"from":"“It was about seven in the morning, and I …","to":"… agreeable asylum from the snow and rain.","q":"I perceived a small hut","tag":"Theme","m":"1st human contact: old man flees","b":"The shepherd flees in terror at the sight of him, and villagers attack him with stones. His first lessons about humans are violence and rejection, based purely on looks.","lt":"Psych","l":"Children learn social behavior through how others treat them (Bandura's social learning theory). The Creature's early 'lessons' teach him that humans are hostile."},{"id":129,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[312,313],"start":13716.7,"t":13812.3,"from":"“Here, then, I retreated and lay down happy to …","to":"… of the cottage it was tolerably warm.","q":"from the barbarity of man","tag":"Irony","m":"'barbarity of MAN': irony, humans = savage","b":"The Creature calls humans barbaric, reversing Walton's 'savage' label in Letter 4. He sleeps in a 'kennel', treated like an animal.","lt":"Language","l":"'Barbarian' comes from the Greek 'barbaros', meaning someone whose speech sounds like 'bar-bar' (a foreigner). It shows how labels of 'savage' often reflect the speaker's prejudice."},{"id":130,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[314,315],"start":13812.7,"t":14039.3,"from":"“Being thus provided, I resolved to reside in this …","to":"… the window, unable to bear these emotions.","q":"they were a mixture of pain and pleasure","tag":"Theme","m":"Watches De Laceys: learns emotion","b":"Through a chink in the wall he sees the old man play music and the girl weep. He feels empathy, a complex emotion, for the first time.","lt":"Psych","l":"Empathy is feeling what others feel. Mirror neurons are linked to it; we feel others' emotions by simulating them. The Creature's empathy proves he is deeply human."},{"id":131,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[316,317],"start":14039.7,"t":14146.5,"from":"“Soon after this the young man returned, bearing on …","to":"… morning, directed his steps across the fields.","q":"his eyes and attitude expressed the utmost sadness and despondency","tag":"Character","m":"Felix & Agatha introduced (sad, poor)","b":"The young man (Felix) and girl (Agatha) work hard and seem sad. The Creature watches them like a student and learns their routines.","lt":"Language","l":"The Creature is an outsider observer, like an anthropologist doing 'participant observation'. Writers use outsider narrators to make ordinary human life look strange and new."},{"id":132,"sec":"Chapter 11","p":[318,319],"start":14146.9,"t":14220.2,"from":"“Night quickly shut in, but to my extreme wonder, …","to":"… and retired, as I conjectured, to rest.”","q":"I knew nothing of the science of words or letters","tag":"Theme","m":"Reading = a mystery to him","b":"The Creature sees Felix read aloud but doesn't understand it yet. Literacy will become his path to humanity, and to pain.","lt":"History","l":"Literacy rates in early 1800s Europe were low; many poor people couldn't read. Literacy was a marker of class and power, a theme also seen in Frederick Douglass's narrative (1845)."},{"id":133,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[320,322],"start":14220.6,"t":14310.7,"from":"“I lay on my straw, but I could not …","to":"… he rewarded them by his benevolent smiles.","q":"I longed to join them, but dared not","tag":"Theme","m":"Longs to join family; fears rejection","b":"The Creature admires the cottagers' gentleness and love for the old man. He wants to belong, but past violence makes him afraid.","lt":"Psych","l":"Social anxiety: fear of rejection based on past experience. The Creature's caution is learned, not innate."},{"id":134,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[323,324],"start":14311.1,"t":14420.0,"from":"“They were not entirely happy. The young man and …","to":"… man when they reserved none for themselves.","q":"If such lovely creatures were miserable","tag":"Theme","m":"Cottagers poor, sacrifice food for father","b":"The Creature realizes the family is poor and hungry; the young ones give their food to their father. He learns self-sacrifice by watching it.","lt":"History","l":"Rural poverty and food scarcity were common in early-1800s Europe. 1816, the year Shelley wrote Frankenstein, was the 'Year Without a Summer' (after the Tambora volcano), with famine across Europe."},{"id":135,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[325,327],"start":14420.4,"t":14494.1,"from":"“This trait of kindness moved me sensibly. I had …","to":"… repairing the cottage and cultivating the garden.","q":"I abstained and satisfied myself with berries, nuts, and roots","tag":"Character","m":"Creature stops stealing; secretly helps","b":"When he realizes stealing hurts them, he stops and secretly collects firewood for them. This is moral reasoning: he chooses kindness without being taught rules.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Kohlberg's stages of moral development: the Creature reasons from empathy (others' pain), not fear of punishment. That is an advanced moral stage."},{"id":136,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[328,329],"start":14494.5,"t":14699.8,"from":"“By degrees I made a discovery of still greater …","to":"… especially when he addressed the old man.","q":"This was indeed a godlike science","tag":"Theme","m":"Language = 'godlike science'","b":"The Creature discovers that words cause feelings. He calls language 'godlike', which parallels Victor calling his science godlike. Their ambitions differ: one wants connection, the other power.","lt":"Language","l":"Linguists call this pragmatics, how words act on listeners. Speech act theory (J.L. Austin) argues words DO things: promise, hurt, comfort."},{"id":137,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[330,331],"start":14700.2,"t":14828.1,"from":"“I could mention innumerable instances which, although slight, marked …","to":"… to my eyes had made me acquainted.","q":"which knowledge might enable me to make them overlook the deformity of my figure","tag":"Theme","m":"Plan: master language to be accepted","b":"The Creature hopes words can overcome his looks. He believes eloquence can defeat prejudice. Watch whether he's right.","lt":"Psych","l":"First impressions form in about a tenth of a second (Willis & Todorov, 2006). Overcoming a negative first impression takes much more effort than making a good one."},{"id":138,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[332,333],"start":14828.5,"t":14901.1,"from":"“I had admired the perfect forms of my cottagers—their …","to":"… comfort increased daily as the season advanced.","q":"I viewed myself in a transparent pool","tag":"Symbol","m":"Sees his reflection: self-hatred","b":"The Creature sees his reflection and is horrified to realize he is 'the monster'. He has learned to see himself through human eyes.","lt":"Literature","l":"This reverses the myth of Narcissus, who fell in love with his reflection in a pool. The Creature is repulsed by his own reflection, the first step in internalized self-hatred."},{"id":139,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[334,335],"start":14901.5,"t":14977.2,"from":"“The old man, leaning on his son, walked each …","to":"… then understand the signification of these terms.","q":"good spirit, wonderful","tag":"Irony","m":"Cottagers call him 'good spirit' (irony)","b":"The cottagers call their invisible helper a 'good spirit'. Unseen, the Creature is judged by his actions and seen as good. Seen, he would be judged by his looks.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Philosopher John Rawls proposed a 'veil of ignorance': judge fairly without knowing who people are. Invisible, the Creature is judged justly, which shows how looks distort judgment."},{"id":140,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[336,337],"start":14977.6,"t":15074.2,"from":"“My thoughts now became more active, and I longed …","to":"… deserved better treatment than blows and execration.","q":"It was as the ass and the lap-dog","tag":"Allusion","m":"Aesop fable: wrong way to seek affection","b":"The Creature compares his clumsy voice to the donkey in Aesop's fable, who copied a lap-dog's affection and got beaten. He worries his good intentions won't be enough.","lt":"Literature","l":"Aesop's fables (ancient Greece) teach morals through animals. In 'The Ass and the Lapdog', a donkey imitates a pet dog's affection and is beaten for it. The moral: you can't be what you're not."},{"id":141,"sec":"Chapter 12","p":[338,338],"start":15074.6,"t":15132.3,"from":"“The pleasant showers and genial warmth of spring greatly …","to":"… rays of hope and anticipations of joy.”","q":"Happy, happy earth!","tag":"Setting","m":"Spring = hope (Creature's happiest moment)","b":"Spring brings hope; 'the past was blotted from my memory'. Like Victor at the end of Chapter 6, this happiness comes right before disaster.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Epizeuxis: repeating a word immediately ('Happy, happy') for emotional emphasis. Compare 'Never, never, never' in King Lear."},{"id":142,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[339,341],"start":15132.7,"t":15199.7,"from":"“I now hasten to the more moving part of …","to":"… music when someone tapped at the door.","q":"have made me what I am","tag":"Narration","m":"Creature signals a turning point","b":"The Creature warns that these events 'made me what I am'. He frames his story as cause and effect, the opposite of Victor's 'destiny' explanation.","lt":"Psych","l":"Developmental psychology studies how experiences shape personality. The Creature explains himself through experience; Victor explains himself through fate. Which is more convincing?"},{"id":143,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[342,343],"start":15200.1,"t":15322.3,"from":"“It was a lady on horseback, accompanied by a …","to":"… he raised her and embraced her affectionately.","q":"a thick black veil","tag":"Character","m":"Safie arrives (veiled stranger)","b":"A veiled woman arrives on horseback and says 'Felix'. This is Safie, whose arrival transforms the family.","lt":"History","l":"Veils were worn by many women in the Ottoman Empire, and also by European women for travel and mourning. Safie's veil marks her as foreign and mysterious."},{"id":144,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[344,345],"start":15322.7,"t":15438.0,"from":"“I soon perceived that although the stranger uttered articulate …","to":"… that purpose, but found it utterly impossible.","q":"as the sun dissipates the morning mists","tag":"Theme","m":"Language barrier: Safie can't speak French","b":"Safie doesn't speak French, so she, like the Creature, must learn the language. This is a perfect parallel: the Creature will learn alongside her.","lt":"Language","l":"Sign language and gesture are natural first bridges between languages. A pidgin is a simplified shared language that develops when groups without a common tongue must communicate."},{"id":145,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[346,347],"start":15438.4,"t":15498.1,"from":"“The next morning Felix went out to his work, …","to":"… him the greatest delight by her music.","q":"the Arabian sat at the feet of the old man","tag":"Theme","m":"Music = universal language","b":"Safie plays guitar and sings, moving everyone, including the Creature, to tears. Music communicates where words cannot.","lt":"Science","l":"Studies of music across 300+ cultures (Mehr et al., 2019) find universal patterns: lullabies and dance songs are recognizable worldwide. Music truly crosses languages."},{"id":146,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[348,349],"start":15498.5,"t":15562.5,"from":"“The days now passed as peaceably as before, with …","to":"… in the first village which I entered.","q":"she and I improved rapidly in the knowledge of language","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature learns via Safie's lessons","b":"The Creature learns French by eavesdropping on Safie's lessons, a secret classroom. He never goes out in daylight for fear of attack.","lt":"Science","l":"'Comprehensible input' (Stephen Krashen): we learn languages by hearing speech we can mostly understand. The Creature learns by listening, the way children do."},{"id":147,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[350,352],"start":15562.9,"t":15664.9,"from":"“My days were spent in close attention, that I …","to":"… the hapless fate of its original inhabitants.","q":"wept with Safie over the hapless fate of its original inhabitants","tag":"History","m":"Learns history via Volney's 'Ruins'","b":"Felix teaches from Volney's 'Ruins of Empires', a history of civilizations. The Creature weeps over the fate of Native Americans, showing empathy for other outsiders.","lt":"History","l":"Volney's 'Ruins of Empires' (1791) was a radical book arguing that empires fall through tyranny and inequality. Its account of colonial conquest shaped Romantic political thought."},{"id":148,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[353,354],"start":15665.3,"t":15754.0,"from":"“These wonderful narrations inspired me with strange feelings. Was …","to":"… poverty, of rank, descent, and noble blood.","q":"Was man, indeed, at once so powerful, so virtuous and magnificent, yet so vicious and base?","tag":"Theme","m":"Human nature = contradictory (good+evil)","b":"The Creature is shocked that humans can be both noble and evil. He also learns about property, wealth, rank and class, the structures that exclude him.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Philosophers debate whether humans are basically good (Rousseau) or selfish (Hobbes: life is 'nasty, brutish and short'). The Creature sees evidence for both."},{"id":149,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[355,356],"start":15754.4,"t":15851.8,"from":"“The words induced me to turn towards myself. I …","to":"… the sensations of hunger, thirst, and heat!","q":"sorrow only increased with knowledge","tag":"Theme","m":"Knowledge brings pain (Ecclesiastes)","b":"The Creature learns he has no family, money or name, so by society's rules he is nothing. Knowledge makes him miserable, echoing Victor's warning about knowledge.","lt":"Literature","l":"Ecclesiastes 1:18: 'He that increaseth knowledge increaseth sorrow.' Both Victor and the Creature find that knowing more makes them suffer more."},{"id":150,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[357,358],"start":15852.2,"t":15955.3,"from":"“Of what a strange nature is knowledge! It clings …","to":"… human being to another in mutual bonds.","q":"It clings to the mind when it has once seized on it like a lichen on the rock","tag":"Symbol","m":"Simile: knowledge = lichen (can't remove)","b":"Knowledge can't be unlearned. The Creature wishes he could forget. He learns about family bonds, parents and children, and realizes he has none.","lt":"Science","l":"Lichen is a symbiosis of a fungus and an alga (or cyanobacteria). It clings to bare rock and can live for centuries. That makes it an apt image for knowledge you can't scrape off."},{"id":151,"sec":"Chapter 13","p":[359,360],"start":15955.7,"t":16030.9,"from":"“But where were my friends and relations? No father …","to":"… an innocent, half-painful self-deceit, to call them).”","q":"No father had watched my infant days, no mother had blessed me with smiles and caresses","tag":"Theme","m":"★ 'No father… no mother': abandoned child","b":"The Creature realizes he has no parents and no childhood. Compare this to Victor's loving childhood in Chapter 1. The contrast is deliberate. Then he asks the big question: 'What was I?'","lt":"Psych","l":"Children raised without consistent caregivers (as in Romanian orphanages studied in the 1990s) show lasting emotional effects. Early care shapes the developing brain."},{"id":152,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[361,362],"start":16031.3,"t":16093.3,"from":"“Some time elapsed before I learned the history of …","to":"… accompanied by a moderate fortune, could afford.","q":"The name of the old man was De Lacey","tag":"Character","m":"De Laceys were rich in Paris → fell","b":"The cottagers were once a wealthy, respected Paris family. Their fall from fortune parallels the novel's many falls, and shows that misfortune can strike the virtuous.","lt":"History","l":"Wealthy families could lose everything through political persecution in pre-Revolutionary and Revolutionary France. Shelley sets this in a world of unjust governments."},{"id":153,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[363,364],"start":16093.7,"t":16209.0,"from":"“The father of Safie had been the cause of …","to":"… would fully reward his toil and hazard.","q":"He was a Turkish merchant","tag":"Theme","m":"Safie's father unjustly imprisoned","b":"Safie's father is condemned, the Creature suspects, because of his religion and wealth, not his guilt. Injustice by prejudice again, like Justine and the Creature.","lt":"History","l":"The Ottoman Empire (Turkey) and Christian Europe had centuries of conflict and trade. Muslims in Europe often faced religious prejudice, a backdrop that shapes this subplot."},{"id":154,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[365,366],"start":16209.4,"t":16267.6,"from":"“The Turk quickly perceived the impression that his daughter …","to":"… time she gently deplored her own fate.","q":"Felix was too delicate to accept this offer","tag":"Character","m":"Felix helps Turk; Safie = 'reward'?","b":"Felix rescues the merchant out of justice, then falls for Safie. The father offers his daughter as payment, treating her as property, like Victor's 'gift' of Elizabeth.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Arranged marriages and daughters as 'payment' reflect a time when women had few legal rights. Mary Wollstonecraft's 'A Vindication of the Rights of Woman' (1792) attacked this."},{"id":155,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[367,368],"start":16268.0,"t":16366.8,"from":"“I have copies of these letters, for I found …","to":"… rank in society was enchanting to her.","q":"women were allowed to take a rank in society","tag":"Character","m":"Safie = independent woman (mother's lessons)","b":"Safie's Christian Arab mother taught her to aspire to independence of mind. Safie is the most independent woman in the novel; she later travels alone across Europe.","lt":"History","l":"Mary Shelley's mother, Wollstonecraft, argued women should be educated as rational beings. Safie's mother's teaching echoes Wollstonecraft's ideas directly."},{"id":156,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[369,371],"start":16367.2,"t":16452.4,"from":"“The day for the execution of the Turk was …","to":"… the divine airs of her native country.","q":"Felix had procured passports in the name of his father, sister, and himself","tag":"History","m":"Felix's prison-break plan","b":"Felix helps the Turk escape the night before his execution. His virtue involves breaking an unjust law. Is that right? The novel suggests yes.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Civil disobedience means breaking unjust laws on principle. Thoreau (1849) and Martin Luther King Jr.'s 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) argue there's a duty to disobey unjust laws."},{"id":157,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[372,373],"start":16452.8,"t":16563.8,"from":"“The Turk allowed this intimacy to take place and …","to":"… De Lacey and Agatha by this proceeding.","q":"he had formed far other plans","tag":"Theme","m":"Turk betrays Felix = ingratitude","b":"The Turk secretly plans to break his promise. Felix turns himself in to free his family. Ingratitude is one of the cruelest acts in this novel; the Creature notes it carefully.","lt":"Language","l":"'Far other plans' is an older construction meaning 'very different plans'. Modern English would say 'quite different plans'."},{"id":158,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[374,376],"start":16564.2,"t":16641.8,"from":"“He did not succeed. They remained confined for five …","to":"… now infused new life into his soul.","q":"condemned them to a perpetual exile","tag":"Theme","m":"De Laceys exiled & poor for doing right","b":"The family loses its fortune and is exiled for helping an innocent man. Doing good was punished, which complicates the Creature's lessons about virtue.","lt":"History","l":"Exile was a common punishment for political offenses. Victor Hugo spent 19 years in exile from France, and Napoleon was exiled twice (Elba, then St Helena)."},{"id":159,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[377,378],"start":16642.2,"t":16701.1,"from":"“When the news reached Leghorn that Felix was deprived …","to":"… which had not yet arrived at Leghorn.","q":"The generous nature of Safie was outraged by this command","tag":"Character","m":"Safie defies father (agency)","b":"Safie refuses her father's command and protests. Unlike Elizabeth or Justine, she acts on her own judgment.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'Expostulate' means to argue strongly in protest. Formal Latin-root verbs like this (ex- + postulare, to demand) were typical of educated 19th-century prose."},{"id":160,"sec":"Chapter 14","p":[379,380],"start":16701.5,"t":16795.2,"from":"“When alone, Safie resolved in her own mind the …","to":"… safety at the cottage of her lover.”","q":"A residence in Turkey was abhorrent to her","tag":"Character","m":"Safie travels alone to find Felix","b":"Safie escapes with jewels and money, travels across Europe with a servant who dies, and still finds Felix. She is brave, resourceful and self-directed.","lt":"History","l":"Women travelling alone was rare and risky then. Mary Wollstonecraft travelled through Scandinavia with her baby in 1795 and wrote a travel book about it, an inspiration for Safie."},{"id":161,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[381,383],"start":16795.6,"t":16895.3,"from":"“Such was the history of my beloved cottagers. It …","to":"… friends were employed in their ordinary occupations.","q":"Paradise Lost","tag":"Allusion","m":"3 books: Paradise Lost, Plutarch, Werter","b":"The Creature finds three books that shape his worldview. Each teaches him something different about emotion, virtue and his own origin.","lt":"Literature","l":"Paradise Lost (Milton, 1667) is an epic of Satan's fall and Adam's creation. Plutarch's Lives (around 100 CE) are biographies of Greek and Roman leaders. The Sorrows of Young Werther (Goethe, 1774) tells of a sensitive young man's despair."},{"id":162,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[384,385],"start":16895.7,"t":17035.5,"from":"“I can hardly describe to you the effect of …","to":"… but I was unable to solve them.","q":"Who was I? What was I? Whence did I come? What was my destination?","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Identity questions (existential)","b":"Werther teaches him about feeling and death. Then he asks the deepest questions: who, what, where from, where to. These are the questions of every human being.","lt":"Grammar","l":"A series of short rhetorical questions creates urgency. In philosophy these are existential questions; existentialists like Sartre argued we must create our own meaning."},{"id":163,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[386,387],"start":17035.9,"t":17229.1,"from":"“The volume of Plutarch’s Lives which I possessed contained …","to":"… bitter gall of envy rose within me.","q":"Many times I considered Satan as the fitter emblem of my condition","tag":"Allusion","m":"Creature = Adam? No, Satan (envy)","b":"Like Adam he was created alone, but Adam had a loving Creator. Like Satan he feels envy at others' bliss. Victor, by abandoning him, has turned an Adam into a Satan.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Paradise Lost, Satan says 'Myself am Hell' and envies Adam and Eve's happiness in Eden. The Creature identifies with Satan's alienation, not his evil."},{"id":164,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[388,389],"start":17229.5,"t":17394.1,"from":"“Another circumstance strengthened and confirmed these feelings. Soon after …","to":"… months should have added to my sagacity.","q":"I discovered some papers in the pocket of the dress which I had taken from your laboratory","tag":"Theme","m":"Reads Victor's journal = learns truth","b":"The Creature finds Victor's lab journal in the coat he took and learns exactly how he was made and how Victor was disgusted by him. It's the cruelest kind of knowledge.","lt":"Psych","l":"Discovering you were unwanted is devastating. Adoption researchers note that how origin stories are told to children deeply affects their self-worth."},{"id":165,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[390,391],"start":17394.5,"t":17499.0,"from":"“Several changes, in the meantime, took place in the …","to":"… bitterness of my heart I cursed him.","q":"no Eve soothed my sorrows nor shared my thoughts","tag":"Theme","m":"No Eve: Creature needs a companion","b":"Adam had Eve; the Creature has no one. 'Where was mine?' His Creator abandoned him, and he curses Victor. This sets up his demand for a female companion.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Genesis 2:18 God says 'It is not good that the man should be alone' and creates Eve. The Creature asks for the same mercy God showed Adam."},{"id":166,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[392,393],"start":17499.4,"t":17652.8,"from":"“Autumn passed thus. I saw, with surprise and grief, …","to":"… means be tolerated by my younger protectors.","q":"if in the absence of his children I could gain the good will and mediation of the old De Lacey","tag":"Theme","m":"Plan: approach BLIND De Lacey first","b":"The Creature's plan is clever: the blind father can't see his face and will judge only his words. It's a test of whether character can win over prejudice.","lt":"Ethics","l":"'Blind' auditions: orchestras that started auditioning musicians behind screens (from the 1970s) hired far more women. Removing sight removes bias, exactly the Creature's logic."},{"id":167,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[394,395],"start":17653.2,"t":17741.9,"from":"“One day, when the sun shone on the red …","to":"… I approached the door of their cottage.","q":"with renewed determination I approached the door of their cottage","tag":"Character","m":"Courage: Creature approaches cottage","b":"The old man plays a sad song. The Creature nearly loses courage but gathers his strength. This is the most suspenseful moment of his story.","lt":"Psych","l":"Rejection sensitivity is the anxious expectation of rejection. People who have been rejected often freeze before reaching out; the Creature's hesitation is deeply human."},{"id":168,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[396,400],"start":17742.3,"t":17802.8,"from":"“I knocked. ‘Who is there?’ said the old man. …","to":"… interview, when the old man addressed me.","q":"I am a traveller in want of a little rest","tag":"Character","m":"Creature speaks politely to De Lacey","b":"The Creature introduces himself with perfect manners. Without his appearance, he is simply a polite traveller, and De Lacey welcomes him warmly.","lt":"Language","l":"Polite forms ('you would greatly oblige me if…') use conditional mood to soften requests. Linguists call this negative politeness: avoiding imposing on the listener."},{"id":169,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[401,405],"start":17803.2,"t":17867.6,"from":"‘By your language, stranger, I suppose you are my …","to":"… are good and amiable, do not despair.’","q":"the hearts of men, when unprejudiced by any obvious self-interest, are full of brotherly love and charity","tag":"Irony","m":"De Lacey: people are good (irony!)","b":"De Lacey believes people are basically kind. He is about to be proven tragically wrong by his own son.","lt":"Ethics","l":"This reflects Enlightenment optimism about human nature. Compare Anne Frank: 'In spite of everything, I still believe that people are really good at heart.' Is the novel optimistic or pessimistic?"},{"id":170,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[406,410],"start":17868.0,"t":17926.3,"from":"“‘They are kind—they are the most excellent creatures in …","to":"“‘Near this spot.’","q":"a fatal prejudice clouds their eyes","tag":"Theme","m":"★ 'Fatal prejudice clouds their eyes'","b":"The Creature describes his whole problem: people see 'a detestable monster' instead of a kind friend. It's a statement about prejudice itself.","lt":"History","l":"Prejudice based on appearance includes racism and discrimination against people with disabilities or disfigurements. Some critics read the Creature as a symbol of any marginalized group."},{"id":171,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[411,413],"start":17926.7,"t":17994.2,"from":"“The old man paused and then continued, ‘If you …","to":"… I do not feel for your misfortunes.’","q":"I am blind and cannot judge of your countenance","tag":"Theme","m":"Blind man = only one to see truly","b":"De Lacey, who can't see, is the only person who judges the Creature fairly. There's a paradox: the blind man sees the truth that sighted people miss.","lt":"Literature","l":"The wise blind figure is ancient: Tiresias, the blind prophet in Oedipus Rex, sees the truth others can't. Shelley uses the same paradox."},{"id":172,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[414,416],"start":17994.6,"t":18057.0,"from":"“‘How can I thank you, my best and only …","to":"… desert me in the hour of trial!’","q":"From your lips first have I heard the voice of kindness directed towards me","tag":"Theme","m":"1st kindness ever: from a blind man","b":"This is the first time anyone has spoken kindly to him. Then the family returns, and he begs De Lacey: 'Save and protect me!'","lt":"Psych","l":"Research finds a single supportive adult can change a struggling child's life ('Harvard Center on the Developing Child'). De Lacey nearly becomes that person."},{"id":173,"sec":"Chapter 15","p":[417,418],"start":18057.4,"t":18127.5,"from":"“‘Great God!’ exclaimed the old man. ‘Who are you?’","to":"… general tumult escaped unperceived to my hovel.”","q":"Who can describe their horror and consternation on beholding me?","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Rejected by De Laceys: turning point","b":"Agatha faints, Safie flees, and Felix beats him. The Creature could have torn Felix apart but refrains. This rejection is the turning point that breaks his hope.","lt":"Psych","l":"Neuroscientist Naomi Eisenberger found social rejection activates the same brain areas as physical pain. 'Hurt feelings' are literally pain."},{"id":174,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[419,420],"start":18127.9,"t":18214.0,"from":"“Cursed, cursed creator! Why did I live? Why, in …","to":"… have sat down and enjoyed the ruin.","q":"I, like the arch-fiend, bore a hell within me","tag":"Allusion","m":"Creature becomes Satan: rage begins","b":"Now he truly identifies with Satan ('the arch-fiend'). His rage turns outward: he wants to destroy. Notice the shift from victim to avenger.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Paradise Lost, Satan says 'Which way I fly is Hell; myself am Hell.' Hell as an inner state, not a place, is a powerful idea the Creature borrows."},{"id":175,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[421,423],"start":18214.4,"t":18313.0,"from":"“But this was a luxury of sensation that could …","to":"… my representations win him to my party.","q":"I did not believe my errors to be irretrievable","tag":"Character","m":"Still hopeful: plans to try again","b":"Even after the beating, he blames his own timing and hopes to try again. His persistence is heartbreaking.","lt":"Psych","l":"Self-blame after rejection is common; people often think 'I did it wrong' rather than 'they were unfair'. That protects hope but harms self-esteem."},{"id":176,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[424,425],"start":18313.4,"t":18377.4,"from":"“These thoughts calmed me, and in the afternoon I …","to":"… cannot describe the agony of this suspense.","q":"I cannot describe the agony of this suspense","tag":"Narration","m":"Suspense: cottagers don't come out","b":"He waits all morning and the cottage stays silent. Shelley stretches the suspense.","lt":"Language","l":"Pacing slows in suspense scenes: short events stretched across longer descriptions. Filmmakers do the same with slow motion and lingering shots."},{"id":177,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[426,428],"start":18377.8,"t":18451.9,"from":"“Presently two countrymen passed by, but pausing near the …","to":"… and let me fly from this place.’","q":"Take possession of your tenement and let me fly from this place","tag":"Theme","m":"De Laceys flee forever","b":"Felix breaks the lease and leaves. His sister and Safie 'will never recover from their horror'. The Creature's one hope is gone forever.","lt":"Language","l":"'Tenement' originally meant any rented dwelling; today it suggests a run-down apartment building. This is another example of words changing meaning over time."},{"id":178,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[429,430],"start":18452.3,"t":18531.6,"from":"“Felix trembled violently as he said this. He and …","to":"… moon had sunk to commence my operations.","q":"I placed a variety of combustibles around the cottage","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature's 1st destructive act (no harm to ppl)","b":"In rage he destroys the garden and prepares to burn the cottage. It's destruction of property, not people, at least for now.","lt":"Psych","l":"Displaced aggression: anger aimed at something other than its true cause. He can't punish the family, so he attacks objects."},{"id":179,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[431,432],"start":18532.0,"t":18592.5,"from":"“As the night advanced, a fierce wind arose from …","to":"… and sought for refuge in the woods.","q":"licked it with their forked and destroying tongues","tag":"Symbol","m":"Burns cottage: fire = destruction","b":"Fire, which once gave him warmth and comfort, now destroys. The Promethean gift turns violent.","lt":"Language","l":"Personification and metaphor: flames 'licked' with 'forked tongues', like serpents. It links the fire back to the serpent of Eden."},{"id":180,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[433,434],"start":18592.9,"t":18693.2,"from":"“And now, with the world before me, whither should …","to":"… other being that wore the human form.","q":"I learned from your papers that you were my father","tag":"Theme","m":"Seeks Victor: 'my father,' 'my creator'","b":"The Creature turns to his only possible family: Victor. He seeks justice from the one being who owes him care.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Parental responsibility is a legal and moral duty. Laws on child abandonment and child support exist because society agrees parents owe their children care."},{"id":181,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[435,436],"start":18693.6,"t":18836.2,"from":"“My travels were long and the sufferings I endured …","to":"… sun, which bestowed such joy upon me.","q":"dared to be happy","tag":"Setting","m":"Spring brings brief joy again","b":"Travelling to Geneva at night, he suffers in winter, but spring makes him briefly happy again. Nature still moves him.","lt":"Science","l":"Seasonal affective disorder (SAD) shows light and seasons affect mood; spring sunlight raises serotonin. The Creature's spring joy is biologically real."},{"id":182,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[437,438],"start":18836.6,"t":18960.4,"from":"“I continued to wind among the paths of the …","to":"… me; my pulses paused, and I fainted.","q":"I vowed eternal hatred and vengeance to all mankind","tag":"Theme","m":"Saves girl → SHOT. Vows revenge on all","b":"He rescues a drowning girl, and her companion shoots him. This is the final betrayal: his good deed is punished, and he swears eternal vengeance.","lt":"Ethics","l":"'No good deed goes unpunished' is an ironic saying. Good Samaritan laws now protect people who help in emergencies from being sued, recognizing helpers shouldn't be harmed."},{"id":183,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[439,440],"start":18960.8,"t":19015.3,"from":"“For some weeks I led a miserable life in …","to":"… not made for the enjoyment of pleasure.","q":"I was not made for the enjoyment of pleasure","tag":"Theme","m":"Wounded; joy becomes a 'mockery'","b":"With a bullet in his shoulder, he concludes he wasn't made for happiness. His hatred is now settling into his identity.","lt":"Psych","l":"Learned helplessness (Martin Seligman): after repeated bad outcomes, people stop believing they can change anything. The Creature has stopped expecting good."},{"id":184,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[441,443],"start":19015.7,"t":19081.4,"from":"“But my toils now drew near a close, and …","to":"… be so desolate in this peopled earth.","q":"this little creature was unprejudiced","tag":"Theme","m":"Hopes a CHILD won't be prejudiced","b":"He sees a boy and reasons that a child is too young to have learned prejudice. He plans to raise the boy as a friend. His logic is innocent, but his methods are not.","lt":"Psych","l":"Research suggests prejudice is learned: babies show some preferences early, but stronger bias develops through social learning. The Creature's theory is partly right."},{"id":185,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[444,448],"start":19081.8,"t":19144.2,"from":"“Urged by this impulse, I seized on the boy …","to":"… revenge; you shall be my first victim.’","q":"My papa is a syndic—he is M. Frankenstein","tag":"Theme","m":"The boy is WILLIAM Frankenstein","b":"The child screams 'monster' and names his father: Frankenstein. The coincidence turns the Creature's hope into revenge. Notice that the child already has the prejudice he hoped wouldn't exist.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Dashes in dialogue show a speaker rushing, breaking off or panicking. William's speech is fragmented: 'My papa is a syndic—he is M. Frankenstein—he will punish you.'"},{"id":186,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[449,451],"start":19144.6,"t":19217.2,"from":"“The child still struggled and loaded me with epithets …","to":"… to one expressive of disgust and affright.","q":"I grasped his throat to silence him, and in a moment he lay dead at my feet","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Creature kills William; takes locket","b":"The murder is quick, and the Creature feels 'hellish triumph': he can create desolation too. He takes the miniature of Caroline and is moved, then enraged, by her beauty.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Is the Creature fully responsible? Courts consider 'mitigating circumstances', factors like abuse or provocation that reduce blame without excusing a crime."},{"id":187,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[452,453],"start":19217.6,"t":19279.8,"from":"“Can you wonder that such thoughts transported me with …","to":"… affection from thine eyes; my beloved, awake!’","q":"Awake, fairest, thy lover is near","tag":"Character","m":"Finds sleeping Justine in barn","b":"He finds Justine asleep and whispers to her like a lover. He longs for the affection he knows she would never give him.","lt":"Language","l":"'Thy' and 'thine' are archaic second-person forms (informal 'you'). Ironically, they were once used for intimacy, as here, and also in prayer to God."},{"id":188,"sec":"Chapter 16","p":[454,455],"start":19280.2,"t":19384.2,"from":"“The sleeper stirred; a thrill of terror ran through …","to":"… same defects. This being you must create.”","q":"This being you must create","tag":"Theme","m":"Frames Justine; DEMANDS a female mate","b":"He plants the miniature on Justine, deliberately framing her: 'not I, but she, shall suffer.' Then he makes his demand: create a female companion like himself.","lt":"Ethics","l":"This is the novel's ethical dilemma: should Victor create a second being? Critics debate whether the Creature's request is just, or whether creating a mate risks a 'race of devils'."},{"id":189,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[456,459],"start":19384.6,"t":19459.0,"from":"The being finished speaking and fixed his looks upon …","to":"… torture me, but I will never consent.”","q":"you may torture me, but I will never consent","tag":"Character","m":"Victor refuses at first","b":"Victor refuses, fearing 'joint wickedness'. His refusal is based on fear of what two Creatures might do.","lt":"Science","l":"This echoes debates on de-extinction and genetic engineering: should scientists create new species? What if they reproduce? Ecologists warn of invasive species."},{"id":190,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[460,461],"start":19459.4,"t":19553.3,"from":"“You are in the wrong,” replied the fiend; “and …","to":"… but presently he calmed himself and proceeded—","q":"I am malicious because I am miserable","tag":"Theme","m":"★ 'Malicious because miserable'","b":"The Creature's thesis in one line: his evil is caused by his misery. Treat him well, and he'd be good. He threatens to 'desolate' Victor's heart if refused.","lt":"Psych","l":"The frustration–aggression hypothesis (Dollard, 1939): blocked goals and suffering lead to aggression. Criminologists study how deprivation and exclusion predict violence."},{"id":191,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[462,463],"start":19553.7,"t":19653.6,"from":"“I intended to reason. This passion is detrimental to …","to":"… saw my change of feeling and continued,","q":"I would make peace with the whole kind!","tag":"Theme","m":"One kindness = peace with all mankind","b":"For one creature's love, he'd forgive everyone. Victor starts to feel he does owe his creation some happiness, and his position begins to change.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Hyperbole and repetition ('a hundred and a hundredfold') show intense emotion. Italic 'you' shows emphasis; in speech this would be vocal stress."},{"id":192,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[464,465],"start":19654.0,"t":19748.2,"from":"“If you consent, neither you nor any other human …","to":"… argue the point, for I cannot consent.”","q":"I will go to the vast wilds of South America","tag":"Character","m":"Promises exile to S. America; vegetarian","b":"He promises to leave humanity forever and live peacefully on acorns and berries. He is vegetarian and gentle by nature, which undercuts the 'monster' label.","lt":"History","l":"Percy Shelley wrote pamphlets promoting vegetarianism (1813). South America represented untamed wilderness to Europeans; Humboldt's explorations there (1799–1804) were famous."},{"id":193,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[466,467],"start":19748.6,"t":19818.5,"from":"“How inconstant are your feelings! But a moment ago …","to":"… was yet in my power to bestow.","q":"How inconstant are your feelings!","tag":"Character","m":"Victor wavers: sympathy vs. disgust","b":"Victor feels sympathy when he listens, but disgust when he looks. Sight overpowers reason, the same pattern as everyone else in the novel.","lt":"Psych","l":"Cognitive dissonance (Festinger, 1957): holding two conflicting beliefs causes discomfort. Victor flips between pity and hatred because he can't hold both."},{"id":194,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[468,469],"start":19818.9,"t":19875.1,"from":"“You swear,” I said, “to be harmless; but have …","to":"… events from which I am now excluded.”","q":"I shall feel the affections of a sensitive being and become linked to the chain of existence","tag":"Theme","m":"Wants to be 'linked to chain of existence'","b":"The Creature argues that companionship will make him virtuous; isolation is what made him vicious. He wants to belong to the community of living things.","lt":"History","l":"The 'Great Chain of Being' was a medieval and Renaissance idea ranking all things from God to rocks. The Creature has no place in the chain, which is a profound exclusion."},{"id":195,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[470,471],"start":19875.5,"t":19946.9,"from":"I paused some time to reflect on all he …","to":"… who will accompany you in your exile.”","q":"I consent to your demand","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor AGREES to make a female","b":"Victor agrees, with conditions: the pair must leave Europe forever. He reasons that justice requires it. Note it as a moment of moral growth that he will later reverse.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Contract ethics: promises create obligations. When Victor breaks this promise later, is he justified by future risk, or is he betraying a just agreement?"},{"id":196,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[472,474],"start":19947.3,"t":20065.1,"from":"“I swear,” he cried, “by the sun, and by …","to":"… depart, depart, and leave me in darkness.”","q":"by the sun, and by the blue sky of heaven, and by the fire of love that burns my heart","tag":"Character","m":"Creature swears an oath (polysyndeton)","b":"The Creature swears by nature and love. Victor, alone on the mountain, despairs, and the stars seem to mock him.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Polysyndeton: repeating 'and' ('by the sun, and by… and by…') to slow down and add solemnity. The opposite is asyndeton (leaving out 'and'): 'I came, I saw, I conquered.'"},{"id":197,"sec":"Chapter 17","p":[475,476],"start":20065.5,"t":20153.1,"from":"These were wild and miserable thoughts, but I cannot …","to":"… had to me the reality of life.","q":"I resolved to dedicate myself to my most abhorred task","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Victor returns home; dreads the task","b":"Victor returns home haggard and resolves to do the work to save his family. But he dreads it, and that dread will grow into procrastination and fear.","lt":"Language","l":"A 'siroc' (sirocco) is a hot Mediterranean wind from the Sahara. Shelley uses vivid, exotic vocabulary to describe Victor's mental storm."},{"id":198,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[477,478],"start":20153.5,"t":20277.6,"from":"Day after day, week after week, passed away on …","to":"… father, calling me aside, thus addressed me,","q":"I could not collect the courage to recommence my work","tag":"Character","m":"Victor procrastinates: dread & delay","b":"Weeks pass and Victor can't start. His avoidance of responsibility is a pattern: he delays, hides, and hopes problems vanish.","lt":"Psych","l":"Procrastination is often emotional avoidance, not laziness: we delay tasks that trigger negative feelings (Tim Pychyl's research). Victor's dread is extreme procrastination."},{"id":199,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[479,481],"start":20278.0,"t":20363.1,"from":"“I am happy to remark, my dear son, that …","to":"… poignant misery which you appear to feel.”","q":"considering yourself as bound in honour to Elizabeth","tag":"Character","m":"Father thinks V. doesn't want to marry E.","b":"Alphonse misreads Victor's gloom as reluctance to marry Elizabeth. Because Victor keeps secrets, everyone misunderstands him.","lt":"Language","l":"Misunderstanding drives plots in comedy and tragedy alike. Shakespeare's plots (e.g. Romeo and Juliet) often hinge on information one character has and another lacks."},{"id":200,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[482,483],"start":20363.5,"t":20451.2,"from":"“My dear father, reassure yourself. I love my cousin …","to":"… I conjure you, with confidence and sincerity.”","q":"My future hopes and prospects are entirely bound up in the expectation of our union","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor says he loves Elizabeth","b":"Victor insists he loves Elizabeth. Alphonse proposes an immediate wedding. Victor's secret makes the happy plan feel ominous.","lt":"History","l":"Marrying a cousin or adopted sibling was legal and fairly common in 1800s Europe; Charles Darwin married his first cousin. Attitudes have shifted since."},{"id":201,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[484,485],"start":20451.6,"t":20592.0,"from":"I listened to my father in silence and remained …","to":"… an end to my slavery for ever.","q":"some accident might meanwhile occur to destroy him","tag":"Character","m":"Victor wishes problem would vanish","b":"Victor wants to finish his task first, or hopes the monster will simply die. Wishful thinking replaces action again.","lt":"Psych","l":"Magical thinking is believing a problem will resolve itself without action. It's a common coping strategy that rarely works."},{"id":202,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[486,487],"start":20592.4,"t":20691.3,"from":"These feelings dictated my answer to my father. I …","to":"… my task or to contemplate its progress?","q":"I expressed a wish to visit England","tag":"Theme","m":"Goes to England (hides real reason)","b":"Victor lies about his reasons for travelling; he needs English scientists' knowledge. Clerval will join him. Victor even hopes Henry will shield him from the monster.","lt":"Science","l":"Britain was a world center of science around 1800: the Royal Society, Humphry Davy's electrochemistry, and the Royal Institution's public lectures drew crowds."},{"id":203,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[488,489],"start":20691.7,"t":20781.2,"from":"To England, therefore, I was bound, and it was …","to":"… family from the danger of his machinations.","q":"the fiend would follow me and exempt my family from the danger of his machinations","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Victor assumes he's the only target ✗","b":"Victor assumes the monster will follow him and leave his family alone. He thinks only of himself as the target, a fatal misjudgment.","lt":"Psych","l":"Egocentric bias: overestimating your own centrality to events. Victor's misreading of the Creature's threats will cost Clerval and Elizabeth their lives."},{"id":204,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[490,491],"start":20781.6,"t":20857.0,"from":"It was in the latter end of September that …","to":"… was to occupy me whilst they endured.","q":"my eyes were fixed and unobserving","tag":"Setting","m":"Can't enjoy scenery (vs. Clerval)","b":"Victor travels through beautiful scenery but sees nothing, consumed by his task. Compare Chapter 4, where obsession blinded him to nature too.","lt":"Language","l":"'Bourne' means destination or limit. Hamlet's 'undiscovered country from whose bourn no traveller returns' (death) uses the same word, a subtle death hint."},{"id":205,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[492,493],"start":20857.4,"t":20994.7,"from":"After some days spent in listless indolence, during which …","to":"… river and populous towns occupy the scene.","q":"Alas, how great was the contrast between us!","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval joyful vs. Victor gloomy","b":"Clerval is 'alive to every new scene'; Victor is dead to them. Clerval represents the person Victor could have been.","lt":"Science","l":"The Rhine Gorge (castles, vineyards, cliffs) is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The Romantic 'Rhine journey' became popular partly through writers like Byron and Shelley."},{"id":206,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[494,494],"start":20995.1,"t":21138.0,"from":"We travelled at the time of the vintage and …","to":"… of the mountains of our own country.”","q":"even I was pleased","tag":"Setting","m":"Rhine journey: brief peace","b":"Floating down the Rhine during the harvest, even Victor feels some pleasure. Clerval's speech about the friendly spirit of the place shows his poetic soul.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Repetition of 'even I' emphasizes how unlikely his pleasure is. Writers repeat a phrase to stress contrast or surprise."},{"id":207,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[495,496],"start":21138.4,"t":21208.8,"from":"Clerval! Beloved friend! Even now it delights me to …","to":"… or any interest Unborrow’d from the eye.","q":"He was a being formed in the","tag":"Allusion","m":"Tribute to Clerval + Wordsworth quote","b":"Victor praises Clerval and quotes Wordsworth's 'Tintern Abbey' about loving nature for itself. The past tense hints that Clerval is gone.","lt":"Literature","l":"'Tintern Abbey' (1798) by Wordsworth celebrates nature's power to restore the mind. Victor uses it to describe Clerval, who lives in harmony with nature as Victor cannot."},{"id":208,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[497,500],"start":21209.2,"t":21276.8,"from":"And where does he now exist? Is this gentle …","to":"… river was too gentle to aid us.","q":"And where does he now exist?","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Victor mourns Clerval (death foreshadowed)","b":"Victor breaks off his tale to mourn Henry, revealing in advance that Clerval will die. We now read the rest of the journey with dread.","lt":"Language","l":"Prolepsis (a flash-forward) reveals a future event early. It shifts suspense from 'what happens?' to 'how does it happen?'"},{"id":209,"sec":"Chapter 18","p":[501,502],"start":21277.2,"t":21330.8,"from":"Our journey here lost the interest arising from beautiful …","to":"… and the Tower famed in English history.","q":"I first saw the white cliffs of Britain","tag":"Setting","m":"Arrive in England: history tour","b":"Arriving in England, they see sites of English history (Tilbury, Greenwich, St Paul's, the Tower). Clerval loves history; Victor sees only his task.","lt":"History","l":"Tilbury Fort is where Queen Elizabeth I gave her famous speech to troops facing the Spanish Armada (1588): 'I have the heart and stomach of a king.'"},{"id":210,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[503,504],"start":21331.2,"t":21423.8,"from":"London was our present point of rest; we determined …","to":"… those names filled my soul with anguish.","q":"I saw an insurmountable barrier placed between me and my fellow men","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor isolated by guilt in London","b":"In London Victor avoids people. His guilt creates a 'barrier sealed with blood' between him and humanity, the same isolation the Creature feels.","lt":"Psych","l":"Guilt and shame differ: guilt says 'I did something bad'; shame says 'I am bad' (Brené Brown). Shame isolates. Victor feels both."},{"id":211,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[505,506],"start":21424.2,"t":21558.8,"from":"But in Clerval I saw the image of my …","to":"… with which Nature adorns her chosen dwelling-places.","q":"in Clerval I saw the image of my former self","tag":"Theme","m":"Clerval = Victor's former self (double)","b":"Victor sees his younger, curious self in Clerval. Clerval plans to work in India, using languages to help. His ambition is healthy and outward-looking.","lt":"History","l":"The British East India Company ruled much of India by 1800. Clerval's plan to 'assist the progress of European colonization' reflects attitudes we now critique as imperialist."},{"id":212,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[507,508],"start":21559.2,"t":21616.5,"from":"We had arrived in England at the beginning of …","to":"… stately deer were all novelties to us.","q":"the majestic oaks, the quantity of game, and the herds of stately deer","tag":"Setting","m":"Travel north via Windsor","b":"They plan a scenic route north. Windsor's forest delights them. These travel sections slow the story before the crisis.","lt":"History","l":"Windsor Castle has been a royal residence for over 900 years; Windsor Great Park was a royal hunting forest. Mary Shelley lived near Windsor while writing parts of her work."},{"id":213,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[509,510],"start":21616.9,"t":21751.4,"from":"From thence we proceeded to Oxford. As we entered …","to":"… pitiable to others and intolerable to myself.","q":"But I am a blasted tree; the bolt has entered my soul","tag":"Symbol","m":"'Blasted tree' = callback to lightning oak","b":"At Oxford, Victor calls himself a 'blasted tree', recalling the oak destroyed by lightning in Chapter 2. He has been struck down by the same force he once admired.","lt":"History","l":"Oxford sided with King Charles I during the English Civil War (1642–1651). The king made it his capital; Parliament's victory led to his execution in 1649."},{"id":214,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[511,512],"start":21751.8,"t":21856.2,"from":"We passed a considerable period at Oxford, rambling among …","to":"… which that terrible scene was thus associated.","q":"The latter name made me tremble","tag":"Theme","m":"Trauma triggered by a word (Chamounix)","b":"Even a mention of Chamounix makes Victor flee. Small reminders trigger his terror.","lt":"Psych","l":"Trauma triggers: sensory reminders (words, sounds, places) can bring back fear and distress. This is a recognized feature of post-traumatic stress."},{"id":215,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[513,514],"start":21856.6,"t":21941.4,"from":"From Derby, still journeying northwards, we passed two months …","to":"… which also he forsakes for other novelties.","q":"I could now almost fancy myself among the Swiss mountains","tag":"Setting","m":"Lake District = like Switzerland","b":"The Lake District reminds Victor of home. Clerval enjoys new friends here; Victor notes Clerval's restless love of novelty.","lt":"Literature","l":"Cumberland and Westmorland (the Lake District) were home to the 'Lake Poets': Wordsworth, Coleridge and Southey. The area is now a national park."},{"id":216,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[515,516],"start":21941.8,"t":22067.9,"from":"We had scarcely visited the various lakes of Cumberland …","to":"… arrive at the termination of my journey.","q":"But I was impatient to arrive at the termination of my journey","tag":"Character","m":"Edinburgh: Clerval happy, Victor anxious","b":"In Edinburgh, Clerval enjoys the city while Victor is impatient. He wants to finish the task, but also dreads it.","lt":"History","l":"Edinburgh was called the 'Athens of the North' for its Scottish Enlightenment thinkers: David Hume, Adam Smith and James Hutton (father of geology)."},{"id":217,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[517,518],"start":22068.3,"t":22142.4,"from":"We left Edinburgh in a week, passing through Coupar, …","to":"… which I cannot do in your absence.”","q":"I had rather be with you","tag":"Character","m":"Last time V. sees Clerval alive","b":"Victor leaves Clerval in Perth to work alone. Clerval's sweet farewell, that he feels at home only with Victor, is heartbreaking given what follows.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'I had rather' is an older form of 'I would rather'. It survives in some formal and dialect English and appears often in Shakespeare."},{"id":218,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[519,520],"start":22142.8,"t":22207.2,"from":"Having parted from my friend, I determined to visit …","to":"… mainland, which was about five miles distant.","q":"I determined to visit some remote spot of Scotland and finish my work in solitude","tag":"Setting","m":"Orkney island: remote, barren, isolated","b":"Victor chooses a barren island in the Orkneys to work alone, repeating the isolation of Ingolstadt. The landscape is bleak and the people poor.","lt":"Science","l":"Orkney is an archipelago off northern Scotland with harsh weather and few trees. It is also home to Skara Brae, a 5,000-year-old Neolithic village."},{"id":219,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[521,522],"start":22207.6,"t":22300.0,"from":"On the whole island there were but three miserable …","to":"… to the roarings of the giant ocean.","q":"The thatch had fallen in, the walls were unplastered","tag":"Setting","m":"Squalid hut = Victor's new lab","b":"Victor's new lab is a squalid hut. He compares Switzerland's gentle lakes with the 'roarings of the giant ocean', with nature now hostile and wild.","lt":"Language","l":"Contrast (antithesis) between settings: Switzerland is gentle like 'a lively infant'; the sea is a roaring 'giant'. Shelley uses setting to show Victor's state of mind."},{"id":220,"sec":"Chapter 19","p":[523,525],"start":22300.4,"t":22406.8,"from":"In this manner I distributed my occupations when I …","to":"… made my heart sicken in my bosom.","q":"obscure forebodings of evil that made my heart sicken in my bosom","tag":"Theme","m":"2nd creation = done in horror (not passion)","b":"This time Victor works with full awareness of the horror. Without passion's 'blindness', the work disgusts him. Contrast this with the excitement of Chapter 4.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Informed consent and full awareness of consequences are basic research ethics. Victor now sees clearly what he's doing, which makes his doubt more significant."},{"id":221,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[526,527],"start":22407.2,"t":22570.6,"from":"I sat one evening in my laboratory; the sun …","to":"… the existence of the whole human race.","q":"whose selfishness had not hesitated to buy its own peace at the price, perhaps, of the existence of the whole human race","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor's reasoning: risk to humanity","b":"Victor imagines the female might refuse the bargain, or the pair might breed 'a race of devils'. He decides he can't risk the future of humanity for his own peace.","lt":"Science","l":"Biologists worry about releasing engineered organisms that can reproduce (e.g. gene drives in mosquitoes). Victor's fear of a self-replicating species is a real modern concern."},{"id":222,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[528,529],"start":22571.0,"t":22636.6,"from":"I trembled and my heart failed within me, when, …","to":"… howl of devilish despair and revenge, withdrew.","q":"tore to pieces the thing on which I was engaged","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor DESTROYS the female","b":"Seeing the Creature's grin at the window, Victor tears apart the half-made female. The Creature watches his only hope destroyed and howls in despair.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Is breaking a promise ever right? Kant says lying and promise-breaking are always wrong; utilitarians say do what produces the best outcome. Victor chooses the outcome. Do you agree?"},{"id":223,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[530,531],"start":22637.0,"t":22696.1,"from":"I left the room, and locking the door, made …","to":"… a person landed close to my house.","q":"a person landed close to my house","tag":"Setting","m":"Isolation + silence = suspense","b":"Victor vows never to resume the work. Alone in the silence, he hears oars, and someone lands. Shelley builds horror through sound.","lt":"Language","l":"Sound imagery creates suspense in horror: footsteps, creaking doors, silence. Films use a technique called 'diegetic sound' to make audiences feel what characters hear."},{"id":224,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[532,534],"start":22696.5,"t":22780.3,"from":"In a few minutes after, I heard the creaking …","to":"… hunger; do you dare destroy my hopes?”","q":"do you dare destroy my hopes?","tag":"Character","m":"Creature recounts his suffering to follow V.","b":"The Creature has followed Victor across Europe, enduring cold and hunger, only to watch his hope be destroyed. His fury is understandable.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Anaphora: repeating 'I have…' at the start of clauses ('I have dwelt… I have endured…'). Speakers use it to build emotional intensity, as in Martin Luther King Jr.'s 'I have a dream.'"},{"id":225,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[535,537],"start":22780.7,"t":22838.6,"from":"“Begone! I do break my promise; never will I …","to":"… your words will only exasperate my rage.”","q":"you believe yourself miserable, but I can make you so wretched","tag":"Theme","m":"Power reversal: Creature calls V. 'Slave'","b":"'You are my creator, but I am your master; obey!' Power flips: the creation now controls the creator.","lt":"Literature","l":"Hegel's master–slave dialectic (1807) argues that masters depend on their slaves and roles can reverse. The Creature and Victor dramatize this power struggle."},{"id":226,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[538,539],"start":22839.0,"t":22928.1,"from":"The monster saw my determination in my face and …","to":"… beneath words. Leave me; I am inexorable.”","q":"Shall each man","tag":"Theme","m":"'Each beast has his mate, and I alone?'","b":"The Creature's anguish: every man and animal has a mate, but he is denied one. This is his strongest argument about the cruelty of total isolation.","lt":"Science","l":"Social isolation harms social animals: Harry Harlow's monkey studies (1950s–60s) showed isolated monkeys developed severe behavioral problems. Isolation damages any social creature."},{"id":227,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[540,543],"start":22928.5,"t":23042.8,"from":"“It is well. I go; but remember, I shall …","to":"… before my enemy without a bitter struggle.","q":"I shall be with you on your wedding-night","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"★ THREAT: 'with you on your wedding-night'","b":"The Creature's most famous threat. Victor assumes it means HE will die that night, never imagining Elizabeth is the target. It is another self-centered misreading.","lt":"Language","l":"Ambiguity: the threat can be read more than one way. Victor's misinterpretation is a classic tragic error, and readers often guess the truth before he does."},{"id":228,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[544,545],"start":23043.2,"t":23152.5,"from":"The night passed away, and the sun rose from …","to":"… yet distinct and oppressive as a reality.","q":"the words of the fiend rang in my ears like a death-knell","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor thinks of himself as target","b":"Victor walks the beach thinking of the threat; he even wishes he could leave humanity, like the Creature. The 'death-knell' simile points to his own funeral.","lt":"History","l":"A death knell is a bell rung to announce a death. Church bells marked deaths and funerals in villages, a custom that gave us the phrase 'death knell' for the end of anything."},{"id":229,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[546,547],"start":23152.9,"t":23295.5,"from":"The sun had far descended, and I still sat …","to":"… in cleaning and arranging my chemical apparatus.","q":"determined to throw them into the sea that very night","tag":"Character","m":"Clerval's letter; V. disposes of remains","b":"Clerval writes asking Victor to rejoin him. Victor gathers the female's remains in a basket with stones to sink them at sea.","lt":"Science","l":"Disposing of biological material at sea was common then. Today strict regulations control disposal of biological and hazardous waste to protect ecosystems."},{"id":230,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[548,549],"start":23295.9,"t":23428.7,"from":"Nothing could be more complete than the alteration that …","to":"… in a short time I slept soundly.","q":"I slept soundly","tag":"Theme","m":"Out at sea: Victor sleeps in boat","b":"After sinking the remains, Victor feels relieved and falls asleep in the boat. His carelessness puts him at the mercy of the winds.","lt":"Language","l":"'Keel' is the bottom spine of a boat. Many everyday idioms come from sailing: 'even keel' (balanced), 'learn the ropes', 'by and large', 'taken aback'."},{"id":231,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[550,551],"start":23429.1,"t":23559.7,"from":"I do not know how long I remained in …","to":"… line of high land towards the south.","q":"I found that the wind was northeast","tag":"Setting","m":"Lost at sea, nature controls him","b":"Victor wakes far from land, without a compass, at nature's mercy. The man who wanted to control nature now cannot even steer a boat.","lt":"Science","l":"Before GPS, sailors navigated by compass, stars and 'dead reckoning' (estimating position from speed, time and heading). Without a compass Victor is truly lost."},{"id":232,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[552,553],"start":23560.1,"t":23640.6,"from":"Almost spent, as I was, by fatigue and the …","to":"… bounding with joy at my unexpected escape.","q":"How mutable are our feelings","tag":"Theme","m":"Wants to live after wishing to die","b":"Facing death at sea, Victor discovers he wants to live. Our 'clinging love' of life persists even in misery. He lands on an unknown shore.","lt":"Psych","l":"Self-preservation instinct is deep: even people in despair often fight to survive when in danger. This is part of why crisis support saves lives."},{"id":233,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[554,556],"start":23641.0,"t":23712.5,"from":"As I was occupied in fixing the boat and …","to":"… of Englishmen to receive strangers so inhospitably.”","q":"Surely it is not the custom of Englishmen to receive strangers so inhospitably","tag":"Irony","m":"Victor met with hostility (like Creature!)","b":"The townspeople crowd around Victor with angry faces. For once Victor is the stranger received with hostility, just as the Creature always is.","lt":"History","l":"Hospitality to strangers was a sacred duty in many cultures, as in ancient Greece's 'xenia'. Its breakdown signals danger in literature."},{"id":234,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[557,560],"start":23712.9,"t":23782.0,"from":"“I do not know,” said the man, “what the …","to":"… who was found murdered here last night.”","q":"it is the custom of the Irish to hate villains","tag":"Setting","m":"Ireland! Victor accused of murder","b":"Victor is in Ireland, not England, and he's taken to the magistrate over a murder. The irony is that Victor is suspected just as Justine was.","lt":"History","l":"In 1800 the Act of Union joined Ireland to Great Britain. Irish–English tensions were high, which colors the exchange about 'the custom of Englishmen'."},{"id":235,"sec":"Chapter 20","p":[561,562],"start":23782.4,"t":23842.0,"from":"This answer startled me, but I presently recovered myself. …","to":"… relate, in proper detail, to my recollection.","q":"I was innocent; that could easily be proved","tag":"Irony","m":"V. thinks innocence = easy to prove (cf. Justine)","b":"Victor assumes his innocence can easily be proven, the same naïve faith his father once had for Justine. We know how that went.","lt":"Language","l":"Dramatic irony again: the reader recalls Justine's fate while Victor confidently expects justice. Shelley deliberately rhymes these scenes."},{"id":236,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[563,564],"start":23842.4,"t":23956.4,"from":"I was soon introduced into the presence of the …","to":"… black mark of fingers on his neck.","q":"the black mark of fingers on his neck","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Strangled young man: same as William","b":"Witnesses describe a young man strangled with finger marks on his neck. This is the Creature's signature. Victor (and we) start to fear who it is.","lt":"Science","l":"Forensic pathologists today study bruise patterns, like finger marks, to identify how a person died. The first forensic medicine textbooks appeared in the early 1800s."},{"id":237,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[565,567],"start":23956.8,"t":24028.4,"from":"The first part of this deposition did not in …","to":"… shore where the corpse was afterwards found.","q":"I remembered the murder of my brother","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor's agitation = looks guilty","b":"Victor's agitation makes him look guilty, just as Justine's confusion did. A woman saw a boat with one man near the shore.","lt":"Psych","l":"Observers misread emotional distress as evidence of guilt; studies show juries are influenced by defendants' demeanor. It's the same error that condemned Justine."},{"id":238,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[568,569],"start":24028.8,"t":24078.7,"from":"Another woman confirmed the account of the fishermen having …","to":"… place where I had deposited the corpse.","q":"Another woman confirmed the account","tag":"Theme","m":"Circumstantial evidence vs. Victor","b":"Testimony about the wind and his boat builds a case against Victor. Circumstantial evidence piles up, mirroring Justine's trial.","lt":"Language","l":"'Deposition' (formal witness testimony) and 'apothecary' (pharmacist) are period legal and medical terms. Note the use of '——' to hide a town's name, like dashes hiding dates."},{"id":239,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[570,571],"start":24079.1,"t":24177.5,"from":"Mr. Kirwin, on hearing this evidence, desired that I …","to":"… but you, Clerval, my friend, my benefactor—”","q":"Have my murderous machinations deprived you also, my dearest Henry, of life?","tag":"Theme","m":"★ CLERVAL MURDERED (3rd victim)","b":"The body is Clerval. Victor throws himself on it, calling himself the murderer. This is the Creature's revenge for the destroyed female, matching companion for companion.","lt":"Ethics","l":"'Eye for an eye' (lex talionis) is retributive justice. The Creature takes Victor's companion in exchange for his own. It is revenge in perfect symmetry."},{"id":240,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[572,574],"start":24177.9,"t":24267.3,"from":"The human frame could no longer support the agonies …","to":"… of the wheel, continually renewed the torture?","q":"like the turning of the wheel, continually renewed the torture","tag":"Symbol","m":"Fever: 'wheel' of torture","b":"Victor collapses into a two-month fever, raving that he murdered William, Justine and Clerval. He wonders why he can't die.","lt":"History","l":"The 'breaking wheel' was a medieval torture device. The 'wheel of fortune' (rota fortunae) was a medieval symbol of fate's endless ups and downs."},{"id":241,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[575,576],"start":24267.7,"t":24342.9,"from":"But I was doomed to live and in two …","to":"… that I had heard during my sufferings.","q":"surrounded by gaolers, turnkeys, bolts, and all the miserable apparatus of a dungeon","tag":"Setting","m":"Victor wakes in PRISON (like Justine)","b":"Victor wakes in a prison cell, exactly where Justine spent her last days. His fate keeps mirroring the victims of his silence.","lt":"Language","l":"'Gaol' is the old British spelling of 'jail' (both pronounced 'jail'). 'Turnkey' is a jailer who keeps the keys. Spelling variants often survive in British English."},{"id":242,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[577,580],"start":24343.3,"t":24416.8,"from":"I replied in the same language, with a feeble …","to":"… my mind with the force of reality.","q":"I am sorry that I am still alive to feel this misery and horror","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor wishes he were dead","b":"The nurse is cold and indifferent. Victor wishes he'd died. His life now feels like a dream with no reality.","lt":"Psych","l":"Depersonalization and derealization (feeling that life is unreal or dreamlike) are common responses to extreme trauma and stress."},{"id":243,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[581,582],"start":24417.2,"t":24492.5,"from":"As the images that floated before me became more …","to":"… visits were short and with long intervals.","q":"no one was near me who soothed me with the gentle voice of love","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor alone, unloved (now like Creature)","b":"Victor has no one to comfort him. For the first time he experiences what the Creature always endured: illness and suffering without love.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Empathy through experience: we often understand others' pain only once we feel it. Victor now lives the Creature's condition, but does he learn from it?"},{"id":244,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[583,585],"start":24492.9,"t":24558.4,"from":"One day, while I was gradually recovering, I was …","to":"… comfort which I am capable of receiving.”","q":"on the whole earth there is no comfort which I am capable of receiving","tag":"Character","m":"Mr. Kirwin: kind magistrate","b":"Mr. Kirwin, the magistrate, is kind and visits Victor. Unlike Justine's judges, he treats Victor humanely, though Victor rejects all comfort.","lt":"History","l":"A magistrate was a local judge handling minor cases and preliminary hearings. Kirwin's fairness contrasts with the Geneva court that condemned Justine."},{"id":245,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[586,588],"start":24558.8,"t":24620.4,"from":"“I know that the sympathy of a stranger can …","to":"… were, by some fiend across your path.”","q":"placed, as it were, by some fiend across your path","tag":"Irony","m":"Kirwin says 'by some fiend' (literal!)","b":"Kirwin says it was as if 'some fiend' placed the body in Victor's path. He means it figuratively, but it's literally true.","lt":"Language","l":"Literal vs. figurative meaning: Kirwin uses an idiom without realizing it's exactly what happened. Writers create irony by making figures of speech literally true."},{"id":246,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[589,591],"start":24620.8,"t":24687.6,"from":"As Mr. Kirwin said this, notwithstanding the agitation I …","to":"… whose murder I am now to lament?”","q":"whose murder I am now to lament?","tag":"Theme","m":"V. expects more deaths","b":"Kirwin found letters showing who Victor is and wrote to Geneva. Victor immediately assumes another family member has died.","lt":"Psych","l":"Hypervigilance, constantly expecting danger, develops after repeated trauma. Victor's mind now jumps to catastrophe."},{"id":247,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[592,596],"start":24688.0,"t":24743.9,"from":"“Your family is perfectly well,” said Mr. Kirwin with …","to":"… welcome instead of inspiring such violent repugnance.”","q":"someone, a friend, is come to visit you","tag":"Character","m":"V. thinks the visitor is the monster","b":"Told that a visitor has come, Victor thinks it's the monster and panics. Kirwin reads the panic as guilt.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'Is come' uses 'be' as the helping verb for 'come', an older perfect tense ('he is come' = 'he has come'). It survives in the Christmas carol 'Joy to the World, the Lord is come.'"},{"id":248,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[597,601],"start":24744.3,"t":24828.1,"from":"“My father!” cried I, while every feature and every …","to":"… seems to pursue you. And poor Clerval—”","q":"You travelled to seek happiness, but a fatality seems to pursue you","tag":"Character","m":"Father arrives: comfort & 'fatality'","b":"Alphonse arrives and Victor's anguish turns to joy. Even his father now speaks of a 'fatality' pursuing him, reinforcing Victor's belief in doom.","lt":"Language","l":"'Fatality' here means fate or doom, not a death. The word's main meaning has narrowed over time to 'a death in an accident'."},{"id":249,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[602,605],"start":24828.5,"t":24942.1,"from":"The name of my unfortunate and murdered friend was …","to":"… me and my destroyer in its ruins.","q":"wishing for some mighty revolution that might bury me and my destroyer in its ruins","tag":"Theme","m":"Wants to die w/ the Creature","b":"Victor wishes for a catastrophe that would destroy him and the Creature together. Their fates are now tied: creator and creation, joined in destruction.","lt":"Literature","l":"Samson brings down the temple on himself and his enemies (Judges 16). Victor's wish for mutual destruction echoes this biblical image, and foreshadows the ending."},{"id":250,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[606,607],"start":24942.5,"t":25050.4,"from":"The season of the assizes approached. I had already …","to":"… saw them in my chamber at Ingolstadt.","q":"The season of the assizes approached","tag":"Theme","m":"Trial: acquitted (alibi in Orkneys)","b":"Victor is acquitted because witnesses prove he was in the Orkneys at the time. Unlike Justine, he's freed, perhaps thanks to his class and connections.","lt":"History","l":"The assizes were periodic courts held in county towns by travelling judges. They lasted in England and Wales until 1972."},{"id":251,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[608,609],"start":25050.8,"t":25172.7,"from":"My father tried to awaken in me the feelings …","to":"… and day preyed upon my wasted frame.","q":"I was a shattered wreck—the shadow of a human being","tag":"Character","m":"Victor = 'skeleton', a shadow","b":"Victor is physically wasted, 'a mere skeleton'. Ironically he now resembles a corpse, like the materials he once used.","lt":"Language","l":"Metaphor ('a shattered wreck') and hyperbole ('a mere skeleton') show his decline. Notice how often Victor describes himself in ship or wreck imagery, echoing Walton's frame."},{"id":252,"sec":"Chapter 21","p":[610,611],"start":25173.1,"t":25344.1,"from":"Still, as I urged our leaving Ireland with such …","to":"… mind is by its structure peculiarly susceptible.","q":"a sense of security, a feeling that a truce was established","tag":"Theme","m":"Leaves Ireland; brief calm at sea","b":"Sailing from Ireland at night, Victor feels a brief 'truce' with fate. Calm moments in this novel always come before disaster.","lt":"History","l":"Havre-de-Grâce (now Le Havre) is a major French port founded by King Francis I in 1517. It was the main gateway between France and England and America."},{"id":253,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[612,613],"start":25344.5,"t":25435.0,"from":"The voyage came to an end. We landed, and …","to":"… prove to me the futility of pride.","q":"he endeavoured to prove to me the futility of pride","tag":"Character","m":"Father misreads V.'s despair as pride","b":"In Paris, Alphonse thinks Victor is ashamed of being accused. He keeps misunderstanding because Victor keeps hiding the truth.","lt":"Language","l":"'Futility' means uselessness (from Latin futilis, leaky, as in a vessel that can't hold water). Words often carry hidden metaphors in their roots."},{"id":254,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[614,615],"start":25435.4,"t":25545.4,"from":"“Alas! My father,” said I, “how little do you …","to":"… relieved the burden of my mysterious woe.","q":"Justine, poor unhappy Justine, was as innocent as I","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor: 'I murdered them' (half-confession)","b":"Victor blurts out that he is the murderer of William, Justine and Henry, but never explains. His half-confessions relieve him without helping anyone.","lt":"Psych","l":"Confession can relieve the psychological burden of secrets (James Pennebaker's research on expressive writing). Victor confesses just enough to feel better, not enough to act."},{"id":255,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[616,618],"start":25545.8,"t":25611.4,"from":"Upon this occasion my father said, with an expression …","to":"… suffered me to speak of my misfortunes.","q":"I am not mad","tag":"Narration","m":"'I am not mad': V.'s sanity doubted","b":"His father thinks he's raving; Victor insists he's sane, as he did to Walton. Others' disbelief isolates him further.","lt":"History","l":"In the 1800s 'madness' could get people locked in asylums like Bedlam (London). Fear of being called mad was real, and it's why Victor stays silent."},{"id":256,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[619,622],"start":25611.8,"t":25688.2,"from":"As time passed away I became more calm; misery …","to":"… not totally void of comfort and tranquillity.","q":"sufficient for me was the consciousness of them","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth's letter: 'do you love another?'","b":"Victor learns to hide his feelings. Elizabeth writes, worried that he loves someone else and is marrying her only out of duty.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Inversion: 'sufficient for me was the consciousness' puts the complement first for emphasis. Normal order would be 'the consciousness was sufficient for me'."},{"id":257,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[623,625],"start":25688.6,"t":25790.0,"from":"“Yet I fear that the same feelings now exist …","to":"… with simple truth—Do you not love another?","q":"Do you not love another?","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth: honest & selfless","b":"Elizabeth offers Victor his freedom if he loves someone else. She puts his happiness before her own, and she is braver about honesty than Victor ever is.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Elizabeth asks for 'simple truth', the honesty Victor never gives. Transparency in relationships is linked to trust and satisfaction."},{"id":258,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[626,627],"start":25790.4,"t":25901.6,"from":"“You have travelled; you have spent several years of …","to":"… mine, I shall need no other happiness.","q":"if I see but one smile on your lips","tag":"Character","m":"Elizabeth asks only for his happiness","b":"Elizabeth tells Victor not to come if it would cause him pain; one smile would be enough for her. Her selflessness contrasts with Victor's self-absorption.","lt":"Language","l":"'But' here means 'only' ('if I see but one smile'). This older meaning survives in phrases like 'all but' and 'but a moment'."},{"id":259,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[628,630],"start":25902.0,"t":25991.9,"from":"This letter revived in my memory what I had …","to":"… guilt which would pursue me until death.","q":"I will be with you on your wedding-night!","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Victor remembers threat: thinks of HIMSELF","b":"Victor recalls the threat and calls it 'my sentence', still assuming he'll be the victim. He prepares to fight to the death.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Italics in a quotation mark repeated words remembered, or emphasis. In handwriting, show emphasis by underlining, the traditional proofreader's mark for italics."},{"id":260,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[631,632],"start":25992.3,"t":26131.4,"from":"Sweet and beloved Elizabeth! I read and reread her …","to":"… entreat, and I know you will comply.”","q":"but the apple was already eaten","tag":"Allusion","m":"'Apple already eaten' = Fall from Eden","b":"Victor's dreams of happiness are ruined by the 'apple': his forbidden knowledge. He promises to tell Elizabeth a 'dreadful secret' the day after the wedding.","lt":"Literature","l":"The apple and the angel with a flaming sword come from Genesis 3 and Paradise Lost: Adam and Eve are driven from Eden after eating forbidden fruit. Victor sees himself as fallen."},{"id":261,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[633,634],"start":26131.8,"t":26190.2,"from":"In about a week after the arrival of Elizabeth’s …","to":"… the multitude of miseries that overcame me.","q":"a real insanity possessed me","tag":"Character","m":"Victor's mood swings (rage ↔ despair)","b":"Back in Geneva, Victor alternates between fury and numbness. Elizabeth is also changed: thinner, sadder, but gentler.","lt":"Psych","l":"Emotional dysregulation, intense swings between anger and numbness, is common after severe trauma and grief."},{"id":262,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[635,638],"start":26190.6,"t":26251.3,"from":"Elizabeth alone had the power to draw me from …","to":"… death, to the happiness of my cousin.”","q":"I will consecrate myself, in life or death, to the happiness of my cousin","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"'in life or death': wedding set","b":"Victor says he will dedicate himself to Elizabeth 'in life or death'. The phrasing is ominous.","lt":"Language","l":"Double meanings: 'consecrate' (make sacred) is used for weddings and for burials. Shelley chooses words that work for both marriage and death."},{"id":263,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[639,640],"start":26251.7,"t":26328.4,"from":"“My dear Victor, do not speak thus. Heavy misfortunes …","to":"… I imagined, the seal to my fate.","q":"thus put, as I imagined, the seal to my fate","tag":"Theme","m":"Wedding in 10 days = 'seal to my fate'","b":"Elizabeth urges them to cling to what remains. Victor agrees to marry in ten days, believing he's sealing his own death. He is wrong about whose.","lt":"Grammar","l":"'As I imagined' is a parenthetical aside admitting his own error in hindsight. Retrospective narrators often signal mistakes this way."},{"id":264,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[641,642],"start":26328.8,"t":26405.2,"from":"Great God! If for one instant I had thought …","to":"… no trace but deep and everlasting regret.","q":"If for one instant I had thought what might be the hellish intention of my fiendish adversary","tag":"Narration","m":"Victor's hindsight: 'If I had known…'","b":"Victor, telling the story later, says he would have exiled himself if he'd understood the threat. Retrospective narration lets him (and us) see what he couldn't.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Third conditional: 'If I had thought…, I would have banished…' describes an unreal past and regret. Structure: if + past perfect, would have + past participle."},{"id":265,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[643,644],"start":26405.6,"t":26496.2,"from":"Preparations were made for the event, congratulatory visits were …","to":"… occurrence which no accident could possibly prevent.","q":"an occurrence which no accident could possibly prevent","tag":"Irony","m":"'No accident could prevent': irony","b":"Everyone treats the wedding as certain. Victor hides his anxiety, carries pistols and a dagger, and prepares to fight. The reader dreads what's coming.","lt":"Language","l":"Tempting fate is a common literary setup: when characters say 'nothing can go wrong', readers expect disaster. Horror films use this trope constantly."},{"id":266,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[645,647],"start":26496.6,"t":26598.7,"from":"Elizabeth seemed happy; my tranquil demeanour contributed greatly to …","to":"… invader who should wish to enslave it.","q":"a presentiment of evil pervaded her","tag":"Setting","m":"Wedding day: boat trip, Elizabeth uneasy","b":"After the wedding they sail across Lake Geneva to Evian. Elizabeth senses danger. The scenery is lovely, but the mood is anxious.","lt":"History","l":"Evian is a French spa town on Lake Geneva, famous today for its bottled mineral water. Shelley sets this final happy journey on the same lake where she wrote the book."},{"id":267,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[648,649],"start":26599.1,"t":26666.2,"from":"I took the hand of Elizabeth. “You are sorrowful, …","to":"… How happy and serene all nature appears!”","q":"What a divine day! How happy and serene all nature appears!","tag":"Irony","m":"Elizabeth's 'divine day' = dramatic irony","b":"Elizabeth tries to cheer them both by pointing out the beauty around them. Her joy hurts to read because we suspect what's coming.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Exclamatory sentences ('What a divine day!') express strong emotion. In formal writing they're rare; in dialogue they reveal a character's feelings."},{"id":268,"sec":"Chapter 22","p":[650,652],"start":26666.6,"t":26748.5,"from":"Thus Elizabeth endeavoured to divert her thoughts and mine …","to":"… me and cling to me for ever.","q":"those cares and fears revive which soon were to clasp me and cling to me for ever","tag":"Foreshadowing","m":"Sunset landing = doom approaching","b":"The sun sets as they land. The day's beauty ends, and Victor's fears return 'for ever'. Darkness falls literally and figuratively.","lt":"Language","l":"Personification: cares and fears 'clasp' and 'cling' like hands. Shelley foreshadows the Creature's grasp, which will soon take Elizabeth."},{"id":269,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[653,655],"start":26748.9,"t":26823.2,"from":"It was eight o’clock when we landed; we walked …","to":"… or that of my adversary was extinguished.","q":"my right hand grasped a pistol which was hidden in my bosom","tag":"Theme","m":"V. armed, guards HIMSELF, not Elizabeth","b":"Victor patrols with a pistol, ready to defend himself. He's focused on his own battle, not on protecting Elizabeth.","lt":"History","l":"Pistols of the era (flintlocks) fired one shot and took time to reload. Victor's single shot will be useless against the Creature's speed."},{"id":270,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[656,659],"start":26823.6,"t":26918.3,"from":"Elizabeth observed my agitation for some time in timid …","to":"… repeated, and I rushed into the room.","q":"What is it that agitates you, my dear Victor? What is it you fear?","tag":"Theme","m":"★ V. sends Elizabeth away ALONE = fatal","b":"Victor sends Elizabeth to bed alone so he can search for the Creature. It's the fatal mistake: he leaves her unprotected. Then he hears a scream.","lt":"Psych","l":"Tunnel vision under threat: stress narrows attention to one danger, and people miss other obvious risks. Victor is so sure he's the target that he leaves her alone."},{"id":271,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[660,661],"start":26918.7,"t":27020.7,"from":"Great God! Why did I not then expire! Why …","to":"… had ceased to issue from her lips.","q":"The murderous mark of the fiend’s grasp was on her neck","tag":"Theme","m":"★ ELIZABETH MURDERED (4th victim)","b":"Elizabeth lies dead across the bed, strangled. The threat was never about Victor's life; it was about making him suffer as the Creature suffers, alone.","lt":"Art","l":"Henry Fuseli's painting 'The Nightmare' (1781) shows a woman sprawled across a bed with a demon. Many critics think Shelley's image of Elizabeth echoes it; Fuseli knew Mary's mother."},{"id":272,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[662,663],"start":27021.1,"t":27092.2,"from":"While I still hung over her in the agony …","to":"… different directions among the woods and vines.","q":"the pale yellow light of the moon illuminate the chamber","tag":"Symbol","m":"Creature grins at window; V. fires & misses","b":"The Creature grins at the window, pointing at the corpse. Victor fires and misses, and the Creature escapes into the lake. Others think Victor imagined him.","lt":"Language","l":"The 'yellow' light echoes the Creature's 'dull yellow eye' on the night of his creation. Shelley links colors and images across the novel."},{"id":273,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[664,665],"start":27092.6,"t":27183.6,"from":"I attempted to accompany them and proceeded a short …","to":"… return to Geneva with all possible speed.","q":"my father even now might be writhing under his grasp","tag":"Character","m":"Fears for father & Ernest; rushes home","b":"Victor collapses, then realizes his father and Ernest may be next. Fear finally drives him to action.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Modal verbs ('might be writhing', 'might be dead') express possibility. Here they build a chain of feared scenarios."},{"id":274,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[666,667],"start":27184.0,"t":27302.7,"from":"There were no horses to be procured, and I …","to":"… words, what remains of my hideous narration.","q":"one by one, my friends were snatched away; I was left desolate","tag":"Narration","m":"Victor speeds up the story (summary)","b":"Victor tells Walton he must summarize: he's exhausted. Narrative pace accelerates toward the end.","lt":"Language","l":"Narrative pacing: summary covers lots of time quickly; scene slows down for detail. Victor shifts to summary as his strength fails."},{"id":275,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[668,669],"start":27303.1,"t":27392.2,"from":"I arrived at Geneva. My father and Ernest yet …","to":"… a solitary cell had been my habitation.","q":"For they had called me mad","tag":"Theme","m":"Father dies of grief; V. in asylum","b":"Alphonse dies of grief days later. Victor is locked in a cell as mad for months. Everything and everyone is gone.","lt":"History","l":"Asylums of the era often used solitary confinement and restraints. Reformers like Philippe Pinel in France (1790s) pushed for more humane 'moral treatment'."},{"id":276,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[670,671],"start":27392.6,"t":27455.6,"from":"Liberty, however, had been a useless gift to me, …","to":"… authority for the apprehension of the murderer.","q":"at the same time awakened to revenge","tag":"Theme","m":"Revenge = only reason to live","b":"Freed, Victor awakens only to revenge. His passion for discovery has transformed into a passion for destruction.","lt":"Psych","l":"Revenge fantasies can give purpose after loss, but research (Carlsmith, 2008) shows taking revenge usually makes people feel worse, not better."},{"id":277,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[672,673],"start":27456.0,"t":27519.2,"from":"The magistrate listened to me with attention and kindness. …","to":"… and never deviating into invective or exclamation.","q":"never deviating into invective or exclamation","tag":"Theme","m":"Finally tells a magistrate (too late)","b":"Victor finally tells the truth to an official, calmly and precisely. If he'd done this after William's murder, Justine might have lived.","lt":"Language","l":"'Invective' means insulting, abusive language. Victor strips his speech of emotion to be believed, the same strategy the Creature used with De Lacey."},{"id":278,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[674,676],"start":27519.6,"t":27614.6,"from":"The magistrate appeared at first perfectly incredulous, but as …","to":"… or what region he may now inhabit.”","q":"no one can conjecture to what place he has wandered","tag":"Character","m":"Magistrate believes, but can't help","b":"The magistrate half-believes but says catching a creature who lives in glaciers and caves is impossible.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Even when people are believed, institutions may lack the means to act. It's an issue in real justice systems with limited resources."},{"id":279,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[677,678],"start":27615.0,"t":27665.6,"from":"“I do not doubt that he hovers near the …","to":"… should make up your mind to disappointment.”","q":"he may be hunted like the chamois and destroyed as a beast of prey","tag":"Theme","m":"V. wants creature hunted like an animal","b":"Victor wants his creation hunted down like an animal. He completely denies the Creature's humanity.","lt":"Science","l":"The chamois is a goat-antelope of the Alps, agile on steep cliffs. It was hunted almost to local extinction in places, and its skin gave us the word 'shammy' cloth."},{"id":280,"sec":"Chapter 23","p":[679,682],"start":27666.0,"t":27753.9,"from":"“That cannot be; but all that I can say …","to":"… meditate on some other mode of action.","q":"how ignorant art thou in thy pride of wisdom!","tag":"Irony","m":"V. calls magistrate proud (irony!)","b":"Victor admits revenge is 'a vice' but 'the devouring and only passion of my soul'. He accuses the magistrate of 'pride of wisdom', which is exactly Victor's own sin.","lt":"Psych","l":"Projection (Freud) is seeing your own flaws in others. Victor accuses others of pride, ignorance and monstrousness, the qualities critics see in him."},{"id":281,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[683,685],"start":27754.3,"t":27827.0,"from":"My present situation was one in which all voluntary …","to":"… die and leave my adversary in being.","q":"But revenge kept me alive; I dared not die and leave my adversary in being","tag":"Theme","m":"Revenge sustains Victor (like Creature)","b":"Victor leaves Geneva forever. Revenge is all that keeps him alive. He and the Creature are now driven by the same single passion.","lt":"Literature","l":"Captain Ahab in 'Moby-Dick' (1851) chases the white whale out of revenge, destroying himself and his crew. Both he and Victor are consumed by obsessive pursuit."},{"id":282,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[686,687],"start":27827.4,"t":27956.3,"from":"When I quitted Geneva my first labour was to …","to":"… feel the despair that now torments me.”","q":"And I call on you, spirits of the dead","tag":"Theme","m":"Vow at family graves","b":"At the graves of William, Elizabeth and his father, Victor swears revenge, calling on spirits. He makes a sacred ritual of vengeance.","lt":"Literature","l":"Hamlet swears revenge after his father's ghost appears. Revenge tragedy is a genre (Shakespeare, Seneca) where vengeance destroys the avenger too."},{"id":283,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[688,689],"start":27956.7,"t":28018.4,"from":"I had begun my adjuration with solemnity and an …","to":"… determined to live, and I am satisfied.”","q":"I am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have determined to live, and I am satisfied","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature WANTS Victor to chase him","b":"The Creature laughs and whispers that he's glad Victor will live. He wants Victor to suffer, and to stay connected to him through hatred.","lt":"Psych","l":"Toxic bonds: hatred can bind people as tightly as love. Psychologists note that obsessive conflict keeps people emotionally attached to each other."},{"id":284,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[690,692],"start":28018.8,"t":28157.3,"from":"I darted towards the spot from which the sound …","to":"… few drops that revived me, and vanish.","q":"he fled with more than mortal speed","tag":"Theme","m":"Chase begins: Rhône → Black Sea → Russia","b":"The chase begins: from the Rhône, across the Mediterranean and the Black Sea, through Tartary and Russia. Victor believes spirits help him with food and rain.","lt":"History","l":"'Tartary' was a vague European name for central and northern Asia (Siberia, Mongolia, Central Asia). Old maps labelled huge regions with it."},{"id":285,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[693,694],"start":28157.7,"t":28290.1,"from":"I followed, when I could, the courses of the …","to":"… as the ardent desire of my soul.","q":"as the mechanical impulse of some power of which I was unconscious","tag":"Theme","m":"Victor becomes a machine of revenge","b":"Victor says he pursues 'more as a task enjoined by heaven' than by desire. He has become an instrument, almost a creature himself, driven by forces outside his will.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Victor again denies free will: he's 'mechanical', driven by 'some power'. Philosophers call this an excuse of determinism. Is he responsible for choosing revenge?"},{"id":286,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[695,696],"start":28290.5,"t":28365.0,"from":"What his feelings were whom I pursued I cannot …","to":"… of my tedious toil and horrible pilgrimage!","q":"My reign is not yet over","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature leaves messages + food (guides V.)","b":"The Creature carves taunting messages and even leaves food so Victor won't die. He leads Victor north deliberately, making him feel the cold and suffering he has felt.","lt":"Psych","l":"The Creature reverses roles: now he is the 'parent' providing food and Victor the dependent. It's a cruel mirror of the care Victor never gave."},{"id":287,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[697,699],"start":28365.4,"t":28473.6,"from":"As I still pursued my journey to the northward, …","to":"… gibe, to meet and grapple with him.","q":"the snows thickened and the cold increased","tag":"Setting","m":"Victor reaches the frozen sea","b":"Victor follows the Creature north to the frozen ocean. The story has come full circle to the icy setting of the opening letters.","lt":"Literature","l":"Circular or frame structure: the novel ends where it began. Dante's Hell is also icy at its deepest point (Cocytus); ice there means total isolation from love."},{"id":288,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[700,701],"start":28474.0,"t":28605.1,"from":"Some weeks before this period I had procured a …","to":"… and revenge, I prepared for my journey.","q":"Some weeks before this period I had procured a sledge and dogs","tag":"Setting","m":"Dogsled chase (callback to Letter 4)","b":"Victor trades for a sledge and dogs and gains ground. Now we understand the sledges Walton's crew saw: these are the two travelers from Letter 4.","lt":"History","l":"Dog sledding was developed by Arctic peoples like the Inuit and Chukchi thousands of years ago. European explorers learned it from them, often too late."},{"id":289,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[702,704],"start":28605.5,"t":28722.8,"from":"I exchanged my land-sledge for one fashioned for the …","to":"… emotions that oppressed me, I wept aloud.","q":"I wept aloud","tag":"Theme","m":"Sees Creature on ice → weeps","b":"When Victor finally sees the Creature nearby, he weeps with emotion. Is it rage, hope, or the closeness of his own 'child'? The feeling is strangely intimate.","lt":"Psych","l":"Intense emotions can be mixed: tears can come from joy, rage or relief. The Victor–Creature bond is so intense that even hatred feels intimate."},{"id":290,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[705,706],"start":28723.2,"t":28812.8,"from":"But this was not the time for delay; I …","to":"… thus preparing for me a hideous death.","q":"I was left drifting on a scattered piece of ice","tag":"Setting","m":"Ice breaks: Victor stranded","b":"Just as Victor nears his enemy, the ice cracks and a sea opens between them. Nature again defeats human will.","lt":"Science","l":"Sea ice breaks up through ocean swells and temperature changes. Floating ice floes can drift for months; Arctic explorers sometimes survived for months on them."},{"id":291,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[707,708],"start":28813.2,"t":28956.9,"from":"In this manner many appalling hours passed; several of …","to":"… hover near and direct the steel aright.","q":"thrust your sword into his heart","tag":"Narration","m":"V. asks Walton to KILL the Creature","b":"Victor's story ends where Walton found him. He asks Walton to swear to kill the Creature if Victor dies, passing his revenge on like an inheritance.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Victor tries to recruit Walton into his vendetta. Cycles of revenge passed down across generations appear in feuds worldwide (e.g. the Hatfields and McCoys)."},{"id":292,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[709,712],"start":28957.3,"t":29054.4,"from":"You have read this strange and terrific story, Margaret; …","to":"… but on this point he was impenetrable.","q":"Such a monster has, then, really existence!","tag":"Narration","m":"Back to Walton: letters as PROOF","b":"Walton returns as narrator. He says Felix and Safie's letters and the monster's sighting prove Victor's story true. Victor refuses to share how he made the Creature.","lt":"Language","l":"Authentication devices are 'evidence' inside fiction (letters, documents) that make a story feel real. Shelley stacks proofs to make the impossible believable."},{"id":293,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[713,715],"start":29054.8,"t":29163.8,"from":"“Are you mad, my friend?” said he. “Or whither …","to":"… almost as imposing and interesting as truth.","q":"Would you also create for yourself and the world a demoniacal enemy?","tag":"Theme","m":"V. guards the secret (finally learns?)","b":"Victor refuses to share his method; he has learned that some knowledge is too dangerous to spread. He also edits Walton's notes to 'correct' his story.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Dual-use research: knowledge that can help or harm (e.g. virus engineering). Scientists still debate whether to publish dangerous findings."},{"id":294,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[716,717],"start":29164.2,"t":29314.0,"from":"Our conversations are not always confined to his own …","to":"… I fell, never, never again to rise.”","q":"until I fell, never, never again to rise","tag":"Allusion","m":"V. compares himself to fallen archangel","b":"Victor compares himself to 'the archangel who aspired to omnipotence', Satan. Earlier the Creature compared himself to Satan. Both now see themselves as fallen.","lt":"Literature","l":"In Paradise Lost, Satan's fall comes from pride ('Better to reign in Hell than serve in Heaven'). Victor's ambition to be a creator-god is that same aspiring pride."},{"id":295,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[718,719],"start":29314.4,"t":29449.1,"from":"Must I then lose this admirable being? I have …","to":"… will be fulfilled and I may die.”","q":"I must pursue and destroy the being to whom I gave existence","tag":"Character","m":"Walton longs to keep V. as friend","b":"Walton has finally found his friend and is about to lose him. Victor says he can't start again; his only remaining purpose is destruction.","lt":"Psych","l":"Anticipatory grief means mourning someone before they die. Walton experiences this as Victor fades."},{"id":296,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[720,723],"start":29449.5,"t":29525.0,"from":"I write to you, encompassed by peril and ignorant …","to":"… Heaven bless you and make you so!","q":"I am surrounded by mountains of ice which admit of no escape","tag":"Setting","m":"Ship trapped in ice: crew in danger","b":"Walton's ship is trapped in the ice and his crew fears death. His own ambition has now endangered everyone, like Victor's.","lt":"History","l":"Shackleton's Endurance (1915) was trapped and crushed by Antarctic ice; remarkably, all 28 men survived. The wreck was found in 2022, 3,000 m deep."},{"id":297,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[724,726],"start":29525.4,"t":29590.2,"from":"My unfortunate guest regards me with the tenderest compassion. …","to":"… you, yet I cannot forbear recording it.","q":"I almost dread a mutiny caused by this despair","tag":"Theme","m":"Crew wants to go home; mutiny?","b":"Victor encourages Walton while the crew grows desperate. Walton fears mutiny. Leaders' ambitions versus followers' lives become the question.","lt":"History","l":"Mutinies were real dangers at sea: the famous mutiny on HMS Bounty (1789) saw sailors set their captain adrift. Henry Hudson's crew did the same to him in 1611."},{"id":298,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[727,728],"start":29590.6,"t":29686.4,"from":"We are still surrounded by mountains of ice, still …","to":"… I would instantly direct my course southwards.","q":"I would instantly direct my course southwards","tag":"Theme","m":"Crew demands: turn back if freed","b":"The sailors ask Walton to promise to return south if the ice frees them. This tests whether Walton will repeat Victor's mistake.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Leaders' duty of care: a captain is responsible for the lives of the crew. Modern ethics in exploration, medicine and business stress weighing others' safety above personal goals."},{"id":299,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[729,730],"start":29686.8,"t":29835.9,"from":"This speech troubled me. I had not despaired, nor …","to":"… to turn their backs on the foe.”","q":"Return as heroes who have fought and conquered","tag":"Character","m":"V.'s speech: 'Be heroes! Don't turn back'","b":"Dying Victor gives a rousing speech urging the crew to continue for glory. Even now he values ambition above life; he hasn't fully learned his lesson.","lt":"Language","l":"Persuasive rhetoric uses pathos (emotion), ethos (credibility) and logos (logic). Victor uses pathos: shame, honor and glory. Identify each appeal in his speech."},{"id":300,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[731,733],"start":29836.3,"t":29889.3,"from":"He spoke this with a voice so modulated to …","to":"… willingly continue to endure their present hardships.","q":"I had rather die than return shamefully, my purpose unfulfilled","tag":"Character","m":"Walton torn: glory vs. crew's lives","b":"The crew is moved but unconvinced. Walton says he'd rather die than return defeated. He is at the same crossroads Victor once faced.","lt":"Psych","l":"Sunk cost fallacy: continuing something because you've already invested so much, even when quitting is wiser. Walton and Victor both struggle with it."},{"id":301,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[734,738],"start":29889.7,"t":30001.8,"from":"The die is cast; I have consented to return …","to":"… “because they will soon return to England.”","q":"The die is cast; I have consented to return if we are not destroyed","tag":"Theme","m":"★ WALTON TURNS BACK (learns lesson)","b":"Walton agrees to turn back. He calls it cowardice, but readers see wisdom. He chooses his crew's lives over glory, the choice Victor never made.","lt":"History","l":"'The die is cast' (alea iacta est) is what Julius Caesar said crossing the Rubicon in 49 BCE, a decision with no return. Walton ironically uses it for his retreat."},{"id":302,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[739,742],"start":30002.2,"t":30064.4,"from":"“Alas! Yes; I cannot withstand their demands. I cannot …","to":"… had certainly not many hours to live.","q":"You may give up your purpose, but mine is assigned to me by heaven","tag":"Character","m":"V. refuses to give up; collapses","b":"Victor insists he must continue, but collapses. The surgeon says he has only hours to live. His obsession outlasts his body.","lt":"Language","l":"Victor again says his purpose is 'assigned' by heaven, using the passive voice to avoid responsibility. Notice: 'is assigned to me' (passive) versus 'I chose' (active)."},{"id":303,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[743,744],"start":30064.8,"t":30220.4,"from":"His sentence was pronounced, and I could only grieve …","to":"… I may still be misled by passion.","q":"I dare not ask you to do what I think right, for I may still be misled by passion","tag":"Theme","m":"V. dying: admits he may be wrong","b":"Victor reviews his life. He says he was right to refuse to make a female, since his duty to humanity came first, but admits his judgment may be clouded by passion.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Victor weighs duties: to his creature versus to humanity. Philosophers call this a conflict of obligations. Do you agree he chose correctly?"},{"id":304,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[745,746],"start":30220.8,"t":30284.0,"from":"“That he should live to be an instrument of …","to":"… gentle smile passed away from his lips.","q":"Seek happiness in tranquillity and avoid ambition","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Victor's last words: 'avoid ambition'… or not?","b":"Victor's final advice: avoid ambition, 'even if it be only the apparently innocent one of distinguishing yourself in science'. Then he adds that another may succeed where he failed. He contradicts himself until the end.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Is ambition good or dangerous? Society celebrates innovators like Edison and Musk, yet also warns of 'moving fast and breaking things'. Victor's final words capture this tension."},{"id":305,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[747,749],"start":30284.4,"t":30351.3,"from":"Margaret, what comment can I make on the untimely …","to":"… incomplete without this final and wonderful catastrophe.","q":"what comment can I make on the untimely extinction of this glorious spirit?","tag":"Character","m":"Walton mourns Victor; hears a noise","b":"Walton grieves Victor and calls him 'glorious'. He still admires him. Then a sound from the cabin interrupts: there is one more scene.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Rhetorical questions in grief express what can't be answered. Walton's questions mirror Victor's earlier 'Why did I not then expire!'"},{"id":306,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[750,751],"start":30351.7,"t":30419.4,"from":"I entered the cabin where lay the remains of …","to":"… the wildest rage of some uncontrollable passion.","q":"gigantic in stature, yet uncouth and distorted in its proportions","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Creature mourns over Victor's body","b":"The Creature hangs over Victor's coffin, grieving. Walton sees him with his own eyes, and he can't bear to look at his face.","lt":"Art","l":"The image of a mourner over a body echoes the Pietà (Mary holding Jesus). The Creature mourning his creator is a reversed parent–child image."},{"id":307,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[752,753],"start":30419.8,"t":30491.7,"from":"“That is also my victim!” he exclaimed. “In his …","to":"… pause of the tempest of his passion.","q":"Oh, Frankenstein! Generous and self-devoted being!","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature asks Victor's pardon (too late)","b":"The Creature calls Victor 'my victim' and asks him for pardon. His grief seems real. Even after everything, he wanted his creator's love.","lt":"Psych","l":"Children who are mistreated often still long for their parent's love and approval. The Creature grieving his abuser is psychologically realistic."},{"id":308,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[754,755],"start":30492.1,"t":30556.2,"from":"“Your repentance,” I said, “is now superfluous. If you …","to":"… torture such as you cannot even imagine.","q":"My heart was fashioned to be susceptible of love and sympathy","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature: crimes tortured him too","b":"Walton says repentance is too late. The Creature replies that he suffered more than Victor did; every murder tortured him. His heart was made for love.","lt":"Language","l":"'Fashioned' means made or shaped. The Creature was literally fashioned by Victor's hands. The word links his heart to his physical making."},{"id":309,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[756,757],"start":30556.6,"t":30700.1,"from":"“After the murder of Clerval I returned to Switzerland, …","to":"… your malignity is withdrawn from your power.”","q":"It is not pity that you feel","tag":"Character","m":"Walton accuses Creature of hypocrisy","b":"The Creature explains that Victor's hope for happiness drove him to kill Elizabeth. Walton, influenced by Victor, angrily accuses him of false grief.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Can a murderer truly feel remorse? Restorative justice programs bring offenders and victims together, and many offenders do express genuine remorse."},{"id":310,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[758,759],"start":30700.5,"t":30876.4,"from":"“Oh, it is not thus—not thus,” interrupted the being. …","to":"… boils at the recollection of this injustice.","q":"I, the miserable and the abandoned, am an abortion, to be spurned at, and kicked, and trampled on","tag":"Theme","m":"★ Creature: Victor's sins also deserve blame","b":"The Creature asks why only he is called a criminal when Felix drove him away and a man shot him after he saved a girl. He doesn't excuse himself, but demands that others share the blame.","lt":"Ethics","l":"Shared responsibility: when harm emerges from many people's actions, who is to blame? Sociologists call this a 'diffusion of responsibility'."},{"id":311,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[760,761],"start":30876.8,"t":31027.6,"from":"“But it is true that I am a wretch. …","to":"… can I find rest but in death?","q":"where can I find rest but in death?","tag":"Theme","m":"Creature admits guilt; plans own death","b":"The Creature fully admits his crimes: 'I am a wretch.' He has no hope left; death is his only consolation.","lt":"Grammar","l":"Anaphora again: 'I have murdered… I have strangled…' The repeated 'I have' is a full confession, the confession Victor never gave."},{"id":312,"sec":"Chapter 24","p":[762,764],"start":31028.0,"t":31128.0,"from":"“Farewell! I leave you, and in you the last …","to":"… waves and lost in darkness and distance.","q":"He was soon borne away by the waves and lost in darkness and distance","tag":"Theme","m":"END: Creature leaves to burn himself","b":"The Creature vows to build a funeral pyre at the North Pole and leaves on an ice raft. We never see him die. The ending is open: darkness, distance, unfinished.","lt":"Literature","l":"An open (ambiguous) ending lets readers decide. Modern adaptations, from the 1931 film to Guillermo del Toro's 2025 'Frankenstein', keep reimagining the Creature's fate."}],"quiz":[{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"Who is Robert Walton writing to?","o":["His wife, Elizabeth","His sister, Margaret Saville","His friend, Henry Clerval","His father, Alphonse"],"a":1,"e":"All of Walton's letters are addressed to his sister, Mrs. Margaret Saville, in England."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"What is Walton's goal?","o":["To find gold in Russia","To rescue a lost ship","To reach the North Pole and discover a passage / the secret of the magnet","To map Africa"],"a":2,"e":"He hopes to reach the pole, find a northern passage, and learn 'the secret of the magnet'."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Character","q":"What does Walton say he lacks most on his voyage?","o":["Money","A friend","A map","A doctor"],"a":1,"e":"'I have no friend, Margaret' — his loneliness parallels Victor's and the Creature's."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Structure","q":"The novel's opening in letters is called…","o":["Stream of consciousness","An epistolary frame narrative","A soliloquy","Free indirect discourse"],"a":1,"e":"Letters (epistolary form) frame Victor's story, which in turn frames the Creature's — a nested structure."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Plot","q":"What do the sailors see before they find Victor?","o":["A whale","A burning ship","A giant figure on a dog sledge","A polar bear"],"a":2,"e":"They see a gigantic being on a sledge — the Creature — the day before they rescue Victor."},{"s":"Letters","t":"Theme","q":"Why does Victor decide to tell Walton his story?","o":["To ask for money","To warn him against dangerous ambition","To get passage home","To confess to a crime he will be tried for"],"a":1,"e":"He sees Walton seeking knowledge as he did and hopes his tale will 'dash the cup from your lips'."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Character","q":"How does Elizabeth Lavenza join the Frankenstein family (1831 edition)?","o":["She is Victor's biological sister","Caroline adopts her from a poor Italian family","She is Clerval's sister","She arrives as a servant"],"a":1,"e":"Caroline finds her living with a poor peasant family near Lake Como and adopts her."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Theme","q":"How does young Victor view Elizabeth after his mother calls her 'a pretty present'?","o":["As a rival","As a possession — 'mine to protect'","As a servant","As a stranger"],"a":1,"e":"He 'looked upon Elizabeth as mine' — an early sign of his possessive, self-centered thinking."},{"s":"Chapter 1","t":"Theme","q":"What duty does Victor say his parents felt toward him?","o":["To make him rich","A deep sense of duty toward 'the being to which they had given life'","To send him to war","None — they ignored him"],"a":1,"e":"This sets up the contrast with Victor's total failure of duty toward his own creation."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Character","q":"Which pair best contrasts Victor's and Clerval's interests?","o":["Music vs. painting","Secrets of nature/science vs. moral relations of people","Money vs. fame","War vs. peace"],"a":1,"e":"Victor seeks 'the secrets of heaven and earth'; Clerval cares about 'the moral relations of things'."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Plot","q":"Which author first sparks Victor's interest in alchemy?","o":["Isaac Newton","Cornelius Agrippa","John Milton","Plutarch"],"a":1,"e":"He finds a volume of Cornelius Agrippa at an inn near Thonon at age 13."},{"s":"Chapter 2","t":"Symbol","q":"What does Victor see at age 15 that changes his interests?","o":["A shipwreck","Lightning destroy an oak tree","A dead body","A comet"],"a":1,"e":"The lightning-blasted oak introduces him to electricity — and later he calls himself 'a blasted tree'."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Plot","q":"How does Caroline Frankenstein die?","o":["In childbirth","Of scarlet fever caught nursing Elizabeth","Murdered","In a boating accident"],"a":1,"e":"She catches scarlet fever while nursing Elizabeth; her dying wish is for Victor and Elizabeth to marry."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"Why does Victor dislike Professor Krempe at first?","o":["Krempe fails him","Krempe mocks his alchemists and has a 'repulsive countenance'","Krempe steals his work","Krempe is French"],"a":1,"e":"Victor judges Krempe partly by appearance — ironic, given what happens to the Creature."},{"s":"Chapter 3","t":"Character","q":"Which professor inspires Victor to pursue modern chemistry?","o":["M. Krempe","M. Waldman","Mr. Kirwin","M. De Lacey"],"a":1,"e":"M. Waldman's kind manner and lecture about scientists who 'command the thunders of heaven' win Victor over."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Theme","q":"'Learn from me… how dangerous is the acquirement of knowledge.' To whom is Victor speaking here?","o":["Elizabeth","Clerval","Walton","The Creature"],"a":2,"e":"Victor interrupts his story to warn Walton directly."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Plot","q":"Why does Victor make the Creature about eight feet tall?","o":["To make it strong for war","Because small parts were hard to work with — speed was his goal","Because the Creature asked","By accident"],"a":1,"e":"The 'minuteness of the parts' slowed him down — a shortcut with terrible consequences."},{"s":"Chapter 4","t":"Theme","q":"What does Victor neglect while building the Creature?","o":["Only his health","His health, nature, and his family","Only his studies","Nothing — he stays balanced"],"a":1,"e":"He ignores the seasons, stops writing home, and becomes ill and nervous."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Plot","q":"When does the Creature come to life?","o":["A summer morning","A dreary night of November","Christmas Eve","During a thunderstorm in July"],"a":1,"e":"'It was on a dreary night of November that I beheld the accomplishment of my toils.'"},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Theme","q":"What is Victor's immediate reaction to his living creation?","o":["Joy and pride","He flees in horror and disgust","He teaches it to speak","He shows it to Clerval"],"a":1,"e":"'Breathless horror and disgust filled my heart' — he abandons it at once."},{"s":"Chapter 5","t":"Character","q":"Who nurses Victor through his long fever?","o":["Elizabeth","His father","Henry Clerval","Justine"],"a":2,"e":"Clerval nurses him all winter and hides the seriousness from Victor's family."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"Who is Justine Moritz?","o":["Victor's cousin","A servant girl taken into the Frankenstein household","Clerval's fiancée","A professor's daughter"],"a":1,"e":"Elizabeth's letter tells Justine's history: neglected by her mother, welcomed by Caroline."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Character","q":"What does Clerval study at Ingolstadt?","o":["Chemistry","Oriental languages (Persian, Arabic, Sanskrit)","Law","Medicine"],"a":1,"e":"Victor joins him — languages are an escape from the science that now sickens him."},{"s":"Chapter 6","t":"Structure","q":"How does Chapter 6 end?","o":["With a murder","With Victor at a peak of happiness after a walking tour","With Victor's arrest","With the Creature speaking"],"a":1,"e":"Victor is joyful — setting up the reversal of the next chapter's news."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Plot","q":"What news does Victor's father send?","o":["Elizabeth is ill","William has been murdered","The family is bankrupt","Ernest has joined the army"],"a":1,"e":"'William is dead!… Victor, he is murdered!'"},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Symbol","q":"What object is missing from William's body?","o":["A gold watch","A miniature portrait of Caroline","A letter","A ring"],"a":1,"e":"The miniature becomes the evidence that frames Justine."},{"s":"Chapter 7","t":"Theme","q":"Why does Victor stay silent about the Creature after seeing it near Geneva?","o":["He's sworn an oath","He thinks no one would believe him and he'd be called mad","He wants the Creature to escape","He's not sure it was the Creature"],"a":1,"e":"He chooses his reputation over speaking out — a choice that costs Justine her life."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Plot","q":"Why does Justine confess to a crime she didn't commit?","o":["She wants to protect Victor","Her confessor threatened her with damnation","She was drunk","She wanted fame"],"a":1,"e":"'I confessed a lie… that I might obtain absolution.'"},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Character","q":"Who speaks up for Justine at the trial?","o":["Victor","Elizabeth","Alphonse","Clerval"],"a":1,"e":"Elizabeth testifies bravely; Victor, who knows the truth, says nothing."},{"s":"Chapter 8","t":"Theme","q":"Victor claims during Justine's trial that…","o":["Justine suffers more than him","He suffers more than Justine, who faces execution","He will confess tomorrow","The Creature is innocent"],"a":1,"e":"A striking example of his self-centeredness."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Theme","q":"Victor says, 'I, not in deed, but in effect, was the true murderer.' What does this mean?","o":["He killed them with his hands","He didn't commit the act but caused it","He was framed","He is joking"],"a":1,"e":"He distinguishes direct action from responsibility for consequences."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Setting","q":"Where does Victor go to seek comfort?","o":["Paris","The valley of Chamounix and Mont Blanc","London","Ingolstadt"],"a":1,"e":"The sublime Alps briefly soothe him — 'maternal Nature bade me weep no more'."},{"s":"Chapter 9","t":"Theme","q":"Elizabeth says after Justine's death that men appear to her as…","o":["angels","monsters thirsting for each other's blood","children","strangers"],"a":1,"e":"She loses faith in humanity, saying the murderer walks free 'and perhaps respected' — unknowingly describing Victor."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Setting","q":"Where does Victor meet the Creature face to face?","o":["In his lab","On the glacier (Mer de Glace) near Montanvert","In Geneva","On a ship"],"a":1,"e":"The Creature bounds across the sea of ice toward him."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Theme","q":"Complete the Creature's line: 'I was benevolent and good; misery made me a…'","o":["man","fiend","god","slave"],"a":1,"e":"The core of his nurture argument: suffering made him evil."},{"s":"Chapter 10","t":"Allusion","q":"'I ought to be thy Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel.' This alludes to…","o":["The Odyssey","Paradise Lost / Genesis","Hamlet","Aesop's Fables"],"a":1,"e":"The Creature compares himself to Adam and Satan from Milton's Paradise Lost."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Theme","q":"How does the Creature first learn about the world?","o":["From books","Through his senses and trial-and-error","From Victor","From the De Laceys directly"],"a":1,"e":"He discovers light, cold, fire and food through experience — like Locke's 'blank slate'."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Plot","q":"What happens when the Creature enters a village?","o":["He is welcomed","Villagers attack him with stones and weapons","He is ignored","He is arrested"],"a":1,"e":"His first lessons about humans are violence and rejection."},{"s":"Chapter 11","t":"Symbol","q":"Fire, discovered by the Creature, connects to which subtitle figure?","o":["Narcissus","Prometheus","Hercules","Orpheus"],"a":1,"e":"Prometheus stole fire for humanity — the novel is subtitled 'The Modern Prometheus'."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Character","q":"Why does the Creature stop stealing the cottagers' food?","o":["He's caught","He realizes it causes them pain","He finds better food","Victor tells him to"],"a":1,"e":"Empathy leads him to stop, and he secretly gathers firewood for them."},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Theme","q":"What does the Creature call language?","o":["A curse","A godlike science","A trick","Useless"],"a":1,"e":"He sees words produce pleasure or pain — 'This was indeed a godlike science.'"},{"s":"Chapter 12","t":"Symbol","q":"What horrifies the Creature at the pool?","o":["A snake","His own reflection","A drowned body","Felix watching him"],"a":1,"e":"He realizes he is 'the monster' — seeing himself through human eyes."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Character","q":"Who is Safie?","o":["Agatha's sister","A young Arabian woman, Felix's beloved","De Lacey's wife","A servant"],"a":1,"e":"Her arrival brings joy, and her French lessons teach the Creature too."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Context","q":"What book does Felix use to teach Safie history?","o":["Paradise Lost","Volney's Ruins of Empires","Plutarch's Lives","The Bible"],"a":1,"e":"The Creature learns of empires, injustice, and the fate of Native Americans."},{"s":"Chapter 13","t":"Theme","q":"'Sorrow only increased with knowledge.' What does the Creature learn that hurts him?","o":["That he has no money, family, or name — he's an outcast","That he'll die soon","That Victor is dead","That he can't read"],"a":0,"e":"Society values 'descent united with riches'; he has neither."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Plot","q":"Why were the De Laceys exiled from France?","o":["They were thieves","Felix helped Safie's father (a Turkish merchant) escape an unjust execution","They insulted the king","They were Protestant"],"a":1,"e":"Doing the right thing cost them fortune and country."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Theme","q":"How does Safie's father repay Felix?","o":["With gold","By betraying him and taking Safie away","By marrying Agatha","By clearing his name"],"a":1,"e":"His ingratitude is one of the cruelties the Creature notes."},{"s":"Chapter 14","t":"Character","q":"What makes Safie unusual among the novel's women?","o":["She is a scientist","She acts independently, travelling alone to find Felix","She is evil","She never speaks"],"a":1,"e":"Taught by her mother to value independence, she defies her father."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Allusion","q":"Which three books does the Creature find?","o":["Hamlet, Bible, Iliad","Paradise Lost, Plutarch's Lives, The Sorrows of Werter","Odyssey, Aesop, Dante","Rousseau, Locke, Newton"],"a":1,"e":"They teach him about feeling (Werter), virtue (Plutarch), and creation/the Fall (Milton)."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"Why does the Creature approach old De Lacey alone?","o":["He's the strongest","He's blind and can't judge by appearance","He owns the cottage","He speaks German"],"a":1,"e":"The blind man judges him only by his words — and is kind."},{"s":"Chapter 15","t":"Plot","q":"What does Felix do when he finds the Creature with his father?","o":["Welcomes him","Strikes him with a stick and drives him away","Calls the police","Faints"],"a":1,"e":"The Creature could have torn him apart but refrains — then flees."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"What does the Creature do to the De Lacey cottage?","o":["Moves in","Burns it down","Repairs it","Nothing"],"a":1,"e":"After they flee, he destroys the garden and sets the cottage ablaze."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"What happens after the Creature saves a drowning girl?","o":["He's thanked","Her companion shoots him","He's adopted","He is arrested"],"a":1,"e":"His good deed is rewarded with a bullet — he vows vengeance on mankind."},{"s":"Chapter 16","t":"Plot","q":"Why does the Creature kill William?","o":["By accident","William is a Frankenstein — 'you belong then to my enemy'","William attacked him","For the locket"],"a":1,"e":"He hoped a child would be unprejudiced, but William screamed 'monster' and named his father."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Plot","q":"What does the Creature demand from Victor?","o":["Money","A female companion","A home in Geneva","To be destroyed"],"a":1,"e":"'You must create a female for me with whom I can live.'"},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Theme","q":"'I am malicious because I am…'","o":["evil","miserable","ugly","alone forever"],"a":1,"e":"He links his crimes directly to his suffering."},{"s":"Chapter 17","t":"Plot","q":"Where does the Creature promise to go with his mate?","o":["The Arctic","The wilds of South America","England","Geneva"],"a":1,"e":"He promises to live harmlessly on acorns and berries, far from humans."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Plot","q":"Why does Victor travel to England?","o":["To marry Elizabeth","To learn from English scientists so he can make the female","To escape the police","To visit Walton"],"a":1,"e":"He conceals his real reason from his father."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Character","q":"How is Clerval described on the Rhine journey?","o":["Bored","Alive to every new scene — 'the very poetry of nature'","Angry","Sick"],"a":1,"e":"Victor quotes Wordsworth's 'Tintern Abbey' to describe him — and mourns him in advance."},{"s":"Chapter 18","t":"Theme","q":"What mistake does Victor make about the Creature's threat to his family?","o":["He thinks the Creature will follow only him","He tells the police","He takes Elizabeth with him","He forgets about it"],"a":0,"e":"He assumes he alone is the target — a self-centered misjudgment."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Symbol","q":"'I am a blasted tree; the bolt has entered my soul' recalls…","o":["The oak struck by lightning in Chapter 2","Walton's ship","The Creature's birth","Clerval's garden"],"a":0,"e":"Victor has been destroyed by the force he once admired."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Setting","q":"Where does Victor build the second creature?","o":["Ingolstadt","A remote island in the Orkneys","London","Geneva"],"a":1,"e":"He chooses a barren Scottish island to work alone — repeating his isolation."},{"s":"Chapter 19","t":"Theme","q":"How does Victor feel making the second creature compared to the first?","o":["More excited","Horrified and sickened — he works with full awareness","Bored","Proud"],"a":1,"e":"Without passion's 'blindness', the work disgusts him."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"Why does Victor destroy the female?","o":["She attacks him","He fears she may hate the Creature, refuse the bargain, or breed a 'race of devils'","He runs out of materials","Clerval asks him to"],"a":1,"e":"He decides he can't risk the future of humanity — and tears her apart as the Creature watches."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Foreshadowing","q":"What is the Creature's threat after Victor destroys the female?","o":["'I will kill your father.'","'I shall be with you on your wedding-night.'","'I will burn Geneva.'","'You will never see Clerval again.'"],"a":1,"e":"Victor assumes it means his own death."},{"s":"Chapter 20","t":"Plot","q":"Where does Victor land after drifting at sea?","o":["England","Ireland","France","Norway"],"a":1,"e":"'It is the custom of the Irish to hate villains' — he's taken to the magistrate."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Plot","q":"Whose body is Victor shown in Ireland?","o":["William's","Elizabeth's","Henry Clerval's","Walton's"],"a":2,"e":"Clerval has been strangled — the Creature's revenge for the destroyed female."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Character","q":"Who is Mr. Kirwin?","o":["A kind Irish magistrate","A professor","Walton's lieutenant","A fisherman"],"a":0,"e":"He treats Victor humanely and writes to his father."},{"s":"Chapter 21","t":"Theme","q":"How is Victor's situation in Ireland similar to Justine's?","o":["Both are imprisoned and accused of a murder the Creature committed","Both are acquitted","Both confess","Both escape"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike Justine, Victor is acquitted — he was proven to be in the Orkneys."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"What does Elizabeth ask Victor in her letter?","o":["To bring her a gift","Whether he loves someone else","To move to Paris","To forgive Justine"],"a":1,"e":"She offers to release him if he's marrying only from duty."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Allusion","q":"'The apple was already eaten' alludes to…","o":["Snow White","The Fall in Genesis / Paradise Lost","Newton's apple","William Tell"],"a":1,"e":"Victor's forbidden knowledge has already cost him paradise."},{"s":"Chapter 22","t":"Plot","q":"Victor promises to tell Elizabeth his secret…","o":["Before the wedding","The day after the wedding","Never","On her birthday"],"a":1,"e":"He never gets the chance."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"Why is Elizabeth alone when the Creature attacks?","o":["She ran away","Victor sent her to bed so he could search for the Creature","She was at her father's","She was sleeping in the boat"],"a":1,"e":"Believing he was the target, Victor left her unprotected."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Plot","q":"What happens to Alphonse Frankenstein?","o":["He hunts the Creature","He dies of grief","He remarries","He moves to Paris"],"a":1,"e":"He dies a few days after hearing of Elizabeth's death."},{"s":"Chapter 23","t":"Theme","q":"How does the Geneva magistrate respond to Victor's story?","o":["He laughs","He half-believes but says catching the Creature is impossible","He arrests Victor","He forms a hunting party immediately"],"a":1,"e":"Victor storms out, calling him ignorant 'in thy pride of wisdom' — projecting his own flaw."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"What keeps Victor alive during the chase?","o":["Love","Revenge","Hope of a cure","Walton"],"a":1,"e":"'Revenge kept me alive; I dared not die and leave my adversary in being.'"},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"How does the Creature help Victor during the pursuit?","o":["He hides","He leaves food and messages so Victor keeps following","He sends letters","He gives up"],"a":1,"e":"He wants Victor to suffer the cold and misery — but also to keep living."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What decision does Walton make at the end?","o":["Continue north","Turn back for the sake of his crew","Abandon ship","Chase the Creature"],"a":1,"e":"He chooses his men's lives over glory — the choice Victor never made."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Plot","q":"What does the Creature plan to do after Victor's death?","o":["Return to Geneva","Burn himself on a funeral pyre at the North Pole","Find the De Laceys","Make a mate"],"a":1,"e":"He leaves on an ice raft and is 'lost in darkness and distance'."},{"s":"Chapter 24","t":"Theme","q":"What are Victor's final words of advice to Walton?","o":["Follow your dreams no matter what","Seek happiness in tranquillity and avoid ambition — though another may succeed","Kill the Creature for me and be famous","Return to science"],"a":1,"e":"He contradicts himself: avoid ambition, 'yet another may succeed'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Structure","q":"In what order are the three narrators nested?","o":["Victor → Walton → Creature","Walton → Victor → Creature","Creature → Victor → Walton","Walton → Creature → Victor"],"a":1,"e":"Walton's letters contain Victor's story, which contains the Creature's story."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"The subtitle 'The Modern Prometheus' refers to a Titan who…","o":["Built Troy","Stole fire from the gods for humanity and was punished","Killed Medusa","Flew too near the sun"],"a":1,"e":"Victor, like Prometheus, seizes forbidden power and is punished for it."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Context","q":"Where and when did Mary Shelley conceive the novel?","o":["London, 1830","Near Lake Geneva in 1816, during a ghost-story contest with Byron","Paris, 1789","Edinburgh, 1820"],"a":1,"e":"At the Villa Diodati, during the 'Year Without a Summer'."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which statement best captures the 'nature vs. nurture' theme?","o":["The Creature was evil from birth","The Creature's cruelty grows from rejection and isolation","Victor is purely good","Nature has no role"],"a":1,"e":"'I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend.'"},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Theme","q":"Which pair are presented as doubles of each other?","o":["Elizabeth & Safie only","Victor & the Creature, and Victor & Walton","Krempe & Kirwin","William & Ernest"],"a":1,"e":"Both pairs mirror ambition, isolation, and revenge."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Character","q":"How many people close to Victor does the Creature kill?","o":["Two","Three: William, Clerval, Elizabeth (Justine is executed; Alphonse dies of grief)","Five, all by his hand","One"],"a":1,"e":"He directly kills William, Clerval and Elizabeth; Justine and Alphonse are indirect victims."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Language","q":"Victor often blames 'destiny' or 'fate'. Critics say this shows…","o":["He is humble","He avoids personal responsibility","He is religious","He is a reliable narrator"],"a":1,"e":"He frames choices (silence, abandonment) as fate rather than decisions."},{"s":"Big picture","t":"Setting","q":"Why does the novel begin and end in the Arctic?","o":["Shelley lived there","Ice frames the story — symbolizing isolation and the extremes of ambition","It's a mistake","To make a sequel"],"a":1,"e":"The circular frame returns to Walton's icy voyage — and his different choice."}],"ov":{"context":[["Author","Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley (1797–1851). Daughter of feminist Mary Wollstonecraft and philosopher William Godwin; married to poet Percy Bysshe Shelley."],["Written","Begun June 1816 at Lake Geneva during a ghost-story contest with Lord Byron and John Polidori, in the cold, dark 'Year Without a Summer'. Mary was 18."],["Published","1818 (anonymously). Revised edition 1831 — the version in this audiobook (Elizabeth is adopted, not a cousin)."],["Genre","Gothic novel, Romantic novel, and often called the first science-fiction novel."],["Subtitle","'The Modern Prometheus' — the Titan who stole fire from the gods for humans and was eternally punished. Victor seizes the 'spark' of life."],["Science behind it","Galvanism (Galvani's twitching frog legs; Aldini's electrified corpse, 1803), debates over 'vitalism', and the rise of modern chemistry."],["Big influences","Milton's Paradise Lost, Coleridge's 'Rime of the Ancient Mariner', Rousseau (natural goodness), Locke (the mind as a blank slate), Wollstonecraft (women's education)."]],"structure":"Three nested narrators, like boxes inside boxes: Robert Walton's letters to his sister Margaret (frame) → Victor Frankenstein's story told to Walton (Ch. 1–10, 17–24) → the Creature's story told to Victor (Ch. 11–16, the heart of the book). Each narrator has reasons to shape the truth, so ask who is speaking and why.","summaries":[["Letters 1–4","Walton sails north seeking the pole and glory, lonely for a friend. His crew sees a giant on a sledge, then rescues a dying stranger — Victor — who agrees to tell his story as a warning."],["Chapter 1","Victor's privileged Geneva childhood. His father rescues and marries Caroline Beaufort; they adopt Elizabeth, whom Caroline calls a 'present' for Victor."],["Chapter 2","Harmonious childhood with Elizabeth and Clerval. Victor discovers the alchemists (Agrippa, Paracelsus) and sees lightning destroy an oak."],["Chapter 3","Caroline dies of scarlet fever. Victor goes to Ingolstadt; Krempe mocks his alchemy, but Waldman's lecture fires his ambition to 'unfold the deepest mysteries of creation'."],["Chapter 4","Two years of obsessive study. Victor discovers the secret of life and builds an eight-foot being from charnel-house parts, neglecting health, nature, and family."],["Chapter 5","November night: the Creature wakes. Victor flees in horror, has a nightmare of Elizabeth turning into his mother's corpse, and collapses into a months-long fever; Clerval nurses him."],["Chapter 6","Elizabeth's letter introduces Justine. Victor recovers, avoids science, studies languages with Clerval, and is happy on a spring walking tour."],["Chapter 7","William is murdered. Returning to Geneva in a storm, Victor glimpses the Creature and knows it's the killer — but stays silent. Justine is accused."],["Chapter 8","Justine's trial. Elizabeth defends her; Victor says nothing. Justine falsely confesses under pressure and is executed."],["Chapter 9","Victor sinks into guilt and isolation, considers suicide, and seeks comfort in the Alps at Chamounix."],["Chapter 10","On the Mer de Glace, the Creature confronts Victor: 'I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend.' Victor agrees to hear his story."],["Chapter 11","The Creature's earliest memories: senses, cold, fire, rejection by villagers. He hides in a hovel beside the De Lacey cottage."],["Chapter 12","He watches the poor, loving De Laceys, stops stealing their food, secretly helps them, discovers language, and is horrified by his reflection."],["Chapter 13","Safie arrives. The Creature learns language and history alongside her and realizes he has no family, money, or place in society."],["Chapter 14","The De Laceys' backstory: exiled for rescuing Safie's father, who then betrayed them. Safie defied him to find Felix."],["Chapter 15","He reads Paradise Lost, Plutarch, and Werter; finds Victor's journal. He wins blind De Lacey's kindness — then Felix beats him away."],["Chapter 16","The family flees; he burns the cottage, is shot after saving a girl, kills William, frames Justine, and demands a female companion."],["Chapter 17","Victor refuses, then — moved by the Creature's arguments — agrees, if the pair will leave Europe forever."],["Chapter 18","Victor delays, then travels to England with Clerval (Rhine journey). The wedding to Elizabeth will follow his return."],["Chapter 19","Travels through London, Oxford, the Lakes, and Scotland. Victor leaves Clerval and builds the female on a bleak Orkney island."],["Chapter 20","Fearing a 'race of devils', Victor destroys the female. The Creature vows, 'I shall be with you on your wedding-night.' Victor drifts to Ireland and is arrested."],["Chapter 21","The murder victim is Clerval. Victor falls ill in prison, is visited by his father, and is acquitted."],["Chapter 22","Elizabeth's letter; they return to Geneva and marry. Victor arms himself, believing he is the target."],["Chapter 23","On the wedding night the Creature strangles Elizabeth. Alphonse dies of grief. Victor, called mad, vows revenge."],["Chapter 24","Victor chases the Creature to the Arctic and dies on Walton's ship. Walton turns back. The Creature mourns Victor and leaves to burn himself at the pole."]],"characters":[["Victor Frankenstein","Brilliant, ambitious Genevan scientist who creates life then abandons it. Secretive, self-pitying, blames 'destiny'. Tragic hero (or villain?)."],["The Creature","Never named. Born gentle and curious; learns language and literature; turned violent by rejection. Eloquent, intelligent, and the novel's moral challenge."],["Robert Walton","Arctic explorer and frame narrator; Victor's double. His decision to turn back is the lesson Victor never learned."],["Elizabeth Lavenza","Victor's adopted 'more than sister' and bride. Kind, brave (defends Justine), but largely passive. Killed on her wedding night."],["Henry Clerval","Victor's best friend; loves languages, poetry, and people. Represents a healthy, balanced mind. Murdered in Ireland."],["Alphonse Frankenstein","Victor's loving, respected father. Dies of grief after Elizabeth's death."],["William Frankenstein","Victor's young brother; the Creature's first victim."],["Justine Moritz","Servant raised by the Frankensteins; framed for William's murder and executed after a coerced confession."],["Caroline Beaufort","Victor's mother; dies of scarlet fever nursing Elizabeth."],["M. Krempe / M. Waldman","Ingolstadt professors: Krempe blunt and 'repulsive'; Waldman gentle, inspiring Victor's turn to chemistry."],["The De Laceys","Old blind father, Felix, and Agatha — exiled French family the Creature watches and loves."],["Safie","Arabian woman who travels alone to find Felix; the Creature learns language alongside her."],["Mr. Kirwin","Kind Irish magistrate who treats Victor fairly."]],"themes":[["Dangerous knowledge & ambition","Victor and Walton pursue glory past all limits. 'Learn from me… how dangerous is the acquirement of knowledge.'"],["Creator's responsibility","Victor gives life but no love or care. 'I ought to be thy Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel.'"],["Nature vs. nurture","The Creature is made good and turned evil by rejection. 'I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend.'"],["Isolation & the need for companionship","Walton wants a friend; Victor isolates himself; the Creature begs for a mate. 'I am malicious because I am miserable.'"],["Appearance & prejudice","Everyone (except the blind De Lacey) judges the Creature by his face. 'A fatal prejudice clouds their eyes.'"],["Revenge","Revenge consumes both creator and creature until they are mirror images in the Arctic."],["Nature & the sublime","Mountains, glaciers, and storms heal, terrify, and judge. Nature restores the characters but punishes those who try to master it."],["Justice & injustice","Justine and the De Laceys suffer for crimes they didn't commit; Victor's silence lets injustice happen."]],"symbols":[["Fire / light","Knowledge and its danger (Prometheus). Warms the Creature, burns him, and later destroys the cottage."],["Lightning","Nature's power and the 'spark' of life — the blasted oak and Victor's own ruin."],["Ice / the Arctic","Isolation, emotional coldness, the limits of ambition; frames the whole novel."],["Paradise Lost","Creation, rebellion, and the Fall — Victor as God/Satan, the Creature as Adam/Satan."],["The miniature of Caroline","Lost motherly love and the false evidence that condemns Justine."],["Eyes & seeing","The Creature's 'dull yellow eye', the blind De Lacey who 'sees' truly, judgment by sight."],["The moon","Watches over key moments — the Creature's birth, his first nights, the window at Elizabeth's death."]],"essays":[["Who is the real monster — Victor or the Creature?","Use Victor's abandonment (Ch. 5), his silence (Ch. 7–8), and the Creature's speeches (Ch. 10, 17, 24)."],["How does Shelley use the nested narrative structure?","Consider reliability, sympathy, and how the Creature's voice sits at the center."],["Is Victor punished for seeking knowledge, or for failing his responsibilities?","Compare Ch. 4's warning with the Creature's 'parental' claims and Victor's last words."],["How does the novel explore isolation?","Trace Walton's letters, Victor's lab, the Creature's hovel, and the Arctic ending."],["What role do women play in Frankenstein?","Elizabeth, Justine, Caroline, Safie, and the destroyed female — passive victims or quiet critiques?"],["How does nature function in the novel?","The sublime Alps, the seasons Victor ignores, storms, and ice."]]}};
